{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p454_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 454, "text": "e. Other gage lights work. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nBulb faulty.Transmission temperature switch \nfaulty. Wire 18 faulty.Wire 2 faulty.Resistor faulty. Wire 10 faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #91.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs 12 vdc present at high \ntransmission temperature \nindicator light socket?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOGo to Step 6 of this \nfault.", "snippet": "e. Other gage lights work. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nBulb faulty.Transmission temperature switch \nfaulty. Wire 18 faulty.Wire 2 faulty.Resistor faulty. Wire 10 faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #91."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p455_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 455, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-403HIGH TRANSMISSION\u001f\nTEMPERATURE\u001f\nINDICATOR\nCENTER\u001f\nCONDUCTOR• Remove rings bracelets watches, necklac es, and any other jewelry before working around vehicle. Jewelry can catch on \nequipment and cause injury or short ac ross electrical circuit and cause severe burns or electrical shock. • Battery acid is harmful to skin and eyes. Always  wear eye protection when working with batteries. VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Remove lens/bulb from high \ntransmission temperature indicator. (2) Set multimeter to volts dc. (3) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (4) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \ncenter conductor of high transmission \ntemperature indicator light socket. (5) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE (TM 10-3930-673-10).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-403HIGH TRANSMISSION\u001f\nTEMPERATURE\u001f\nINDICATOR\nCENTER\u001f\nCONDUCTOR• Remove rings bracelets watches, necklac es, and any other jewelry before working around vehicle. Jewelry can catch"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p455_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 455, "text": "t multimeter positive lead (+) to \ncenter conductor of high transmission \ntemperature indicator light socket. (5) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE (TM 10-3930-673-10).\n(a) If 12 vdc is present, go to Step 2 of \nthis fault. (b) If 12 vdc is not present, go to \nStep 6 of this fault. (6) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). TR01503", "snippet": "t multimeter positive lead (+) to \ncenter conductor of high transmission \ntemperature indicator light socket. (5) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE (TM 10-3930-673-10)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p456_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 456, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-40424. HIGH TRANSMISSION TEMPERATURE INDICATOR DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHigh transmission temperature. Other gage lights work. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nBulb faulty. Transmission temperature switch \nfaulty. Wire 18 faulty.Wire 2 faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nContinuity check. STE/ICE-R #91. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf continuity is not present, replace \nbulb. KNOWN INFO\nHigh transmission temperature. Other gages work. 12 vdc present at socket. Bulb OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTransmission temperature switch \nfaulty. Wire 18 faulty.Wire 2 faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nContinuity check.STE/ICE-R #91. REASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question determines where \ntroubleshooting continues.2\nIs continuity present across \nhigh transmission \ntemperature lens/bulb? YESNOReplace light bulb \n(Para 8-9).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-40424. HIGH TRANSMISSION TEMPERATURE INDICATOR DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHigh transmission temperature. Other gage lights work. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nBulb faulty. Transmiss"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p456_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 456, "text": "/ICE-R #91. REASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question determines where \ntroubleshooting continues.2\nIs continuity present across \nhigh transmission \ntemperature lens/bulb? YESNOReplace light bulb \n(Para 8-9).\n3\nIs continuity present between \nhigh transmission \ntemperature indicator light \nsocket outside conductor and known good ground? YESNOGo to Step 4 of this \nfault. Go to Step 1 of this \nfault.", "snippet": "/ICE-R #91. REASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question determines where \ntroubleshooting continues.2\nIs continuity present across \nhigh transmission \ntemperature lens/bulb? YESNOReplace light bulb \n(Para 8-9)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p457_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 457, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-405HIGH TRANSMISSION\nTEMPERATURE\nINDICATORBULB GROUNDCENTER\nCONDUCTOR\nOUTSIDE\nCONDUCTORCONTINUITY CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to ohms.\n(2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \ncenter conductor of bulb.\n(3) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nbulb ground.\n(a) If continuity is present, go to Step 3 \nof this fault.\n(b) If continuity is not present, replace \nlens/bulb (Para 8-9).\nCONTINUITY CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to ohms.(2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \noutside conductor of high transmission \ntemperature indicator.\n(3) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground.(a) If continuity is present, replace \nlens/bulb (Para 8-9).\n(b) If continuity is not present, go to \nStep 4 of this fault.\nTR01504", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-405HIGH TRANSMISSION\nTEMPERATURE\nINDICATORBULB GROUNDCENTER\nCONDUCTOR\nOUTSIDE\nCONDUCTORCONTINUITY CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to ohms.\n(2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nc"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p458_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 458, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-40624. HIGH TRANSMISSION TEMPERATURE INDICATOR DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHigh transmission temperature. Other gage lights work. 12 vdc present at socket.Bulb OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTransmission temperature switch \nfaulty. Wire 18 faulty. Wire 2 faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nResistance check. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf correct resistance is not \nmeasured, transmission \ntemperature switch is faulty. KNOWN INFO\nHigh transmission temperature.Other gages work. 12 vdc present at socket. Bulb OK.Transmission temperature switch \nOK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 18 faulty.Wire 2 faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nContinuity check.STE/ICE-R #91.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-40624. HIGH TRANSMISSION TEMPERATURE INDICATOR DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHigh transmission temperature. Other gage lights work. 12 vdc present at socket.Bulb OK. POSSIB"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p458_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 458, "text": "mission temperature.Other gages work. 12 vdc present at socket. Bulb OK.Transmission temperature switch \nOK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 18 faulty.Wire 2 faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nContinuity check.STE/ICE-R #91.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf continuity is not present across \nwire, wire is faulty.4\nAre 115 to 140 ohms \nmeasured across \ntransmission temperature \nswitch stabilized at 200°F (93.3°C) for 15 minutes? YESNOReplace transmission \ntemperature switch \n(Para 8-16). 5\nIs continuity present across \nwire 18? YESNORepair or \nreplace wire 18 (Para 8-48).", "snippet": "mission temperature.Other gages work. 12 vdc present at socket. Bulb OK.Transmission temperature switch \nOK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 18 faulty.Wire 2 faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nContinuity check.STE/ICE-R #91."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p459_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 459, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-407HIGH TRANSMISSION\nTEMPERATURE\nINDICATORHIGH TRANSMISSION\nTEMPERATURE\nINDICATOR\nLIGHT SOCKETWIRE 10WIRE 18WIRE 18\nREDUCER\nTRANSMISSION\nTEMPERATURE\nSWITCHNUTLOCKWASHER\nWASHER\nPANELSCREWRESISTANCE CHECK\n(1) Remove nut, lockwasher, washer, and \nwire 18 from transmission temperature \nswitch. (2) Remove transmission temperature \nswitch from reducer. (3) Hang transmission temperature switch \nin pan of water. Do not let switch make \ncontact with pan. (4) Place a thermometer in water.(5) Put heat to pan of water and stir water \nfor consistent temperature of 200°F \n(93.3°C). Allow water temperature to \nstabilize for 15 minutes. Do not allow fingers or skin to contact hot water or hot parts. Serious burns to personnel could result.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-407HIGH TRANSMISSION\nTEMPERATURE\nINDICATORHIGH TRANSMISSION\nTEMPERATURE\nINDICATOR\nLIGHT SOCKETWIRE 10WIRE 18WIRE 18\nREDUCER\nTRANSMISSION\nTEMPERATURE\nSWITCHNUTLOCKWASHER\nWASHER\nPA"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p459_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 459, "text": "onsistent temperature of 200°F \n(93.3°C). Allow water temperature to \nstabilize for 15 minutes. Do not allow fingers or skin to contact hot water or hot parts. Serious burns to personnel could result.\n(6) Set multimeter to ohms.(7) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nstud of transmission temperature switch. (8) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nhex or large threads of transmission \ntemperature switch. (a) If 115 to 140 ohms are not \nmeasured, replace transmission \ntemperature switch (Para 8-16). (b) If 115 to 140 ohms are measured, \ninstall transmission temperature switch (Para 8-16) and go to \nStep 5 of this fault. CONTINUITY CHECK\n(1) Remove four screws and panel. (2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 18 at high transmission \ntemperature indicator terminal. (3) Using a known good jumper wire, \nconnect multimeter negative lead (–) to wire 18 at transmission temperature \nswitch terminal. (a) If continuity is not present, replace \nwire 18 (Para 8-48).", "snippet": "onsistent temperature of 200°F \n(93.3°C). Allow water temperature to \nstabilize for 15 minutes. Do not allow fingers or skin to contact hot water or hot parts. Serious burns to personnel could result."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p459_chunk_2", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 459, "text": "inal. (3) Using a known good jumper wire, \nconnect multimeter negative lead (–) to wire 18 at transmission temperature \nswitch terminal. (a) If continuity is not present, replace \nwire 18 (Para 8-48).\n(b) If continuity is present, go to Step 6 \nof this fault. TR01505", "snippet": "inal. (3) Using a known good jumper wire, \nconnect multimeter negative lead (–) to wire 18 at transmission temperature \nswitch terminal. (a) If continuity is not present, replace \nwire 18 (Para 8-48)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p460_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 460, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-40824. HIGH TRANSMISSION TEMPERATURE INDICATOR DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nParking brake OK.\nOther gages work.\n12 vdc not measured at socket.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nResistor faulty.\nWire 10 faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nContinuity test.STE/ICE-R #91.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf 1500 ohms are not measured, \nresistor is faulty.6\nAre 1500 ohms present \nacross resistor?\nYESNOReplace resistor \n(Para 8-9).\nRepair or replace wire 10 (Para 8-48).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-40824. HIGH TRANSMISSION TEMPERATURE INDICATOR DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nParking brake OK.\nOther gages work.\n12 vdc not measured at socket.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nResistor f"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p461_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 461, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-409HIGH\nTRANSMISSION\nTEMPERATURE\nINDICATORHIGH TRANSMISSION\nTEMPERATURE\nINDICATOR\nSOCKET WIRE 10WIRE 18\nPANELSCREWRESISTOR COVERRESISTANCE TEST\n(1) Remove cover and disconnect resistor \nfrom connector.\n(2) Attach multimeter positive lead (+) to \nresistor at circuit breaker side.\n(3) Attach multimeter negative lead (–) to \nresistor at light side.\n(a) If 1500 ohms resistance is \nmeasured, replace wire 10 \n(Para 8-48).\n(b) If 1500 ohms resistance is not \nmeasured, replace resistor (Para 8-9).\nTR01506", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-409HIGH\nTRANSMISSION\nTEMPERATURE\nINDICATORHIGH TRANSMISSION\nTEMPERATURE\nINDICATOR\nSOCKET WIRE 10WIRE 18\nPANELSCREWRESISTOR COVERRESISTANCE TEST\n(1) Remove cover and disconnect re"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p462_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 462, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-4102\n25. FRONT WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nMultimeter, Digital (Item 9, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (TM 10-3930-673-10)Parking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)Personnel Required\nTwo\nReferences\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P.\nKNOWN INFO\nWasher motor OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 30 faulty.\nFront wiper switch faulty.\nWire 32 faulty.Wire 31 faulty.\nFront wiper motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test.\nSTE/ICE-R #89.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf 24 vdc is not present, wire 30 \nfaulty.1\nIs 24 vdc present on wire 30?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNORepair wire 30 \n(Para 8-48).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-4102\n25. FRONT WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p463_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 463, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-411ENGINE \nSTART\nSWITCH\nWIRE 30\nFRONT WIPER\nSWITCH• Remove rings bracelets watches, necklac es, and any other jewelry before working around vehicle. Jewelry can catch on \nequipment and cause injury or short ac ross electrical circuit and cause severe burns or electrical shock. • Battery acid is harmful to skin and eyes. Always  wear eye protection when working with batteries. VOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Remove battery cover (Para 8-42). (2) Disconnect negative battery cable \n(Para 8-44). (3) Remove four screws from left-hand \ninstrument panel. (4) Lift panel out to get to back of front \nwiper switch. (5) Set multimeter to volts dc. (6) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 30 where wire 30 connects to front \nwiper switch.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-411ENGINE \nSTART\nSWITCH\nWIRE 30\nFRONT WIPER\nSWITCH• Remove rings bracelets watches, necklac es, and any other jewelry before working around vehicle. Jewelry can catch on \nequipme"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p463_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 463, "text": "ent panel. (4) Lift panel out to get to back of front \nwiper switch. (5) Set multimeter to volts dc. (6) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 30 where wire 30 connects to front \nwiper switch.\n(7) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (8) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (9) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).(a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 2 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, repair \nwire 30 (Para 8-48). (10) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (11) Disconnect negative battery cable \n(Para 8-44).TR01152", "snippet": "ent panel. (4) Lift panel out to get to back of front \nwiper switch. (5) Set multimeter to volts dc. (6) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 30 where wire 30 connects to front \nwiper switch."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p464_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 464, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-41225. FRONT WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor OK. Wire 30 OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFront wiper switch faulty. Wire 32 faulty. Wire 31 faulty. Front wiper motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf 24 vdc is not present, front wiper \nswitch faulty. KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor OK. Wire 30 OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFront wiper switch faulty.Wire 32 faulty.Wire 31 faulty. Front wiper motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf 24 vdc is not present, front wiper \nswitch faulty.2\nIs 24 vdc present at front \nwiper switch where wire 31 \nconnects (HIGH)? YESNOReplace front wiper \nswitch (Para 8-7). 3\nIs 24 vdc present at front \nwiper switch where wire 32 \nconnects (LOW)?", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-41225. FRONT WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor OK. Wire 30 OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFront wiper switch faulty. Wire 32 faulty. Wire 31 faulty. Front w"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p464_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 464, "text": "faulty.2\nIs 24 vdc present at front \nwiper switch where wire 31 \nconnects (HIGH)? YESNOReplace front wiper \nswitch (Para 8-7). 3\nIs 24 vdc present at front \nwiper switch where wire 32 \nconnects (LOW)?\nYESNOReplace front wiper \nswitch (Para 8-7).", "snippet": "faulty.2\nIs 24 vdc present at front \nwiper switch where wire 31 \nconnects (HIGH)? YESNOReplace front wiper \nswitch (Para 8-7). 3\nIs 24 vdc present at front \nwiper switch where wire 32 \nconnects (LOW)?"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p465_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 465, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-413WIRE 32WIRE 31\nFRONT WIPER\nSWITCHENGINE \nSTART\nSWITCHVOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 31 where wire 31 connects to front wiper switch. (3) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (4) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (5) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE (TM 10-3930-673-10). (6) Turn front wiper switch to HI position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).(a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 3 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, replace \nfront wiper switch (Para 8-7). (7) Turn front wiper switch to OFF position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (8) Turn engine start switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-413WIRE 32WIRE 31\nFRONT WIPER\nSWITCHENGINE \nSTART\nSWITCHVOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 31 where wire 31 connects "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p465_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 465, "text": "is fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, replace \nfront wiper switch (Para 8-7). (7) Turn front wiper switch to OFF position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (8) Turn engine start switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).\n(9) Disconnect negative battery cable \n(Para 8-44). VOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 32 where wire 32 connects to front \nwiper switch. (3) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (4) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (5) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (6) Turn front wiper switch to LO position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).(a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 4 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, replace \nfront wiper switch (Para 8-7). (7) Turn front wiper switch to OFF position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (8) Turn engine start switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (9) Disconnect negative battery cable  \n(Para 8-44).TR01153", "snippet": "is fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, replace \nfront wiper switch (Para 8-7). (7) Turn front wiper switch to OFF position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (8) Turn engine start switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p466_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 466, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-41425. FRONT WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor OK. Wire 30 OK. Front wiper switch OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 32 faulty. Wire 31 faulty. Front wiper motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf 24 vdc is not present, wire 32 \nfaulty. KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor OK. Wire 30 OK. Front wiper switch OK. Wire 32 OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 31 faulty. Front wiper motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf 24 vdc is not present, wire 31 \nfaulty.4\nIs 24 vdc present on wire 32 \nwhere wire 32 connects to \nfront wiper motor? YESNORepair wire 32 \n(Para 8-48). 5\nIs 24 vdc present on wire 31 \nwhere wire 31 connects to \nfront wiper motor? YESNORepair wire 31 \n(Para 8-48).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-41425. FRONT WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor OK. Wire 30 OK. Front wiper switch OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 32 faulty. Wire 31 faulty. Front wiper"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p466_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 466, "text": "wire 32 \nwhere wire 32 connects to \nfront wiper motor? YESNORepair wire 32 \n(Para 8-48). 5\nIs 24 vdc present on wire 31 \nwhere wire 31 connects to \nfront wiper motor? YESNORepair wire 31 \n(Para 8-48).\nReplace front wiper motor (Para 17-2).", "snippet": "wire 32 \nwhere wire 32 connects to \nfront wiper motor? YESNORepair wire 32 \n(Para 8-48). 5\nIs 24 vdc present on wire 31 \nwhere wire 31 connects to \nfront wiper motor? YESNORepair wire 31 \n(Para 8-48)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p467_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 467, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-415\nVOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 32 where wire 32 connects to front wiper motor. (3) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (4) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (5) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE (TM 10-3930-673-10). (6) Turn front wiper switch to LO position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).(a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 5 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, repair \nwire 32 (Para 8-48). (7) Turn front wiper switch to OFF position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (8) Turn engine start switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (9) Disconnect negative battery cable \n(Para 8-44). VOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-415\nVOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 32 where wire 32 connects to front wiper motor. (3) Connect multimeter negativ"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p467_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 467, "text": "er switch to OFF position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (8) Turn engine start switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (9) Disconnect negative battery cable \n(Para 8-44). VOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc.\n(2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 31 where wire 31 connects to front \nwiper motor. (3) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (4) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (5) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (6) Turn front wiper switch to HI position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).(a) If 24 vdc is present, replace front \nwiper motor (Para 17-2). (b) If 24 vdc is not present, repair \nwire 31 (Para 8-48). (7) Turn front wiper switch to OFF position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (8) Turn engine start switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (9) Install battery cover (Para 8-42). (10) Install four screws to secure left-hand \ninstrument panel.TR01154", "snippet": "er switch to OFF position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (8) Turn engine start switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (9) Disconnect negative battery cable \n(Para 8-44). VOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p468_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 468, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-4162\n26. REAR WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nMultimeter, Digital (Item 9, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (TM 10-3930-673-10)Parking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)Personnel Required\nTwo\nReferences\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P.\nKNOWN INFO\nWasher motor OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 30 faulty.\nRear wiper switch faulty.\nWire 34 faulty.Wire 33 faulty.\nRear wiper motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test.\nSTE/ICE-R #89.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf 24 vdc is not present, wire 30 \nfaulty.1\nIs 24 vdc present on wire 30?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNORepair wire 30 \n(Para 8-48).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-4162\n26. REAR WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p469_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 469, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-417WIRE 30\nREAR WIPER\nSWITCHENGINE START\nSWITCH• Remove rings, bracelets, watches, necklac es, and any other jewelry before working around vehicle. Jewelry can catch on \nequipment and cause injury or short ac ross electrical circuit and cause severe burns or electrical shock. • Battery acid is harmful to skin and eyes. Always  wear eye protection when working with batteries. VOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Remove battery cover (Para 8-42). (2) Disconnect negative battery cable \n(Para 8-44). (3) Remove four screws from left-hand \ninstrument panel. (4) Lift panel out to get to back of rear \nwiper switch. (5) Set multimeter to volts dc.(6) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 30 where wire 30 connects to rear wiper switch.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-417WIRE 30\nREAR WIPER\nSWITCHENGINE START\nSWITCH• Remove rings, bracelets, watches, necklac es, and any other jewelry before working around vehicle. Jewelry can catch on \nequipmen"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p469_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 469, "text": "trument panel. (4) Lift panel out to get to back of rear \nwiper switch. (5) Set multimeter to volts dc.(6) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 30 where wire 30 connects to rear wiper switch.\n(7) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (8) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (9) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE (TM 10-3930-673-10). (a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 2 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, repair \nwire 30 (Para 8-48). (10) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (11) Disconnect negative battery cable \n(Para 8-44).TR01155", "snippet": "trument panel. (4) Lift panel out to get to back of rear \nwiper switch. (5) Set multimeter to volts dc.(6) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 30 where wire 30 connects to rear wiper switch."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p470_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 470, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-41826. REAR WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor OK. Wire 30 OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nRear wiper switch faulty. Wire 34 faulty. Wire 33 faulty. Rear wiper motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf 24 vdc is not present, rear wiper \nswitch faulty. KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor OK. Wire 30 OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nRear wiper switch faulty.Wire 34 faulty.Wire 33 faulty. Rear wiper motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf 24 vdc is not present, rear wiper \nswitch faulty.2\nIs 24 vdc present at rear wiper \nswitch where wire 33 \nconnects? YESNOReplace rear wiper \nswitch (Para 8-7). 3\nIs 24 vdc present at rear wiper \nswitch where wire 34 \nconnects?", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-41826. REAR WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor OK. Wire 30 OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nRear wiper switch faulty. Wire 34 faulty. Wire 33 faulty. Rear wipe"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p470_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 470, "text": "r wiper \nswitch faulty.2\nIs 24 vdc present at rear wiper \nswitch where wire 33 \nconnects? YESNOReplace rear wiper \nswitch (Para 8-7). 3\nIs 24 vdc present at rear wiper \nswitch where wire 34 \nconnects?\nYESNOReplace rear wiper \nswitch (Para 8-7).", "snippet": "r wiper \nswitch faulty.2\nIs 24 vdc present at rear wiper \nswitch where wire 33 \nconnects? YESNOReplace rear wiper \nswitch (Para 8-7). 3\nIs 24 vdc present at rear wiper \nswitch where wire 34 \nconnects?"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p471_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 471, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-419WIRE 34WIRE 33\nREAR WIPER\nSWITCHENGINE START\nSWITCHVOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 33 where wire 33 connects to rear wiper switch. (3) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (4) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (5) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE (TM 10-3930-673-10). (6) Turn rear wiper switch to HI position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).(a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 3 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, replace \nrear wiper switch (Para 8-7). (7) Turn rear wiper switch to OFF position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (8) Turn engine start switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-419WIRE 34WIRE 33\nREAR WIPER\nSWITCHENGINE START\nSWITCHVOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 33 where wire 33 connects to"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p471_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 471, "text": "this fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, replace \nrear wiper switch (Para 8-7). (7) Turn rear wiper switch to OFF position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (8) Turn engine start switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).\n(9) Disconnect negative battery cable \n(Para 8-44). VOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 34 where wire 34 connects to rear \nwiper switch. (3) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (4) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (5) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (6) Turn rear wiper switch to LO position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).(a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 4 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, replace \nrear wiper switch (Para 8-7). (7) Turn rear wiper switch to OFF position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (8) Turn engine start switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (9) Disconnect negative battery cable \n(Para 8-44).TR01156", "snippet": "this fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, replace \nrear wiper switch (Para 8-7). (7) Turn rear wiper switch to OFF position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (8) Turn engine start switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p472_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 472, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-42026. REAR WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor OK. Wire 30. Rear wiper motor OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 34 faulty. Wire 33 faulty. Rear wiper motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf 24 vdc is not present, wire 34 \nfaulty. KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor OK. Wire 30 OK. Rear wiper switch OK. Wire 34 OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 33 faulty. Rear wiper motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf 24 vdc is not present, wire 33 \nfaulty.4\nIs 24 vdc present at wire 34 \nwhere wire 34 connects to \nrear wiper motor? YESNORepair wire 34 \n(Para 8-48). 5\nIf 24 vdc present at wire 33 \nwhere wire 33 connects to \nrear wiper motor? YESNORepair wire 33 \n(Para 8-48).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-42026. REAR WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor OK. Wire 30. Rear wiper motor OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 34 faulty. Wire 33 faulty. Rear wiper motor "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p472_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 472, "text": "t wire 34 \nwhere wire 34 connects to \nrear wiper motor? YESNORepair wire 34 \n(Para 8-48). 5\nIf 24 vdc present at wire 33 \nwhere wire 33 connects to \nrear wiper motor? YESNORepair wire 33 \n(Para 8-48).\nReplace rear wiper motor (Para 17-3).", "snippet": "t wire 34 \nwhere wire 34 connects to \nrear wiper motor? YESNORepair wire 34 \n(Para 8-48). 5\nIf 24 vdc present at wire 33 \nwhere wire 33 connects to \nrear wiper motor? YESNORepair wire 33 \n(Para 8-48)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p473_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 473, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-421WIRE 34\u001f\nTO MOTORWIRE 33\u001f\nTO MOTOR\nREAR WIPER\u001f\nSWITCHVOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Remove rear wiper motor from vehicle \n(Para 17-3). (3) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 34 where wire 34 connects to rear \nwiper motor. (4) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (5) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (6) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (7) Turn rear wiper switch to LO position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).(a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 5 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, repair \nwire 34 (Para 8-48). (8) Turn rear wiper switch to OFF position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-421WIRE 34\u001f\nTO MOTORWIRE 33\u001f\nTO MOTOR\nREAR WIPER\u001f\nSWITCHVOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Remove rear wiper motor from vehicle \n(Para 17-3). (3) Connect multimete"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p473_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 473, "text": " \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).(a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 5 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, repair \nwire 34 (Para 8-48). (8) Turn rear wiper switch to OFF position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).\n(9) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (10) Disconnect negative battery cable \n(Para 8-44). VOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 33 where wire 33 connects to rear \nwiper motor. (3) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (4) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (5) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (6) Turn rear wiper switch to HI position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).(a) If 24 vdc is present, replace rear \nwiper motor (Para 17-3). (b) If 24 vdc is not present, repair \nwire 33 (Para 8-48). (7) Turn rear wiper switch to OFF position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).", "snippet": " \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).(a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 5 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, repair \nwire 34 (Para 8-48). (8) Turn rear wiper switch to OFF position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p473_chunk_2", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 473, "text": "930-673-10).(a) If 24 vdc is present, replace rear \nwiper motor (Para 17-3). (b) If 24 vdc is not present, repair \nwire 33 (Para 8-48). (7) Turn rear wiper switch to OFF position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).\n(8) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (9) Install rear wiper motor (Para 17-3). (10) Install battery cover (Para 8-42). (11) Install four screws to secure left-hand \ninstrument panel.TR01157", "snippet": "930-673-10).(a) If 24 vdc is present, replace rear \nwiper motor (Para 17-3). (b) If 24 vdc is not present, repair \nwire 33 (Para 8-48). (7) Turn rear wiper switch to OFF position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p474_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 474, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-4222\n27. FRONT AND REAR WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nMultimeter, Digital (Item 9, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (TM 10-3930-673-10)Parking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)Personnel Required\nTwo\nReferences\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor does not work. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nBattery cable faulty. Wire 9 faulty. Connector P2 faulty.Connector J2 faulty. Circuit breaker CB6 faulty. Wire 1 faulty.Run relay faulty. Wire 3 faulty. Circuit breaker CB3 faulty.Wire 30 faulty. Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-4222\n27. FRONT AND REAR WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p474_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 474, "text": "aulty.Connector J2 faulty. Circuit breaker CB6 faulty. Wire 1 faulty.Run relay faulty. Wire 3 faulty. Circuit breaker CB3 faulty.Wire 30 faulty. Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs 24 vdc present at positive \ncable of battery?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOService or replace \nbattery (Para 8-42).", "snippet": "aulty.Connector J2 faulty. Circuit breaker CB6 faulty. Wire 1 faulty.Run relay faulty. Wire 3 faulty. Circuit breaker CB3 faulty.Wire 30 faulty. Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p475_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 475, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-423NEGATIVE\u001f\nBATTERY\u001f\nCABLECOVER\nBATTERY\nNEGATIVE\u001f\nBATTERY\u001f\nCABLECOVER\nBATTERYPOSITIVE LEAD\nPOSITIVE LEAD• Remove rings bracelets watches, necklac es, and any other jewelry before working around vehicle. Jewelry can catch on \nequipment and cause injury or short ac ross electrical circuit and cause severe burns or electrical shock. • Battery acid is harmful to skin and eyes. Always  wear eye protection when working with batteries. VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Remove battery cover (Para 8-42). (2) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). (3) Set multimeter to volts dc.(4) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-423NEGATIVE\u001f\nBATTERY\u001f\nCABLECOVER\nBATTERY\nNEGATIVE\u001f\nBATTERY\u001f\nCABLECOVER\nBATTERYPOSITIVE LEAD\nPOSITIVE LEAD• Remove rings bracelets watches, necklac es, and any other jewelry befor"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p475_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 475, "text": "tery cover (Para 8-42). (2) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). (3) Set multimeter to volts dc.(4) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground.\n(5) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \npositive lead of battery.(a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 2 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, service or \nreplace battery (Para 8-42). TR01158", "snippet": "tery cover (Para 8-42). (2) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). (3) Set multimeter to volts dc.(4) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p476_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 476, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-42427. FRONT AND REAR WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor does not work. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nBattery cable faulty. Wire 9 faulty.Connector P2 faulty. Connector J2 faulty. Circuit breaker CB6 faulty.Wire 1 faulty. Run relay faulty. Wire 3 faulty.Circuit breaker CB3 faulty. Wire 30 faulty. Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, battery \ncable faulty. KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor does not work. Battery cable OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 9 faulty.Connector P2 faulty.Connector J2 faulty. Circuit breaker CB6 faulty. Wire 1 faulty.Run relay faulty. Wire 3 faulty. Circuit breaker CB3 faulty.Wire 30 faulty. Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-42427. FRONT AND REAR WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor does not work. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nBattery cable faulty. Wire 9 faulty.Connector P2 faulty. C"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p476_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 476, "text": "aulty.Connector J2 faulty. Circuit breaker CB6 faulty. Wire 1 faulty.Run relay faulty. Wire 3 faulty. Circuit breaker CB3 faulty.Wire 30 faulty. Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, wire 9 or \nconnector P2 faulty.2\nIs 24 vdc present on positive \nbattery cable where positive \nbattery cable connects at \nstarter moto r solenoid? YESNORepair battery \ncable (Para 8-42). 3\nIs 24 vdc present on wire 9 \nwhere wire 9 connects to \nconnector P2-1? YESNOGo to Step 12 of \nthis fault.", "snippet": "aulty.Connector J2 faulty. Circuit breaker CB6 faulty. Wire 1 faulty.Run relay faulty. Wire 3 faulty. Circuit breaker CB3 faulty.Wire 30 faulty. Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p477_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 477, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-425POSITIVE\u001f\nBATTERY\u001fCABLE\nSTARTER SOLENOID\nCONNECTOR P2\nCONNECTOR\u001f\nJ2\nCONNECTOR P2P2-1VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (2) Set multimeter to volts dc. (3) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nstarter motor solenoid where positive \nbattery cable connects. (4) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (5) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 4 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, repair \nbattery cable (Para 8-42). (6) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Disconnect connector J2 from \nconnector P2. (2) Set multimeter to volts dc.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-425POSITIVE\u001f\nBATTERY\u001fCABLE\nSTARTER SOLENOID\nCONNECTOR P2\nCONNECTOR\u001f\nJ2\nCONNECTOR P2P2-1VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-4"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p477_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 477, "text": "attery cable (Para 8-42). (6) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Disconnect connector J2 from \nconnector P2. (2) Set multimeter to volts dc.\n(3) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nconnector P2-1. (4) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (5) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44).(a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 4 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, go to \nStep 12 of this fault. (6) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (7) Connect connector J2 to connector P2. TR01159", "snippet": "attery cable (Para 8-42). (6) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Disconnect connector J2 from \nconnector P2. (2) Set multimeter to volts dc."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p478_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 478, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-42627. FRONT AND REAR WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor does not work. Battery cable OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 9 faulty. Connector P2 faulty. Connector J2 faulty. Circuit breaker CB6 faulty.Wire 1 faulty. Run relay faulty. Wire 3 faulty.Circuit breaker CB3 faulty. Wire 30 faulty. Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, wire 9 \nfaulty. KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor does not work. Battery cable OK. Wire 9 OK. Connector P2 OK.Connector J2 OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nCircuit breaker CB6 faulty.Wire 1 faulty.Run relay faulty. Wire 3 faulty. Circuit breaker CB3 faulty.Wire 30 faulty. Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-42627. FRONT AND REAR WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor does not work. Battery cable OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 9 faulty. Connector P2 faulty. Conn"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p478_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 478, "text": "or J2 OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nCircuit breaker CB6 faulty.Wire 1 faulty.Run relay faulty. Wire 3 faulty. Circuit breaker CB3 faulty.Wire 30 faulty. Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, circuit \nbreaker CB6 faulty.4\nIs 24 vdc present on wire 9 \nwhere wire 9 connects to \ncircuit breaker CB6? YESNOGo to Step 11 of \nthis fault. 5\nIs 24 vdc present on wire 1 \nwhere wire 1 connects to \ncircuit breaker CB6? YESNOReplace circuit \nbreaker CB6 (Para 8-11).", "snippet": "or J2 OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nCircuit breaker CB6 faulty.Wire 1 faulty.Run relay faulty. Wire 3 faulty. Circuit breaker CB3 faulty.Wire 30 faulty. Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p479_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 479, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-427SCREW\nCIRCUIT BREAKER CB6 WIRE 1WIRE 9ACCESS \u001f\nPANELVOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (2) Remove four screws and access panel. (3) Set multimeter to volts dc.(4) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 9 where wire 9 connects to circuit breaker CB6. (5) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (6) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 5 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, go to \nStep 11 of this fault. (7) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-427SCREW\nCIRCUIT BREAKER CB6 WIRE 1WIRE 9ACCESS \u001f\nPANELVOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (2) Remove four screws and a"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p479_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 479, "text": "4). (a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 5 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, go to \nStep 11 of this fault. (7) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44).\nVOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc.(2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 1 where wire 1 connects to circuit \nbreaker CB6. (3) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (4) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44).(a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 6 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, replace \ncircuit breaker CB6. (5) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). TR01160", "snippet": "4). (a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 5 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, go to \nStep 11 of this fault. (7) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p480_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 480, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-42827. FRONT AND REAR WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor does not work. Battery cable OK. Wire 9 OK.Connector P2 OK. Connector J2 OK. Circuit breaker CB6 OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 1 faulty. Run relay faulty. Wire 3 faulty.Circuit breaker CB3 faulty. Wire 30 faulty. Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, wire 1 \nfaulty. KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor does not work. Battery cable OK. Wire 9 OK. Connector P2 OK.Connector J2 OK. Circuit breaker CB6 OK. Wire 1 OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nRun relay faulty. Wire 3 faulty. Circuit breaker CB3 faulty.Wire 30 faulty. Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-42827. FRONT AND REAR WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor does not work. Battery cable OK. Wire 9 OK.Connector P2 OK. Connector J2 OK. Circuit breake"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p480_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 480, "text": "onnector J2 OK. Circuit breaker CB6 OK. Wire 1 OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nRun relay faulty. Wire 3 faulty. Circuit breaker CB3 faulty.Wire 30 faulty. Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, run relay \nfaulty.6\nIs 24 vdc present on wire 1 \nwhere wire 1 connects to \nrelay K1? YESNORepair wire 1 (Para \n8-48). 7\nIs 24 vdc present on wire 3 \nwhere wire 3 connects to \nrelay K1? YESNOReplace run relay \n(Para 8-23).", "snippet": "onnector J2 OK. Circuit breaker CB6 OK. Wire 1 OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nRun relay faulty. Wire 3 faulty. Circuit breaker CB3 faulty.Wire 30 faulty. Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p481_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 481, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-429RELAY K1\nWIRE 3 WIRE 1VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (2) Set multimeter to volts dc. (3) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 1 where wire 1 connects to run \nrelay. (4) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (5) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 7 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, repair \nwire 1. (6) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 3 where wire 3 connects to run \nrelay. (3) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-429RELAY K1\nWIRE 3 WIRE 1VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (2) Set multimeter to volts dc. (3) Connect multimeter pos"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p481_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 481, "text": "). VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 3 where wire 3 connects to run \nrelay. (3) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground.\n(4) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (5) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 8 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, replace run \nrelay (Para 8-23). (6) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (7) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44).TR01161", "snippet": "). VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 3 where wire 3 connects to run \nrelay. (3) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p482_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 482, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-43027. FRONT AND REAR WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor does not work. Battery cable OK. Wire 9 OK.Connector P2 OK. Connector J2 OK. Circuit breaker CB6 OK.Wire 1 OK. Run relay OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 3 faulty. Circuit breaker CB3 faulty. Wire 30 faulty. Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, wire 3 \nfaulty. KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor does not work. Battery cable OK. Wire 9 OK. Connector P2 OK.Connector J2 OK. Circuit breaker CB6 OK. Wire 1 OK.Run relay OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 3 faulty.Circuit breaker CB3 faulty.Wire 30 faulty. Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-43027. FRONT AND REAR WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor does not work. Battery cable OK. Wire 9 OK.Connector P2 OK. Connector J2 OK. Circuit breake"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p482_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 482, "text": "2 OK.Connector J2 OK. Circuit breaker CB6 OK. Wire 1 OK.Run relay OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 3 faulty.Circuit breaker CB3 faulty.Wire 30 faulty. Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, circuit \nbreaker CB3 faulty.8\nIs 24 vdc present on wire 3 \nwhere wire 3 connects to \ncircuit breaker CB3? YESNORepair wire 3 (Para \n8-48). 9\nIs 24 vdc present on wire 30 \nwhere wire 30 connects to \ncircuit breaker CB3? YESNOReplace circuit \nbreaker CB3 (Para 8-11).", "snippet": "2 OK.Connector J2 OK. Circuit breaker CB6 OK. Wire 1 OK.Run relay OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 3 faulty.Circuit breaker CB3 faulty.Wire 30 faulty. Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p483_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 483, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-431CIRCUIT BREAKER CB3\nWIRE 3\nWIRE 30VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (2) Set multimeter to volts dc. (3) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 3 where wire 3 connects to circuit \nbreaker CB3. (4) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (5) Connect negative battery cable (1) to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (6) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).(a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 9 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, repair \nwire 3 (Para 8-48). (7) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (8) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-431CIRCUIT BREAKER CB3\nWIRE 3\nWIRE 30VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (2) Set multimeter to volts dc. (3) Connect mu"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p483_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 483, "text": " 24 vdc is not present, repair \nwire 3 (Para 8-48). (7) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (8) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44).\nVOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc.(2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 30 where wire 30 connects to circuit breaker CB3. (3) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (4) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (5) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE (TM 10-3930-673-10). (a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 10 \nof this fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, replace \ncircuit breaker CB3 (Para 8-11). (6) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (7) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44).TR01162", "snippet": " 24 vdc is not present, repair \nwire 3 (Para 8-48). (7) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (8) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p484_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 484, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-43227. FRONT AND REAR WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor does not work. Battery cable OK. Wire 9 OK.Connector P2 OK. Connector J2 OK. Circuit breaker CB6 OK.Wire 1 OK. Run relay OK. Wire 3 OK.Circuit breaker CB3 OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 30 faulty.Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nContinuity test. STE/ICE-R #91. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, wire 30 \nfaulty. KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor does not work. Wire 9 OK. Connector P2 OK. Connector J2 OK.Circuit breaker CB6 OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nBattery cable faulty.Wire 1 faulty.Wire 3 faulty. Circuit breaker CB3 faulty. Wire 30 faulty.Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nContinuity test.STE/ICE-R #91.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-43227. FRONT AND REAR WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor does not work. Battery cable OK. Wire 9 OK.Connector P2 OK. Connector J2 OK. Circuit breake"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p484_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 484, "text": "r J2 OK.Circuit breaker CB6 OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nBattery cable faulty.Wire 1 faulty.Wire 3 faulty. Circuit breaker CB3 faulty. Wire 30 faulty.Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nContinuity test.STE/ICE-R #91.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf continuity is not present, ground \nfaulty.10\nIs continuity present on \nwire 30 between where \nwire 30 connects to circuit \nbreaker CB3 and washer switch? YESNORepair wire 30 \n(Para 8-48). 11\nIs continuity present on wire 9 \nbetween wire 9 and \nconnector J2-1? YESNORepair wire 9 or \nconnector J2 \n(Para 8-48).Repair ground (Para 8-48).", "snippet": "r J2 OK.Circuit breaker CB6 OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nBattery cable faulty.Wire 1 faulty.Wire 3 faulty. Circuit breaker CB3 faulty. Wire 30 faulty.Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nContinuity test.STE/ICE-R #91."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p485_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 485, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-433WASHER SWITCHWIRE 30\nCIRCUIT BREAKER CB3\nCIRCUIT BREAKER CB6WIRE 30WIRE 9\nCONNECTOR J2\nCONNECTOR\u001f\nJ2\nCONNECTOR P2J2-1CONTINUITY TEST\n(1) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (2) Set multimeter to ohms. (3) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 30 where wire 30 connects to \ncircuit breaker CB3. (4) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nwire 30 where wire 30 connects to washer switch. (a) If continuity is present, repair \nground wire. (b) If continuity is not present, repair \nwire 30. (5) Install access panel and four screws. CONTINUITY TEST\n(1) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (2) Disconnect connector J2 from \nconnector P2. (3) Set multimeter to ohms.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-433WASHER SWITCHWIRE 30\nCIRCUIT BREAKER CB3\nCIRCUIT BREAKER CB6WIRE 30WIRE 9\nCONNECTOR J2\nCONNECTOR\u001f\nJ2\nCONNECTOR P2J2-1CONTINUITY TEST\n(1) Disconnect negative battery cable from"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p485_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 485, "text": "ess panel and four screws. CONTINUITY TEST\n(1) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (2) Disconnect connector J2 from \nconnector P2. (3) Set multimeter to ohms.\n(4) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 9 where wire 9 connects to circuit \nbreaker CB6. (5) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nconnector J2-1.(a) If continuity is present, go to Step 5 \nof this fault. (b) If continuity is not present, repair \nwire 9 or connector J2 (Para 8-48). TR01163", "snippet": "ess panel and four screws. CONTINUITY TEST\n(1) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (2) Disconnect connector J2 from \nconnector P2. (3) Set multimeter to ohms."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p486_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 486, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-43427. FRONT AND REAR WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor does not work. Wire 9 OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nBattery cable faulty. Wire 1 faulty. Wire 3 faulty. Circuit breaker CB3 faulty.Wire 30 faulty. Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nContinuity test.STE/ICE-R #91. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf continuity is not present, wire 9 \nor connector P2 faulty. KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor does not work. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nBattery cable faulty.Wire 9 faulty. Connector P2 faulty.Connector J2 faulty. Circuit breaker CB6 faulty. Wire 1 faulty.Wire 3 faulty. Circuit breaker CB3 faulty. Wire 30 faulty.Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test.STE/ICE-R #82.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-43427. FRONT AND REAR WINDSHIELD WIPERS DO NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nWasher motor does not work. Wire 9 OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nBattery cable faulty. Wire 1 faulty. Wire 3 fau"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p486_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 486, "text": "lty. Connector P2 faulty.Connector J2 faulty. Circuit breaker CB6 faulty. Wire 1 faulty.Wire 3 faulty. Circuit breaker CB3 faulty. Wire 30 faulty.Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test.STE/ICE-R #82.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, alternator \nfaulty.12\nIs continuity present on wire 9 \nbetween starter motor \nsolenoid and connector P2-1? YESNO\nGo to Step 4 of this \nfault. 13\nIs 24 voltage output 24 vdc on \nSTE/ICE-R? YESNOReplace alternator \n(Para 8-2).Repair wire 9 or \nconnector P2 \n(Para 8-49). Go to Step 4 of this \nfault.", "snippet": "lty. Connector P2 faulty.Connector J2 faulty. Circuit breaker CB6 faulty. Wire 1 faulty.Wire 3 faulty. Circuit breaker CB3 faulty. Wire 30 faulty.Ground faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test.STE/ICE-R #82."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p487_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 487, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-435CONNECTOR P2\nCONNECTOR\u001f\nJ2\nCONNECTOR P2P2-1\nSTARTER SOLENOID\nDCA-1\u001f\nCONNECTORWIRE 9CONTINUITY TEST\n(1) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (2) Disconnect connector J2 from \nconnector P2. (3) Set multimeter to ohms. (4) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 9 where wire 9 connects to starter \nmotor solenoid. (5) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nconnector P2-1.(a) If continuity is present, go to Step 4 \nof this fault. (b) If continuity is not present, repair \nwire 9 or connector P2 (Para 8-49). VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery. (2) Remove dust cover from DCA-1 \nconnector. (3) Connect cable W1 to the Vehicle Test \nMeter (VTM) and DCA-1 connector.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-435CONNECTOR P2\nCONNECTOR\u001f\nJ2\nCONNECTOR P2P2-1\nSTARTER SOLENOID\nDCA-1\u001f\nCONNECTORWIRE 9CONTINUITY TEST\n(1) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p487_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 487, "text": "). VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery. (2) Remove dust cover from DCA-1 \nconnector. (3) Connect cable W1 to the Vehicle Test \nMeter (VTM) and DCA-1 connector.\n(4) Power up STE/ICE-R VTM in the DCA \nmode (TM 9-4910-571-12&P). (5) Turn auxiliary fuel shut-off switch on. (6) Start engine (TM 10-3930-763-10). (7) Perform STE/ICE test #82. (8) Press and release test button.(9) Observe VTM display for test results. (a) If voltage is present at output of \nalternator, go to Step 4 of this fault. (b) If voltage is not present at output of \nalternator, replace alternator (Para 8-2). (10) Shut off engine (TM 10-3930-673-10). (11) Turn auxiliary fuel shut-off switch off. (12) Power down STE/ICE-R VTM in the \nDCA mode (TM 9-4910-571-12&P). (13) Disconnect DCA cable from DCA-1(2).(14) Install battery cover (Para 8-42). TR01164", "snippet": "). VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery. (2) Remove dust cover from DCA-1 \nconnector. (3) Connect cable W1 to the Vehicle Test \nMeter (VTM) and DCA-1 connector."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p488_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 488, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-4362\n28. WINDSHIELD WASHER DOES NOT OPERATE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nMultimeter, Digital (Item 9, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (TM 10-3930-673-10)Parking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)Personnel Required\nTwo\nReferences\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nFront and rear wipers OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 30 faulty. Washer switch faulty. Wire 35 faulty.Resistor assembly faulty. Washer motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-4362\n28. WINDSHIELD WASHER DOES NOT OPERATE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nM"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p488_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 488, "text": "71-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nFront and rear wipers OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 30 faulty. Washer switch faulty. Wire 35 faulty.Resistor assembly faulty. Washer motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf 24 vdc is not present, wire 30 \nfaulty.1\nIs 24 vdc present at washer \nswitch where wire 30 \nconnects?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNORepair wire 30 \n(Para 8-48).", "snippet": "71-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nFront and rear wipers OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 30 faulty. Washer switch faulty. Wire 35 faulty.Resistor assembly faulty. Washer motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p489_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 489, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-437LEFT-HAND\u001f\nINSTRUMENT PANELSCREW\nWASHER SWITCH\nWIRE 30• Remove rings bracelets watches, necklac es, and any other jewelry before working around vehicle. Jewelry can catch on \nequipment and cause injury or short ac ross electrical circuit and cause severe burns or electrical shock. • Battery acid is harmful to skin and eyes. Always  wear eye protection when working with batteries. VOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Remove battery cover (Para 8-42). (2) Disconnect negative battery cable \n(Para 8-44). (3) Remove four screws from left-hand \ninstrument panel. (4) Lift left-hand instrument panel out to \nget to washer switch. (5) Set multimeter to volts dc. (6) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 30 where wire 30 connects to \nwasher switch.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-437LEFT-HAND\u001f\nINSTRUMENT PANELSCREW\nWASHER SWITCH\nWIRE 30• Remove rings bracelets watches, necklac es, and any other jewelry before working around vehicle. Jewelry can catch on \n"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p489_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 489, "text": " panel. (4) Lift left-hand instrument panel out to \nget to washer switch. (5) Set multimeter to volts dc. (6) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 30 where wire 30 connects to \nwasher switch.\n(7) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (8) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (9) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).(a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 2 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, repair \nwire 30 (Para 8-48). (10) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (11) Disconnect negative battery cable \n(Para 8-44). TR01105", "snippet": " panel. (4) Lift left-hand instrument panel out to \nget to washer switch. (5) Set multimeter to volts dc. (6) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 30 where wire 30 connects to \nwasher switch."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p490_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 490, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-43828. WINDSHIELD WASHER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nWasher motor OK.\nWire 30 OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWasher switch faulty.\nWire 35 faulty.\nResistor assembly faulty.\nWasher motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test.\nSTE/ICE-R.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf 24 vdc is not present, washer \nswitch faulty.\nKNOWN INFO\nWasher motor OK.\nWire 30 OK.\nRear wiper switch OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 35 faulty.\nResistor assembly faulty.\nWasher motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test.\nSTE/ICE-R.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf 24 vdc is not present, wire 35 \nfaulty.2\nIs 24 vdc present at washer \nswitch where wire 35 \nconnects?\nYESNOReplace washer \nswitch (Para 8-7).\n3\nIs 24 vdc present at resistor \nassembly where wire 35 \nconnects?\nYESNORepair wire 35 \n(Para 8-48).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-43828. WINDSHIELD WASHER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nWasher motor OK.\nWire 30 OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWasher switch faulty.\nWire 35 faulty.\nResistor assembly faulty.\nWasher"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p491_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 491, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-439\nVOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 35 where wire 35 connects to washer switch. (3) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (4) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (5) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE (TM 10-3930-673-10). (6) Depress washer switch \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).(a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 3 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, replace \nwasher switch (Para 8-7). (7) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (8) Disconnect negative battery cable \n(Para 8-44). VOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-439\nVOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 35 where wire 35 connects to washer switch. (3) Connect multimeter negative le"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p491_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 491, "text": " replace \nwasher switch (Para 8-7). (7) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (8) Disconnect negative battery cable \n(Para 8-44). VOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc.\n(2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 35 where wire 35 connects to \nresistor assembly. (3) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (4) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (5) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (6) Depress washer switch \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).(a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 4 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, repair \nwire 35. (7) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (8) Disconnect negative battery cable \n(Para 8-44). TR01106", "snippet": " replace \nwasher switch (Para 8-7). (7) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (8) Disconnect negative battery cable \n(Para 8-44). VOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p492_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 492, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-44028. WINDSHIELD WASHER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nWasher motor OK.\nWire 30 OK.\nRear wiper switch OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 35 faulty.\nWasher motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test.STE/ICE-R.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf 24 vdc is not present, wire 35 \nfaulty.\nKNOWN INFO\nWasher motor OK.\nWire 30 OK.\nRear wiper switch OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 35 faulty.\nWasher motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test.STE/ICE-R.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf 24 vdc is not present, wire 35 \nfaulty.4\nIs 24 vdc present at resistor \nassembly where wire 35 leads \nto washer motor?\nYESNOReplace resistor \nassembly (Para 17-4).\n5\nIs 24 vdc present on wire 35 \nwhere wire 35 connects to \nwasher motor?\nYESNORepair wire 35 \n(Para 8-48).\nReplace washer motor (Para 17-4).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-44028. WINDSHIELD WASHER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nWasher motor OK.\nWire 30 OK.\nRear wiper switch OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 35 faulty.\nWasher motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p493_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 493, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-441\nVOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 35 where wire 35 leaves resistor assembly. (3) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (4) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (5) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE (TM 10-3930-673-10). (6) Depress washer switch \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).(a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 5 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, replace \nresistor assembly (Para 17-4). (7) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (8) Disconnect negative battery cable \n(Para 8-44). VOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-441\nVOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 35 where wire 35 leaves resistor assembly. (3) Connect multimeter negative lea"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p493_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 493, "text": "ace \nresistor assembly (Para 17-4). (7) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (8) Disconnect negative battery cable \n(Para 8-44). VOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc.\n(2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nwire 35 where wire 35 connects to \nmotor. (3) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (4) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-44). (5) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (6) Depress washer switch \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).(a) If 24 vdc is present, replace \nwasher motor (Para 17-4). (b) If 24 vdc is not present, repair \nwire 35 (Para 8-48). (7) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (8) Install left-hand instrument panel and \nfour screws. (9) Install battery cover (Para 8-42).TR01107", "snippet": "ace \nresistor assembly (Para 17-4). (7) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (8) Disconnect negative battery cable \n(Para 8-44). VOLTAGE CHECK\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p494_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 494, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-4422\n29. HEATER DOES NOT OPERATE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nMultimeter, Digital (Item 9, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (TM 10-3930-673-10)Parking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)Personnel Required\nTwo\nReferences\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nRed light extinguished. Defrost fans OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 80 faulty.Heater/air conditioner switch faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual Inspection.STE/ICE-R #89.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-4422\n29. HEATER DOES NOT OPERATE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nMultimeter, D"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p494_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 494, "text": "0-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nRed light extinguished. Defrost fans OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 80 faulty.Heater/air conditioner switch faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual Inspection.STE/ICE-R #89.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs red light illuminated on \nheater/air conditioner switch?START\nYESNOGo to Step 2 of this \nfault. Go to Mechanical System Troubleshooting, \nFault 25, Cab heater does not heat cab.", "snippet": "0-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nRed light extinguished. Defrost fans OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 80 faulty.Heater/air conditioner switch faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual Inspection.STE/ICE-R #89."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p495_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 495, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-443HEATER AND\nAIR CONDITIONER\nSWITCHENGINE\nSTART\nSWITCH\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Turn engine start switch to START \nposition, START ENGINE \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).\n(2) Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nHEAT (TM 10-3930-673-10).(a) If light is illuminated, go to \nMechanical System \nTroubleshooting Fault 25, Cab \nheater does not heat cab.\n(b) If light is not illuminated, go to \nStep 2 of this fault.\n(3) Turn heater/air conditioner switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).\n(4) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10).\nTR01072", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-443HEATER AND\nAIR CONDITIONER\nSWITCHENGINE\nSTART\nSWITCH\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Turn engine start switch to START \nposition, START ENGINE \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).\n(2) Turn heater/air "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p496_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 496, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-44429. HEATER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nRed light extinguished.\nDefrost fans OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 80 faulty.\nHeater/air conditioner switch faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage Test.\nSTE/ICE-R #89.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, wire 80 is \nfaulty.2\nIs 24 vdc present at heater/air \nconditioner switch where wire \n80 (red wire) connects pin 5?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.\nYESNORepair wire 80 \n(Para 8-48).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-44429. HEATER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nRed light extinguished.\nDefrost fans OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 80 faulty.\nHeater/air conditioner switch faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVol"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p497_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 497, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-4458\n71\n42\n53\n6\nWIRE 80\nHEATER AND\u001f\nAIR CONDITIONER\u001f\nSWITCHENGINE\u001f\nSTART SWITCHSCREW\nPANELHEATER AND\u001f\nAIR CONDITIONER\u001f\nSWITCHRemove rings, bracelets, watc hes, necklaces, and any other jewelry before work ing around vehicle. Jewelry can catch on equipmen t \nand cause injury or short across el ectrical circuit and cause severe burns or electrical shock. Ba ttery acid is harmful to skin  and eyes. Always wear eye protection when working with batteries. VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Remove battery cover (Para 8-42). (2) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). (3) Remove four screws from heater and \nair conditioner temperature control \npanel. (4) Set multimeter to volts dc.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-4458\n71\n42\n53\n6\nWIRE 80\nHEATER AND\u001f\nAIR CONDITIONER\u001f\nSWITCHENGINE\u001f\nSTART SWITCHSCREW\nPANELHEATER AND\u001f\nAIR CONDITIONER\u001f\nSWITCHRemove rings, bracelets, watc hes, necklaces, and any"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p497_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 497, "text": "). (2) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). (3) Remove four screws from heater and \nair conditioner temperature control \npanel. (4) Set multimeter to volts dc.\n(5) Raise heater and air conditioner \ntemperature control panel out to reach \nheater/air conditioner switch. (6) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nheater/air conditioner switch where wire 80 (red wire) connects pin 5. (7) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (8) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). (9) Turn engine start switch to START \nposition, START ENGINE \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 3 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, repair \nwire 80 (Para 8-48). (10) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (11) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). TR01073", "snippet": "). (2) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). (3) Remove four screws from heater and \nair conditioner temperature control \npanel. (4) Set multimeter to volts dc."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p498_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 498, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-44629. HEATER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nRed light extinguished.\nDefrost fans OK.\nWire 80 OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHeater/air conditioner switch faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage Test.\nSTE/ICE-R #89.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, heater/air \nconditioner switch is faulty.3\nIs voltage present at heater/\nair conditioner switch where \nred/white wire connects pin 2?\nYESNOReplace heater/air \nconditioner switch (Para 8-7).\nGo to Mechanical System Troubleshooting, Fault 25, Cab heater does not heat cab.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-44629. HEATER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nRed light extinguished.\nDefrost fans OK.\nWire 80 OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHeater/air conditioner switch faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p499_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 499, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-4478\n71\n42\n53\n6\nHEATER AND\u001f\nAIR CONDITIONER\u001f\nSWITCHENGINE\u001f\nSTART SWITCHRED/WHITE \u001f\nWIRESCREW\nPANELHEATER AND\u001f\nAIR CONDITIONER\u001f\nSWITCH\nVOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nheater/air conditioner switch where red/white wire connects pin 2. (3) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (4) Turn engine start switch to START \nposition, START ENGINE \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (5) Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nHEATER (TM 10-3930-673-10)(a) If voltage is present, go to \nMechanical System \nTroubleshooting, Fault 25, Cab \nheater does not heat cab. (b) If voltage is not present, replace \nheater/air conditioner switch (Para 8-7).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-4478\n71\n42\n53\n6\nHEATER AND\u001f\nAIR CONDITIONER\u001f\nSWITCHENGINE\u001f\nSTART SWITCHRED/WHITE \u001f\nWIRESCREW\nPANELHEATER AND\u001f\nAIR CONDITIONER\u001f\nSWITCH\nVOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p499_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 499, "text": "-673-10)(a) If voltage is present, go to \nMechanical System \nTroubleshooting, Fault 25, Cab \nheater does not heat cab. (b) If voltage is not present, replace \nheater/air conditioner switch (Para 8-7).\n(6) Turn heater/air conditioner switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10)\n(7) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). TR01074", "snippet": "-673-10)(a) If voltage is present, go to \nMechanical System \nTroubleshooting, Fault 25, Cab \nheater does not heat cab. (b) If voltage is not present, replace \nheater/air conditioner switch (Para 8-7)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p500_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 500, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-4482\n30. AIR CONDITIONER DOES NOT OPERATE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nMultimeter, Digital (Item 9, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (TM 10-3930-673-10)Parking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)Personnel Required\nTwo\nReferences\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nGreen light extinguished. Defrost fans OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 80 faulty.Heater/air conditioner switch faulty.Light green wire faulty. Thermostat faulty. Low pressure cut-off switch faulty.Dark green wire faulty. High pressure cut-off switch faulty. Wire 81 faulty.A/C compressor clutch faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #89.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-4482\n30. AIR CONDITIONER DOES NOT OPERATE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nMult"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p500_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 500, "text": "mostat faulty. Low pressure cut-off switch faulty.Dark green wire faulty. High pressure cut-off switch faulty. Wire 81 faulty.A/C compressor clutch faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #89.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs green light illuminated on \nheater/air conditioner switch?START\nYESNOGo to Step 2 of this \nfault. Go to Mechanical System Troubleshooting \nFault 26, Air conditioner does not cool cab.", "snippet": "mostat faulty. Low pressure cut-off switch faulty.Dark green wire faulty. High pressure cut-off switch faulty. Wire 81 faulty.A/C compressor clutch faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #89."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p501_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 501, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-449HEATER/\u001f\nAIR CONDITIONER\u001f\nSWITCHCAB BLOWER SWITCHENGINE\u001f\nSTART SWITCH\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Turn engine start switch to START \nposition, START ENGINE \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).\n(2) Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nA/C (TM 10-3930-673-10).(a) If light is illuminated, go to \nMechanical System \nTroubleshooting, Fault 26, Air \nconditioner does not cool cab.\n(b) If light is not illuminated, go to \nStep 2 of this fault.\n(3) Turn heater/air conditioner switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).\n(4) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10).\nTR01108", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-449HEATER/\u001f\nAIR CONDITIONER\u001f\nSWITCHCAB BLOWER SWITCHENGINE\u001f\nSTART SWITCH\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Turn engine start switch to START \nposition, START ENGINE \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).\n(2)"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p502_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 502, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-45030. AIR CONDITIONER DO ES NOT OPERATE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nGreen light extinguished.\nDefrost fans OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 80 faulty.\nHeater/air conditioner switch faulty.\nLight green wire faulty.\nThermostat faulty.Low pressure cut-off switch faulty.\nDark green wire faulty.\nHigh pressure cut-off switch faulty.Wire 81 faulty.\nA/C compressor clutch faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage Test.STE/ICE-R #89.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, wire 80 is \nfaulty.2\nIs 24 vdc present at heat/air \nconditioner switch where wire \n80 (red wire) connects pin 5?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.\nYESNORepair wire 80 \n(Para 8-48).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-45030. AIR CONDITIONER DO ES NOT OPERATE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nGreen light extinguished.\nDefrost fans OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 80 faulty.\nHeater/air conditioner switch faulty.\nLig"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p503_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 503, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-4518\n71\n42\n53\n6SCREWHEATER/AIR CONDITIONER \u001f\nSWITCH\nPANEL\nHEATER/\u001f\nAIR CONDITIONER\u001f\nSWITCHCAB BLOWER SWITCHENGINE\u001f\nSTART SWITCH• Remove rings, bracelets, watches, necklac es, and any other jewelry before working around vehicle. Jewelry can catch on \nequipment and cause injury or short ac ross electrical circuit and cause severe burns or electrical shock. • Battery acid is harmful to skin and eyes. Always  wear eye protection when working with batteries. VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Remove battery cover (Para 8-42). (2) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). (3) Remove four screws from heater and \nair conditioner temperature control \npanel. (4) Set multimeter to volts dc.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-4518\n71\n42\n53\n6SCREWHEATER/AIR CONDITIONER \u001f\nSWITCH\nPANEL\nHEATER/\u001f\nAIR CONDITIONER\u001f\nSWITCHCAB BLOWER SWITCHENGINE\u001f\nSTART SWITCH• Remove rings, bracelets, watches, necklac es, and"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p503_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 503, "text": "). (2) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). (3) Remove four screws from heater and \nair conditioner temperature control \npanel. (4) Set multimeter to volts dc.\n(5) Raise heater and air conditioner \ntemperature control panel out to reach heater/air conditioner switch. (6) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nheater/air conditioner switch where wire 80 (red wire) connects pin 5. (7) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (8) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). (9) Turn engine start switch to START \nposition, START ENGINE (TM 10-3930-673-10). (a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 3 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, repair \nwire 80 (Para 8-48). (10) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (11) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). TR01109", "snippet": "). (2) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). (3) Remove four screws from heater and \nair conditioner temperature control \npanel. (4) Set multimeter to volts dc."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p504_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 504, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-45230. AIR CONDITIONER DO ES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nGreen light extinguished. Defrost fans OK. Wire 80 OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHeater/air conditioner switch faulty. Light green wire faulty. Thermostat faulty.Low pressure cut-off switch faulty. Dark green wire faulty. High pressure cut-off switch faulty.Wire 81 faulty. A/C compressor clutch faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage Test.STE/ICE-R #89. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, heater/air \nconditioner switch is faulty. KNOWN INFO\nGreen light extinguished. Defrost fans OK. Wire 80 OK. Heater/air conditioner switch OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nLight green wire faulty. Thermostat faulty. Low pressure cut-off switch faulty.Dark green wire faulty. High pressure cut-off switch faulty.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-45230. AIR CONDITIONER DO ES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nGreen light extinguished. Defrost fans OK. Wire 80 OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHeater/air conditioner switch faulty. Light g"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p504_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 504, "text": "e 80 OK. Heater/air conditioner switch OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nLight green wire faulty. Thermostat faulty. Low pressure cut-off switch faulty.Dark green wire faulty. High pressure cut-off switch faulty.\nWire 81 faulty.A/C compressor clutch faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test.STE/ICE-R #89. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, light green \nwire is faulty.3\nIs 24 vdc present at heater/air \nconditioner switch where light \ngreen wire connects pin 1? YESNOReplace heater/air \nconditioner switch \n(Para 17-9). 4\nIs 24 vdc present at \nthermostat where light green \nwire connects? YESNORepair light green \nwire (Para 8-48).", "snippet": "e 80 OK. Heater/air conditioner switch OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nLight green wire faulty. Thermostat faulty. Low pressure cut-off switch faulty.Dark green wire faulty. High pressure cut-off switch faulty."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p505_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 505, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-453LIGHT GREEN\u001f\nWIRE\nTHERMOSTAT8\n71\n42\n53\n6SCREWHEATER/AIR CONDITIONER \u001f\nSWITCH\nPANELVOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nheater/air conditioner switch where light green wire connects pin 1. (3) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (4) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegtive side of battery (Para 8-42). (5) Turn engine start switch to START \nposition, START ENGINE (TM 10-3930-673-10). (6) Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nA/C (TM 10-3930-673-10)(a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 4 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, replace \nheater/air conditioner switch \n(Para 17-9).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-453LIGHT GREEN\u001f\nWIRE\nTHERMOSTAT8\n71\n42\n53\n6SCREWHEATER/AIR CONDITIONER \u001f\nSWITCH\nPANELVOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nhea"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p505_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 505, "text": " Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nA/C (TM 10-3930-673-10)(a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 4 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, replace \nheater/air conditioner switch \n(Para 17-9).\n(7) Turn heater/air conditioner switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10)\n(8) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (9) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Remove seat (Para 16-12).(2) Set multimeter to volts dc. (3) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nthermostat where light green wire \nconnects. (4) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (5) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42) . (6) Turn engine start switch to START \nposition, START ENGINE \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (7) Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nA/C (TM 10-3930-673-10)(a) If voltage is present, go to Step 5 \nof this fault. (b) If voltage is not present, repair light \ngreen wire (Para 8-48).", "snippet": " Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nA/C (TM 10-3930-673-10)(a) If 24 vdc is present, go to Step 4 of \nthis fault. (b) If 24 vdc is not present, replace \nheater/air conditioner switch \n(Para 17-9)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p505_chunk_2", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 505, "text": "-673-10). (7) Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nA/C (TM 10-3930-673-10)(a) If voltage is present, go to Step 5 \nof this fault. (b) If voltage is not present, repair light \ngreen wire (Para 8-48).\n(8) Turn heater/air conditioner switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10)\n(9) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (10) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42) . TR01110", "snippet": "-673-10). (7) Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nA/C (TM 10-3930-673-10)(a) If voltage is present, go to Step 5 \nof this fault. (b) If voltage is not present, repair light \ngreen wire (Para 8-48)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p506_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 506, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-45430. AIR CONDITIONER DO ES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nGreen light extinguished. Defrost fans OK. Wire 80 OK.Heater/air conditioner switch OK. Light green wire OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nThermostat faulty. Low pressure cut-off switch faulty. Dark green wire faulty. High pressure cut-off switch faulty.Wire 81 faulty. A/C compressor clutch faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage Test.STE/ICE-R #89. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, thermostat \nis faulty. KNOWN INFO\nGreen light extinguished. Defrost fans OK. Wire 80 OK. Heater/air conditioner switch OK.Light green wire OK. Thermostat OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nLow pressure cut-off switch faulty. Dark green wire faulty. High pressure cut-off switch faulty.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-45430. AIR CONDITIONER DO ES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nGreen light extinguished. Defrost fans OK. Wire 80 OK.Heater/air conditioner switch OK. Light green wire OK. POSSIBLE "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p506_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 506, "text": " OK. Wire 80 OK. Heater/air conditioner switch OK.Light green wire OK. Thermostat OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nLow pressure cut-off switch faulty. Dark green wire faulty. High pressure cut-off switch faulty.\nWire 81 faulty.A/C compressor clutch faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage Test.STE/ICE-R #89. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, wire 81 is \nfaulty.5\nIs voltage present at \nthermostat where wire 81 \nconnects? YESNOReplace thermostat \n(Para 17-12). 6\nIs voltage present at low \npressure cut-off switch where \nwire 81 connects? YESNORepair wire 81 \n(Para 8-48).", "snippet": " OK. Wire 80 OK. Heater/air conditioner switch OK.Light green wire OK. Thermostat OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nLow pressure cut-off switch faulty. Dark green wire faulty. High pressure cut-off switch faulty."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p507_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 507, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-455WIRE 81\nLOW PRESSURE\u001f\nCUT-OFF SWITCHWIRE 81FEMALE\u001f\nCONNECTORMALE\u001f\nCONNECTORTHERMOSTATVOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nthermostat where wire 81 connects. (3) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (4) Connect negative battery to negative \nside of battery (Para 8-42). (5) Turn engine start switch to START \nposition, START ENGINE (TM 10-3930-673-10). (6) Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nA/C (TM 10-3930-673-10)(a) If voltage is present, go to Step 6 \nof this fault. (b) If voltage is not present, replace \nthermostat (Para 17-12). (7) Turn heater/air conditioner switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10)\n(8) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-455WIRE 81\nLOW PRESSURE\u001f\nCUT-OFF SWITCHWIRE 81FEMALE\u001f\nCONNECTORMALE\u001f\nCONNECTORTHERMOSTATVOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \n"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p507_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 507, "text": "ult. (b) If voltage is not present, replace \nthermostat (Para 17-12). (7) Turn heater/air conditioner switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10)\n(8) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10).\n(9) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc.(2) Disconnect male connector from \nfemale connector for wire 81. (3) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nlow pressure cut-off switch where wire 81 connects. (4) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (5) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). (6) Turn engine start switch to START \nposition, START ENGINE \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (7) Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nA/C (TM 10-3930-673-10)(a) If voltage is present, go to Step 7 \nof this fault. (b) If voltage is not present, repair \nwire 81 (Para 8-48).", "snippet": "ult. (b) If voltage is not present, replace \nthermostat (Para 17-12). (7) Turn heater/air conditioner switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10)\n(8) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p507_chunk_2", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 507, "text": "M 10-3930-673-10). (7) Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nA/C (TM 10-3930-673-10)(a) If voltage is present, go to Step 7 \nof this fault. (b) If voltage is not present, repair \nwire 81 (Para 8-48).\n(8) Turn heater/air conditioner switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10)\n(9) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (10) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). (11) Connect male connector to female \nconnector for wire 81. TR01111", "snippet": "M 10-3930-673-10). (7) Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nA/C (TM 10-3930-673-10)(a) If voltage is present, go to Step 7 \nof this fault. (b) If voltage is not present, repair \nwire 81 (Para 8-48)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p508_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 508, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-45630. AIR CONDITIONER DO ES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nGreen light extinguished. Defrost fans OK. Wire 80 OK.Heater/air conditioner switch OK. Light green wire OK. Thermostat OK.Wire 81 OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nLow pressure cut-off switch faulty.Dark green wire faulty.High pressure cut-off switch faulty. Wire 81 faulty. A/C compressor clutch faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage Test. STE/ICE-R #89. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, low \npressure cut-off switch is faulty. KNOWN INFO\nGreen light extinguished. Defrost fans OK. Wire 80 OK. Heater/air conditioner switch OK.Light green wire OK. Thermostat OK. Wire 81 OK.Low pressure cut-off switch OK\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nDark green wire faulty.High pressure cut-off switch faulty.Wire 81 faulty.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-45630. AIR CONDITIONER DO ES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nGreen light extinguished. Defrost fans OK. Wire 80 OK.Heater/air conditioner switch OK. Light green wire OK. Thermosta"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p508_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 508, "text": "ter/air conditioner switch OK.Light green wire OK. Thermostat OK. Wire 81 OK.Low pressure cut-off switch OK\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nDark green wire faulty.High pressure cut-off switch faulty.Wire 81 faulty.\nA/C compressor clutch faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage Test. STE/ICE-R #89. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, dark green \nwire is faulty.7\nIs voltage present at low \npressure cut-off switch where \ndark green wi re connects? YESNONotify Direct \nSupport to replace \nlow pressure cut-off \nswitch. 8\nIs voltage present at high \npressure cut-off switch where \ndark green wi re connects? YESNORepair dark green \nwire (Para 8-48).", "snippet": "ter/air conditioner switch OK.Light green wire OK. Thermostat OK. Wire 81 OK.Low pressure cut-off switch OK\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nDark green wire faulty.High pressure cut-off switch faulty.Wire 81 faulty."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p509_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 509, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-457LOW PRESSURE\u001f\nCUT-OFF SWITCHHIGH PRESSURE\u001f\nCUT-OFF SWITCHDARK GREEN \u001f\nWIREFEMALE\u001f\nCONNECTORMALE\u001f\nCONNECTORVOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Disconnect male connector from \nfemale connector for dark green wire at low pressure auto-off switch. (3) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nlow pressure cut-off switch where dark green wire connects. (4) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (5) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). (6) Turn engine start switch to START \nposition, START ENGINE (TM 10-3930-673-10). (7) Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nA/C (TM 10-3930-673-10)(a) If voltage is present, go to Step 8 \nof this fault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-457LOW PRESSURE\u001f\nCUT-OFF SWITCHHIGH PRESSURE\u001f\nCUT-OFF SWITCHDARK GREEN \u001f\nWIREFEMALE\u001f\nCONNECTORMALE\u001f\nCONNECTORVOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Disconnect male conn"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p509_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 509, "text": "Turn engine start switch to START \nposition, START ENGINE (TM 10-3930-673-10). (7) Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nA/C (TM 10-3930-673-10)(a) If voltage is present, go to Step 8 \nof this fault.\n(b) If voltage is not present, notify \nDirect Suport to replace low \npressure cut-off switch. (8) Turn heater/air conditioner switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10)\n(9) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (10) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). (11) Connect male connector to female \nconnector. VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc.(2) Disconnect male connector from \nfemale connector. (3) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nhigh pressure cut-off switch where dark \ngreen wire connects. (4) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (5) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). (6) Turn engine start switch to START \nposition, START ENGINE (TM 10-3930-673-10).", "snippet": "Turn engine start switch to START \nposition, START ENGINE (TM 10-3930-673-10). (7) Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nA/C (TM 10-3930-673-10)(a) If voltage is present, go to Step 8 \nof this fault."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p509_chunk_2", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 509, "text": "negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (5) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). (6) Turn engine start switch to START \nposition, START ENGINE (TM 10-3930-673-10).\n(7) Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nA/C (TM 10-3930-673-10)(a) If voltage is present, go to Step 9 \nof this fault. (b) If voltage is not present, repair dark \ngreen wire (Para 8-48). (8) Turn heater/air conditioner switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10)\n(9) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (10) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). (11) Connect male connector to female \nconnector for dark green wire at high pressure cut-off switch. TR01112", "snippet": "negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (5) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). (6) Turn engine start switch to START \nposition, START ENGINE (TM 10-3930-673-10)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p510_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 510, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-45830. AIR CONDITIONER DO ES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nGreen light extinguished. Defrost fans OK. Wire 80 OK.Heater/air conditioner switch OK. Light green wire OK. Thermostat OK.Wire 81 OK. Low pressure cut-off switch OK\nDark green wire OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHigh pressure cut-off switch faulty. Wire 81 faulty. A/C compressor clutch faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage Test. STE/ICE-R #89. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, high \npressure cut-off switch is faulty. KNOWN INFO\nGreen light extinguished. Defrost fans OK. Wire 80 OK. Heater/air conditioner switch OK.Light green wire OK. Thermostat OK. Wire 81 OK.Low pressure cut-off switch OK\nDark green wire OK. High pressure cut-off switch OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 81 faulty.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-45830. AIR CONDITIONER DO ES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nGreen light extinguished. Defrost fans OK. Wire 80 OK.Heater/air conditioner switch OK. Light green wire OK. Thermosta"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p510_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 510, "text": "K. Heater/air conditioner switch OK.Light green wire OK. Thermostat OK. Wire 81 OK.Low pressure cut-off switch OK\nDark green wire OK. High pressure cut-off switch OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 81 faulty.\nA/C compressor clutch faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, wire 81 is \nfaulty.9\nIs voltage present at high \npressure cut-off switch where \nwire 81 connects? YESNONotify Direct \nSupport to replace \nhigh pressure cut-\noff switch. 10\nIs voltage present at A/C \ncompressor clutch where wire \n81 connects? YESNORepair wire 81 \n(Para 8-48). Notify Direct \nSupport to replace \nA/C compressor \nclutch.", "snippet": "K. Heater/air conditioner switch OK.Light green wire OK. Thermostat OK. Wire 81 OK.Low pressure cut-off switch OK\nDark green wire OK. High pressure cut-off switch OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWire 81 faulty."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p511_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 511, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-459WIRE 81\nA/C COMPRESSOR\u001f\nCLUTCHFEMALE\u001f\nCONNECTOR\nMALE\u001f\nCONNECTORHIGH PRESSURE\u001f\nCUT-OFF SWITCHFEMALE\u001f\nCONNECTORMALE\u001f\nCONNECTOR\nWIRE 81VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Disconnect male connector from \nfemale connector for wire 81 at high pressure cut-off switch. (3) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nhigh pressure cut-off switch where wire 81 connects. (4) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (5) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). (6) Turn engine start switch to START \nposition, START ENGINE (TM 10-3930-673-10). (7) Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nA/C (TM 10-3930-673-10)(a) If voltage is present, go to Step 10 \nof this fault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-459WIRE 81\nA/C COMPRESSOR\u001f\nCLUTCHFEMALE\u001f\nCONNECTOR\nMALE\u001f\nCONNECTORHIGH PRESSURE\u001f\nCUT-OFF SWITCHFEMALE\u001f\nCONNECTORMALE\u001f\nCONNECTOR\nWIRE 81VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p511_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 511, "text": "urn engine start switch to START \nposition, START ENGINE (TM 10-3930-673-10). (7) Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nA/C (TM 10-3930-673-10)(a) If voltage is present, go to Step 10 \nof this fault.\n(b) If voltage is not present, notify \nDirect Support to replace high \npressure cut-off switch. (8) Turn heater/air conditioner switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10)\n(9) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (10) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). (11) Connect male connector to female \nconnector for wire 81 at high pressure \ncut-off switch. VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc.(2) Disconnect male connector from \nfemale connector for wire 81. (3) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nA/C compressor clutch where wire 81 connects. (4) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (5) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42).", "snippet": "urn engine start switch to START \nposition, START ENGINE (TM 10-3930-673-10). (7) Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nA/C (TM 10-3930-673-10)(a) If voltage is present, go to Step 10 \nof this fault."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p511_chunk_2", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 511, "text": " lead (+) to \nA/C compressor clutch where wire 81 connects. (4) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (5) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42).\n(6) Turn engine start switch to START \nposition, START ENGINE \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (7) Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nA/C (TM 10-3930-673-10)(a) If voltage is present, notify Direct \nSupport to replace A/C compressor \nclutch. (b) If voltage is not present, repair \nwire 81 (Para 8-48). (8) Turn heater/air conditioner switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10)\n(9) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). (10) Connect male connector to female \nconnector for wire 81. (11) Install battery cover (Para 8-42). TR01113", "snippet": " lead (+) to \nA/C compressor clutch where wire 81 connects. (4) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (5) Connect negative battery cable to \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p512_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 512, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-4602\n31. CAB BLOWER DOES NOT OPERATE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nMultimeter, Digital (Item 9, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (TM 10-3930-673-10)Parking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)Personnel Required\nTwo\nReferences\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nHeater/air conditioner switch OK. Defrost fans OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nJumper wires faulty.Cab blower switch faulty.Fan speed wires faulty. Fan resistor pack faulty. Fan motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #89.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-4602\n31. CAB BLOWER DOES NOT OPERATE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nMultimete"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p512_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 512, "text": "tch OK. Defrost fans OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nJumper wires faulty.Cab blower switch faulty.Fan speed wires faulty. Fan resistor pack faulty. Fan motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #89.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs green light illuminated on \ncab blower switch?START\nYESNOGo to Step 2 of this \nfault. Go to Step 3 of this fault.", "snippet": "tch OK. Defrost fans OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nJumper wires faulty.Cab blower switch faulty.Fan speed wires faulty. Fan resistor pack faulty. Fan motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #89."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p513_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 513, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-461HEATER/ \u001f\nAIR CONDITIONER \u001f\nSWITCHCAB BLOWER SWITCHENGINE START\u001f\nSWITCH\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).\n(2) Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nHEAT (TM 10-3930-673-10).\n(3) Cycle through all fan speeds, High, \nMedium, Low (TM 10-3930-673-10).\n(a) If light is illuminated, go to Step 3 \nof this fault.\n(b) If light is not illuminated, go to \nStep 2 of this fault.\n(4) Turn heater/air conditioner switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).\n(5) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10).\nTR01114", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-461HEATER/ \u001f\nAIR CONDITIONER \u001f\nSWITCHCAB BLOWER SWITCHENGINE START\u001f\nSWITCH\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE \n(TM 10-3930-67"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p514_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 514, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-46231. CAB BLOWER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nHeater/air conditioner switch OK.\nDefrost fans OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nJumper wires faulty.\nCab blower switch faulty.\nFan speed wires faulty.\nFan resistor pack faulty.Fan motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test.\nSTE/ICE-R #89.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, jumper \nwire is faulty.2\nIs voltage present at cab \nblower switch where jumper \nwire (pink wire) connects \nheater/air conditioner switch and cab blower switch?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.\nYESNORepair jumper wire \n(Para 8-48).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-46231. CAB BLOWER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nHeater/air conditioner switch OK.\nDefrost fans OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nJumper wires faulty.\nCab blower switch faulty.\nFan spee"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p515_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 515, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-4638\n71\n42\n53\n6SCREWCAB BLOWER \u001f\nSWITCH\nPANEL\nHEATER/\u001f\nAIR CONDITIONER\u001f\nSWITCHCAB BLOWER SWITCHENGINE\u001f\nSTART SWITCH• Remove rings, bracelets, watches, necklac es, and any other jewelry before working around vehicle. Jewelry can catch on \nequipment and cause injury or short ac ross electrical circuit and cause severe burns or electrical shock. • Battery acid is harmful to skin and eyes. Always  wear eye protection when working with batteries. VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Remove battery cover (Para 8-42). (2) Disconnect negative battery cable from \nnegative side of battery (Para 8-42). (3) Remove four screws from heater and \nair conditioner temperature control \npanel. (4) Raise heater and air conditioner \ntemperature control panel out to reach heater/air conditioner switch.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-4638\n71\n42\n53\n6SCREWCAB BLOWER \u001f\nSWITCH\nPANEL\nHEATER/\u001f\nAIR CONDITIONER\u001f\nSWITCHCAB BLOWER SWITCHENGINE\u001f\nSTART SWITCH• Remove rings, bracelets, watches, necklac es, and any other j"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p515_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 515, "text": "a 8-42). (3) Remove four screws from heater and \nair conditioner temperature control \npanel. (4) Raise heater and air conditioner \ntemperature control panel out to reach heater/air conditioner switch.\n(5) Set multimeter to volts dc. (6) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \ncab blower switch where jumper wires connect pins 7, 8 and/or 5. (7) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (8) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (9) Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nHEAT (TM 10-3930-673-10).(a) If voltage is present, go to Step 3 \nof this fault. (b) If voltage is not present, repair \njumper wire (Para 8-48). (10) Turn heater/air conditioner switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10)\n(11) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). TR01115", "snippet": "a 8-42). (3) Remove four screws from heater and \nair conditioner temperature control \npanel. (4) Raise heater and air conditioner \ntemperature control panel out to reach heater/air conditioner switch."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p516_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 516, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-46431. CAB BLOWER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHeater/air conditioner switch OK. Defrost fans OK. Jumper wires OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nCab blower switch faulty. Fan speed wires faulty. Fan resistor pack faulty.Fan motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test. STE/ICE-R #89. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, fan switch \nis faulty. KNOWN INFO\nHeater/air conditioner switch OK. Defrost fans OK. Jumper wires OK. Cab blower switch OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFan speed wires faulty. Fan resistor pack faulty. Fan motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test.STE/ICE-R #89. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, fan speed \nwire is faulty.3\nIs voltage present at fan \nswitch where high (orange \nwire), medium (light blue wire), and low (yellow wire) \nconnects?", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-46431. CAB BLOWER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHeater/air conditioner switch OK. Defrost fans OK. Jumper wires OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nCab blower switch faulty. Fan speed wi"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p516_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 516, "text": " #89. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, fan speed \nwire is faulty.3\nIs voltage present at fan \nswitch where high (orange \nwire), medium (light blue wire), and low (yellow wire) \nconnects?\nYESNOReplace fan switch \n(Para 17-9). 4\nIs voltage present at fan \nresistor pack where high \n(orange wire), medium (light blue wire), and low (yellow \nwire) connects? YESNORepair faulty fan \nspeed wire \n(Para 8-48).", "snippet": " #89. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, fan speed \nwire is faulty.3\nIs voltage present at fan \nswitch where high (orange \nwire), medium (light blue wire), and low (yellow wire) \nconnects?"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p517_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 517, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-465ORANGE\u001f\nWIRELT BLUE\u001f\nWIREYELLOW\u001f\nWIRE\nRESISTOR \u001f\nPACKVOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nfan switch where high (orange wire), medium (light blue wire), low (yellow wire) connects pins 1, 3, or 6. (3) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (4) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE (TM 10-3930-673-10). (5) Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nHeat (TM 10-3930-673-10)\n(6) Cycle through all fan speeds, High, \nMedium, Low (TM 10-3930-673-10). (a) If voltage is present, go to Step 4 \nof this fault. (b) If voltage is not present, replace \nfan switch (Para 17-9).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-465ORANGE\u001f\nWIRELT BLUE\u001f\nWIREYELLOW\u001f\nWIRE\nRESISTOR \u001f\nPACKVOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Set multimeter to volts dc. (2) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nfan switch where high (orange wi"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p517_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 517, "text": "73-10)\n(6) Cycle through all fan speeds, High, \nMedium, Low (TM 10-3930-673-10). (a) If voltage is present, go to Step 4 \nof this fault. (b) If voltage is not present, replace \nfan switch (Para 17-9).\n(7) Turn heater/air conditioner switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10)\n(8) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10). VOLTAGE TEST\n(1) Remove seat (Para 16-12). (2) Set multimeter to volts dc. (3) Connect multimeter positive lead (+) to \nfan Resistor Pack connector J1 where \nhigh (orange wire), medium (light blue \nwire), low (yellow wire) connects. (4) Connect multimeter negative lead (–) to \nknown good ground. (5) Turn engine start switch to ON position, \nBUT DO NOT START ENGINE \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (6) Turn heater/air conditioner switch to \nHeat (TM 10-3930-673-10)\n(7) Cycle through all the fan speeds, High, \nMedium, Low (TM 10-3930-673-10).(a) If voltage is present, go to Step 5 \nof this fault. (b) If voltage is not present, repair \nfaulty wire (Para 8-48).", "snippet": "73-10)\n(6) Cycle through all fan speeds, High, \nMedium, Low (TM 10-3930-673-10). (a) If voltage is present, go to Step 4 \nof this fault. (b) If voltage is not present, replace \nfan switch (Para 17-9)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p517_chunk_2", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 517, "text": "10)\n(7) Cycle through all the fan speeds, High, \nMedium, Low (TM 10-3930-673-10).(a) If voltage is present, go to Step 5 \nof this fault. (b) If voltage is not present, repair \nfaulty wire (Para 8-48).\n(8) Turn heater/air conditioner switch OFF \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (9) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10).TR01116", "snippet": "10)\n(7) Cycle through all the fan speeds, High, \nMedium, Low (TM 10-3930-673-10).(a) If voltage is present, go to Step 5 \nof this fault. (b) If voltage is not present, repair \nfaulty wire (Para 8-48)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p518_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 518, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-46631. CAB BLOWER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nHeater/air conditioner switch OK.\nDefrost fans OK.\nJumper wires OK.Cab blower switch OK.\nFan speed wire OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFan resistor pack faulty.\nFan motor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVoltage test.\nSTE/ICE-R #89.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf voltage is not present, fan \nresistor pack is faulty.5\nIs voltage present at fan \nresistor pack connector \nwhere orange wire connects?\nYESNONotify Direct \nSupport to replace fan resistor pack.\nReplace fan motor (Para 17-10).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-46631. CAB BLOWER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nHeater/air conditioner switch OK.\nDefrost fans OK.\nJumper wires OK.Cab blower switch OK.\nFan speed wire OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEM"}
