{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p666_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 666, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6142 \n1. STARTER DOES NOT TURN ENGINE, TURNS ENGINE SLOWLY\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nAll gages OK. Voltmeter reads low. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nBattery terminals corroded, cable \nloose. Battery faulty. Starting system malfunctioning.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where \ntroubleshooting continues.1\nAre battery terminals clean, \ncorrosion free?", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6142 \n1. STARTER DOES NOT TURN ENGINE, TURNS ENGINE SLOWLY\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p666_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 666, "text": "ctioning.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where \ntroubleshooting continues.1\nAre battery terminals clean, \ncorrosion free?\nAre battery \ncable connections properly tightened?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOClean battery \nterminals and tighten connections \n(Para 8-44).", "snippet": "ctioning.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where \ntroubleshooting continues.1\nAre battery terminals clean, \ncorrosion free?"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p667_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 667, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-615CONNECTIONSCOVER\nCOVER\nCONNECTIONSWARNING\n• Drycleaning solvent P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, \nsparks, or open flame. Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F (59 degrees C). Use on ly in a well-ventilated area. Inhaling vap ors \nover a period of time can cause headache and drowsiness. Use gloves to prevent irrita tion or inflammation of the skin. Solvent \nabsorbed through the skin can result in internal disorders. If contact occurs, wash the affected area with water for 15 minutes . For \neyes, flush with water and then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-615CONNECTIONSCOVER\nCOVER\nCONNECTIONSWARNING\n• Drycleaning solvent P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, \nspar"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p667_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 667, "text": "or 15 minutes . For \neyes, flush with water and then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment.\nRefer to \nTM9-247 for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous. Make certain the air stream is directed away from user and other personnel in  \nthe area. To prevent injury, user must wear protec tive goggles or face shield when using compressed air. • Battery acid is harmful to skin and eyes. Always  wear eye protection when working with batteries. CA\nWashing oil seals, electrical cables and flexible hoses with dry \ncleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damage or \ndestroy materials. BATTERY TERMINALS AND CABLE \nCONNECTIONS VISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck battery terminals for corrosion, and check tightness of cable connections.", "snippet": "or 15 minutes . For \neyes, flush with water and then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p667_chunk_2", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 667, "text": "ral spirits will cause serious damage or \ndestroy materials. BATTERY TERMINALS AND CABLE \nCONNECTIONS VISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck battery terminals for corrosion, and check tightness of cable connections.\n(a) If battery terminals are clean, and \ncable connections properly tightened, go to Step 2 of this fault. (b) If terminals are corroded, clean \nterminals, If cabl e connections are \nloose, tighten connections. Restart vehicle. TR01446", "snippet": "ral spirits will cause serious damage or \ndestroy materials. BATTERY TERMINALS AND CABLE \nCONNECTIONS VISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck battery terminals for corrosion, and check tightness of cable connections."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p668_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 668, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6161. STARTER DOES NOT TURN ENGINE, TURNS ENGINE SLOWLY (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nAll gages OK. Voltmeter reads low. Battery terminals, cable \nconnections OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nBattery faulty.Starting system malfunctioning.TEST OPTIONS\nSTE/ICE test #67. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf battery voltage below required \n24 volts, battery will not turn over engine. KNOWN INFO\nAll gages OK.Voltmeter reads low. Battery terminals, cable \nconnections OK. Battery OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nStarting system malfunctioning.TEST OPTIONS\nSTE/ICE Tests #68, 69, 70, 71, 72. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf starting system malfunctioning, \nengine will not start.2\nIs battery voltage at 24 volts \nor higher?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6161. STARTER DOES NOT TURN ENGINE, TURNS ENGINE SLOWLY (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nAll gages OK. Voltmeter reads low. Battery terminals, cable \nconnections OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nBattery "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p668_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 668, "text": "ONS\nSTE/ICE Tests #68, 69, 70, 71, 72. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf starting system malfunctioning, \nengine will not start.2\nIs battery voltage at 24 volts \nor higher?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.\nYESNO\n3\nDoes sequence of STE/ICE \ntests indicate proper starter \nsystem operation? YESNOReplace faulty \ncomponents as \nnecessary (Para 8-42) and (Para 8-43).Service or replace battery as necessary \n(Para 8-42). If fault not \ncorrected, notify \nDirect Support.", "snippet": "ONS\nSTE/ICE Tests #68, 69, 70, 71, 72. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf starting system malfunctioning, \nengine will not start.2\nIs battery voltage at 24 volts \nor higher?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p669_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 669, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-617NEGATIVE\u001f\nBATTERY\u001f\nCABLECOVER\nBATTERY\nNEGATIVE\u001f\nBATTERY\u001f\nCABLECOVER\nBATTERY\nPOSITIVE\u001f\nBATTERY\u001f\nCABLEPOSITIVE LEAD\nPOSITIVE LEAD\nSTARTER MOTORSTARTER\u001f\nSOLENOIDWARNING\n• Remove rings, bracelets, watc hes, necklaces, and any other \njewelry before working around vehi cle. Jewelry can catch on \nequipment and cause injury or short across electr ical circuit \nand cause severe burns or electrical shock. • Battery acid is harmful to skin and eyes. Always wear eye \nprotection when working with batteries. BATTERY VOLTAGE WITH STE/ICE\nUse STE/ICE Test #67 to test voltage of \nbattery. Or, connect multimeter to positive \nbattery cable and known good ground to test \nvoltage. Battery voltage should be 24 volts or higher.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-617NEGATIVE\u001f\nBATTERY\u001f\nCABLECOVER\nBATTERY\nNEGATIVE\u001f\nBATTERY\u001f\nCABLECOVER\nBATTERY\nPOSITIVE\u001f\nBATTERY\u001f\nCABLEPOSITIVE LEAD\nPOSITIVE LEAD\nSTARTER MOTORSTARTER\u001f\nSOLENOIDWARNING\n• Remove "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p669_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 669, "text": "E WITH STE/ICE\nUse STE/ICE Test #67 to test voltage of \nbattery. Or, connect multimeter to positive \nbattery cable and known good ground to test \nvoltage. Battery voltage should be 24 volts or higher.\n(a) If voltage is 24 volts or higher, go \nto Step 3 of this fault. (b) If voltage is below 24 volts, service \nor replace battery as necessary (Para 8-42). Restart vehicle. STE/ICE TESTS ON STARTING SYSTEM\n(1) Perform STE/ICE Test #68. Starter \nmotor voltage should test between 18 and 27.5 volts. If voltage is not within \nrange, replace positive cable \n(Para 8-42). (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #69. Negative \ncable voltage drop should test between 0 and 3 volts. If voltage drop is greater, replace negative cable (Para 8-42). (3) Perform STE/ICE Test #70. Solenoid \nvoltage should test between 18 and 27.5 volts. If voltage is less, replace \nsolenoid (Para 8-3). (4) Perform STE/ICE Test #89. Solenoid \nvoltage drop should test less than 0.3 volts.", "snippet": "E WITH STE/ICE\nUse STE/ICE Test #67 to test voltage of \nbattery. Or, connect multimeter to positive \nbattery cable and known good ground to test \nvoltage. Battery voltage should be 24 volts or higher."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p669_chunk_2", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 669, "text": "st #70. Solenoid \nvoltage should test between 18 and 27.5 volts. If voltage is less, replace \nsolenoid (Para 8-3). (4) Perform STE/ICE Test #89. Solenoid \nvoltage drop should test less than 0.3 volts.\nIf voltage drop is greater, \nreplace solenoid (Para 8-3). (5) Perform STE/ICE Test #71. Starter \ncurrent average should test between 0 and 250 amps. If current is greater, replace starter (Para 8-3). (6) Perform STE/ICE Test #72. Starter \ncurrent first peak should test between 300 and 1000 amps. If peak is not \nwithin range, service or replace battery \n(Para 8-42). TR01447", "snippet": "st #70. Solenoid \nvoltage should test between 18 and 27.5 volts. If voltage is less, replace \nsolenoid (Para 8-3). (4) Perform STE/ICE Test #89. Solenoid \nvoltage drop should test less than 0.3 volts."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p670_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 670, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6182\n2. ENGINE CRANKS BUT DOES NOT START\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nMultimeter (Item 9, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFuel level low. Fuel shutoff solenoid faulty. Air intake and inlet tube blocked.Air cleaner assembly blocked. Turbocharger air intake and/or \nexhaust blocked. Transfer pump and/or fuel filter \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6182\n2. ENGINE CRANKS BUT DOES NOT START\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nMultimeter (Item 9, Appendix F)\nEqui"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p670_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 670, "text": "lenoid faulty. Air intake and inlet tube blocked.Air cleaner assembly blocked. Turbocharger air intake and/or \nexhaust blocked. Transfer pump and/or fuel filter \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where \ntroubleshooting continues.1\nIs there fuel in fuel tank?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOAdd fuel as \nnecessary (Para \n5-9).", "snippet": "lenoid faulty. Air intake and inlet tube blocked.Air cleaner assembly blocked. Turbocharger air intake and/or \nexhaust blocked. Transfer pump and/or fuel filter \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p671_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 671, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-619FUEL LEVEL GAGEWARNING\n• Fuel is very flammable and can explode easily. To avoid serious injury or death, keep fuel aw ay from open fire and keep fire \nextinguisher within easy reach when working with fuel. Do not work on fuel system when engine is hot. Fuel can be ignited by ho t \nengine. When working with fuel, post signs that  read NO SMOKING WITHIN 50 FEET (15 m). • Pressure test procedure results in fuel under high pressure. Be sure that pressure test device is connected properly and use \nsafety shield during test. Failure to do so may result in injury to personnel. • P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, sparks or open flame . Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F ( 59 degrees C).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-619FUEL LEVEL GAGEWARNING\n• Fuel is very flammable and can explode easily. To avoid serious injury or death, keep fuel aw ay from open fire and keep fire \nextinguisher within eas"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p671_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 671, "text": "o personnel. • P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, sparks or open flame . Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F ( 59 degrees C).\nUse only in a well ventilated area. Inhaling vapors over a period of ti me \ncan cause headache and drowsiness. Use gloves  to prevent irritation or inflammation of the skin. Solvent absorbed through the \nskin can result in internal disorders. If contact occurs, wash the affected area with water for 15 minutes. For eyes, flush wit h water \nand then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment. Refer to \nTM9-247 for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous.", "snippet": "o personnel. • P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, sparks or open flame . Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F ( 59 degrees C)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p671_chunk_2", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 671, "text": " for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous.\nMake certain the air stream is directed away from user and other personnel in  \nthe area. To prevent injury, user must wear protec tive goggles or face shield when using compressed air. CAUTION\nWashing oil seals, electrical cables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. ENGINE FUEL LEVEL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Check engine fuel level gage to \ndetermine if there is fuel in fuel tank. (2) Fuel level should be above gage E \nmark. (a) If fuel level is above the E mark, go \nto Step 2 of this fault. (b) If fuel level is near or at the E mark, \nadd fuel as necessary (Para 5-9). Go to Step (3) below. (3) Start engine (TM 10-3930-673-10). If \nengine fails to start, go to Step 2 of this fault. TR01183", "snippet": " for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p672_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 672, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6202. ENGINE CRANKS BUT DOES NOT START (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nFuel level OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFuel shutoff solenoid faulty.\nAir intake and inlet tube blocked.Air cleaner assembly blocked.\nTurbocharger air intake and/or \nexhaust blocked.\nTransfer pump and/or fuel filter \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nResistance and draw test.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf resistance and current draw are \nlow, fuel shutoff solenoid faulty.2\nIs resistance and current \ndraw at fuel shutoff valve \nsolenoid within acceptable limits?\nYESNOFaulty solenoid, notify \nDirect Support.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6202. ENGINE CRANKS BUT DOES NOT START (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nFuel level OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFuel shutoff solenoid faulty.\nAir intake and inlet tube blocked.Air cleaner assembly bl"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p673_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 673, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-621FUEL SHUTOFF SOLENOID \nRESISTANCE AND \nCURRENT DRAW TESTS\n AT FUEL SHUTOFF VALVE SOLENOID\n(1) Set multimeter to ohms to test \nresistance at fuel shutoff solenoid.\n(a) If resistance reads 29.5 ohms ±2.5, \ngo to Step (2) below.\n(b) If resistance is not within \nacceptable limits , notify Direct \nSupport.\n(2) Set multimeter to amps to test current \ndraw at fuel shutoff solenoid.\n(a) If current draw reads \napproximately 1 ampere, go to \nStep (3) below.\n(b) If reading is not correct, notify \nDirect Support.\n(3) Start engine (TM 10-3930-673-10). If \nengine fails to start, go to Step 3 of this \nfault.\nTR01184", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-621FUEL SHUTOFF SOLENOID \nRESISTANCE AND \nCURRENT DRAW TESTS\n AT FUEL SHUTOFF VALVE SOLENOID\n(1) Set multimeter to ohms to test \nresistance at fuel shutoff solenoid.\n(a) If resis"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p674_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 674, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6222. ENGINE CRANKS BUT DOES NOT START (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nFuel level OK. Fuel shutoff solenoid OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir intake and inlet tube blocked. Air cleaner assembly blocked. Turbocharger air intake and/or \nexhaust blocked. Transfer pump and/or fuel filter \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf air intake and inlet tube blocked, \nengine will not start. KNOWN INFO\nFuel level OK. Fuel shutoff solenoid OK. Air intake and inlet tube OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir cleaner assembly blocked. Turbocharger air intake and/or \nexhaust blocked. Transfer pump and/or fuel filter \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf air cleaner is blocked, engine will \nnot start.3\nAre the air intake and inlet \ntube clear and clean?", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6222. ENGINE CRANKS BUT DOES NOT START (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nFuel level OK. Fuel shutoff solenoid OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir intake and inlet tube blocked. Air cleaner assembly block"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p674_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 674, "text": "ed. Transfer pump and/or fuel filter \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf air cleaner is blocked, engine will \nnot start.3\nAre the air intake and inlet \ntube clear and clean?\nYESNOClean air intake \nand inlet tube \n(Para 5-5). 4\nIs air cleaner clear and clean? YESNOClean and/or \nreplace elements \n(Para 5-4).", "snippet": "ed. Transfer pump and/or fuel filter \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf air cleaner is blocked, engine will \nnot start.3\nAre the air intake and inlet \ntube clear and clean?"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p675_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 675, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-623\nAIR INTAKE AND INLET TUBE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Visually inspect air intake and inlet tube \nfor obstructions. (a) If air intake and inlet tube are clear \nand clean, go to Step 4 of this fault. (b) If air intake and tube are blocked, \nclean air intake and tube (Para 5-5). Go to Step (2) below. (2) Start engine (TM 10-3930-673-10). If \nengine fails to start, go to Step 4 of this fault. AIR CLEANER ASSEMBLY\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Remove air cleaner and elements \n(Para 5-4). (2) Visually inspect air cleaner elements \nfor obstructions.(a) If elements are clear and clean, go \nto Step 5 of this fault. (b) If elements and assembly are dirty \nand/or blocked, clean and/or \nreplace elements (Para 5-4). Go to \nStep (3) below. (3) Start engine (TM 10-3930-673-10).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-623\nAIR INTAKE AND INLET TUBE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Visually inspect air intake and inlet tube \nfor obstructions. (a) If air intake and inlet tube are clear \nand clean, go to Ste"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p675_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 675, "text": " and clean, go \nto Step 5 of this fault. (b) If elements and assembly are dirty \nand/or blocked, clean and/or \nreplace elements (Para 5-4). Go to \nStep (3) below. (3) Start engine (TM 10-3930-673-10).\nIf \nengine fails to start, go to Step 5 of this fault. TR01185", "snippet": " and clean, go \nto Step 5 of this fault. (b) If elements and assembly are dirty \nand/or blocked, clean and/or \nreplace elements (Para 5-4). Go to \nStep (3) below. (3) Start engine (TM 10-3930-673-10)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p676_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 676, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6242. ENGINE CRANKS BUT DOES NOT START (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nFuel level OK. Fuel shutoff solenoid OK. Air intake and inlet tube OK.Air cleaner assembly OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTurbocharger air intake and/or \nexhaust blocked. Transfer pump and/or fuel filter \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf turbocharger air intake lines and \nexhaust are blocked, engine will not run smoothly. KNOWN INFO\nFuel level OK.Fuel shutoff solenoid OK. Air intake and inlet tube OK. Air cleaner assembly OK.Turbocharger air intake and/or \nexhaust OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTransfer pump and/or fuel filter \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6242. ENGINE CRANKS BUT DOES NOT START (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nFuel level OK. Fuel shutoff solenoid OK. Air intake and inlet tube OK.Air cleaner assembly OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTurboch"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p676_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 676, "text": "olenoid OK. Air intake and inlet tube OK. Air cleaner assembly OK.Turbocharger air intake and/or \nexhaust OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTransfer pump and/or fuel filter \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf transfer pump and/or fuel filter \nfaulty, fuel will not flow freely.5\nAre turbocharger air intake \nlines and engine exhaust free \nfrom obstructions? YESNOReplace blocked \nintake lines \n(Para 5-7) and/or exhaust \n(Para 6-3). 6\nDoes fuel flow freely through \ntransfer pump and fuel filter? YESNOReplace faulty transfer pump (Para 5-3) and/\nor filter elements (Para 5-15). If fault not corrected, notify Direct Support.", "snippet": "olenoid OK. Air intake and inlet tube OK. Air cleaner assembly OK.Turbocharger air intake and/or \nexhaust OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTransfer pump and/or fuel filter \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p677_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 677, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-625EXHAUST\nAIR INTAKE\nHOSE FITTING\nSECONDARY\u001f\nFILTER ELEMENTTRANSFER \u001f\nPUMPTURBOCHARGER AIR INTAKE LINES \nAND EXHAUST\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Remove turbocharger air intake \n(Para 5-7) and exhaust (Para 6-3). (2) Visually inspect intake and exhaust for \nobstructions. (a) If intake and exhaust are clear, go \nto Step 6 of this fault. (b) If intake and/or exhaust are \nblocked, replace intake (Para 5-7) and/or exhaust (Para 6-3). Go to \nStep (3) below. (3) Start engine (TM 10-3930-673-10). If \nengine fails to start, go to Step 6 of this fault. FUEL FLOW THROUGH FILTER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Loosen hose fitting at top of secondary \nfilter element (Para 5-15). (2) Crank engine for thirty seconds. (a) If fuel flows freely from fitting, \ntransfer pump and fuel filter OK.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-625EXHAUST\nAIR INTAKE\nHOSE FITTING\nSECONDARY\u001f\nFILTER ELEMENTTRANSFER \u001f\nPUMPTURBOCHARGER AIR INTAKE LINES \nAND EXHAUST\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Remove turbocharger air intake \n(Para "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p677_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 677, "text": "ISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Loosen hose fitting at top of secondary \nfilter element (Para 5-15). (2) Crank engine for thirty seconds. (a) If fuel flows freely from fitting, \ntransfer pump and fuel filter OK.\n(b) If fuel does not flow freely, replace \ntransfer pump (Para 5-3) and/or \nfilter elements (Para 5-15). Go to \nStep (3) below. (3) Start engine (TM 10-3930-673-10). If \nengine fails to start, notify Direct Support. TR01186", "snippet": "ISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Loosen hose fitting at top of secondary \nfilter element (Para 5-15). (2) Crank engine for thirty seconds. (a) If fuel flows freely from fitting, \ntransfer pump and fuel filter OK."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p678_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 678, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6262\n3. ENGINE STARTS BUT DOES NOT CONTINUE TO RUN\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine running (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFuel level low. Idle speed too low. Air intake and/or exhaust blocked.Air in fuel system. Fuel waxing (extremely cold \nweather). Fuel contaminated.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. STE/ICE-R.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6262\n3. ENGINE STARTS BUT DOES NOT CONTINUE TO RUN\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Append"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p678_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 678, "text": "E PROBLEMS\nFuel level low. Idle speed too low. Air intake and/or exhaust blocked.Air in fuel system. Fuel waxing (extremely cold \nweather). Fuel contaminated.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. STE/ICE-R.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs there fuel in fuel tank?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOAdd fuel as necessary \n(Para 5-9).", "snippet": "E PROBLEMS\nFuel level low. Idle speed too low. Air intake and/or exhaust blocked.Air in fuel system. Fuel waxing (extremely cold \nweather). Fuel contaminated.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. STE/ICE-R."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p679_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 679, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-627FUEL LEVEL GAGEWARNING\n• Fuel is very flammable and can explode easily. To avoid serious injury or death, keep fuel aw ay from open fire and keep fire \nextinguisher within easy reach when working with fuel. Do not work on fuel system when engine is hot. Fuel can be ignited by ho t \nengine. When working with fuel, post signs that  read NO SMOKING WITHIN 50 FEET (15 m). • Pressure test procedure results in fuel under high pressure. Be sure that pressure test device is connected properly and use \nsafety shield during test. Failure to do so may result in injury to personnel. • P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, sparks or open flame . Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F ( 59 degrees C).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-627FUEL LEVEL GAGEWARNING\n• Fuel is very flammable and can explode easily. To avoid serious injury or death, keep fuel aw ay from open fire and keep fire \nextinguisher within eas"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p679_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 679, "text": "o personnel. • P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, sparks or open flame . Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F ( 59 degrees C).\nUse only in a well ventilated area. Inhaling vapors over a period of ti me \ncan cause headache and drowsiness. Use gloves  to prevent irritation or inflammation of the skin. Solvent absorbed through the \nskin can result in internal disorders. If contact occurs, wash the affected area with water for 15 minutes. For eyes, flush wit h water \nand then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment. Refer to \nTM9-247 for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous.", "snippet": "o personnel. • P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, sparks or open flame . Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F ( 59 degrees C)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p679_chunk_2", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 679, "text": " for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous.\nMake certain the air stream is directed away from user and other personnel in  \nthe area. To prevent injury, user must wear protec tive goggles or face shield when using compressed air. CAUTION\nWashing oil seals, electrical cables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. ENGINE FUEL LEVEL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Check engine fuel level gage to \ndetermine if there is fuel in fuel tank. (2) Fuel level should be above gage E \nmark. (a) If fuel level is above E mark, go to \nStep 2 of this fault. (b) If fuel level is near or at E mark, \nadd fuel as necessary (Para 5-9). TR01187", "snippet": " for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p680_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 680, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6283. ENGINE STARTS BUT DOES NOT CONTINUE TO RUN (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nFuel level OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nIdle speed too low. Air intake and/or exhaust blocked.Air in fuel system. Fuel waxing (extremely cold \nweather). Fuel contaminated.TEST OPTIONS\nSTE/ICE-R. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf idle speed is too low, engine will \nnot continue to run. KNOWN INFO\nFuel level OK. Idle speed OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir intake and/or exhaust blocked.Air in fuel system.Fuel waxing (extremely cold \nweather). Fuel contaminated.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf air intake and/or exhaust \nblocked, engine will not continue to run.2\nIs idle speed adjusted \ncorrectly for accessories and \noperating environment \n(normally: 850 to 950 RPM)?", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6283. ENGINE STARTS BUT DOES NOT CONTINUE TO RUN (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nFuel level OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nIdle speed too low. Air intake and/or exhaust blocked.Air in fuel system. Fue"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p680_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 680, "text": ". REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf air intake and/or exhaust \nblocked, engine will not continue to run.2\nIs idle speed adjusted \ncorrectly for accessories and \noperating environment \n(normally: 850 to 950 RPM)?\nYESNOAdjust idle speed \nas necessary (Para 5-20). 3\nAre air intake and/or exhaust \nfree from obstructions and/or \nrestrictions? YESNOClean air intake and/or \nexhaust as necessary \n(Para 5-5, Para 5-4, and Para 6-3).", "snippet": ". REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf air intake and/or exhaust \nblocked, engine will not continue to run.2\nIs idle speed adjusted \ncorrectly for accessories and \noperating environment \n(normally: 850 to 950 RPM)?"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p681_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 681, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-629ACCELERATOR\u001f\nCABLE\nAIR INTAKE EXHAUSTIDLE SPEED\nSTE/ICE-R RPM CHECK\nUse STE/ICE to check engine RPM at idle \n(Para 5-20).\n(a) If idle RPM within 850 to 950 \nrange, go to Step 3 of this fault.\n(b) If idle RPM is not within specified \nrange, adjust idle speed by \nadjusting accelerator cable as \nnecessary (Para 5-20).\nAIR INTAKE AND EXHAUST\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect air in take and exhaust for \nobstructions and/or restrictions.\n(a) If air intake and exhaust are clean, \ngo to Step 4 of this fault.\n(b) If air intake and exhaust are \nblocked, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and clean \nintake cap and exhaust pipe (Para 5-5, Para 5-4, and Para 6-3).\nTR01188", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-629ACCELERATOR\u001f\nCABLE\nAIR INTAKE EXHAUSTIDLE SPEED\nSTE/ICE-R RPM CHECK\nUse STE/ICE to check engine RPM at idle \n(Para 5-20).\n(a) If idle RPM within 850 to 950 \nrange, go to Step "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p682_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 682, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6303. ENGINE STARTS BUT DOES NOT CONTINUE TO RUN (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nFuel level OK. Idle speed OK. Air intake and exhaust OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir in fuel system. Fuel waxing (extremely cold \nweather). Fuel contaminated.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf air is in fuel system, engine will \nnot run smoothly. KNOWN INFO\nFuel level OK. Idle speed OK. Air intake and exhaust OK. Fuel system OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFuel waxing (extremely cold \nweather). Fuel contaminated.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf fuel waxing occurs, engine will \nnot continue to run.4\nIs fuel system free from air in \nfuel lines? YESNOCheck fuel flow and \nbleed system as \nnecessary (Para 5-11). 5\nIs fuel climatized for \noperating environment?", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6303. ENGINE STARTS BUT DOES NOT CONTINUE TO RUN (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nFuel level OK. Idle speed OK. Air intake and exhaust OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir in fuel system. Fuel waxing (ex"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p682_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 682, "text": "curs, engine will \nnot continue to run.4\nIs fuel system free from air in \nfuel lines? YESNOCheck fuel flow and \nbleed system as \nnecessary (Para 5-11). 5\nIs fuel climatized for \noperating environment?\nYESNOInspect filter, clean \nsystem, replace \nfuel with climatized fuel (Para 5-15).", "snippet": "curs, engine will \nnot continue to run.4\nIs fuel system free from air in \nfuel lines? YESNOCheck fuel flow and \nbleed system as \nnecessary (Para 5-11). 5\nIs fuel climatized for \noperating environment?"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p683_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 683, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-631FUEL FILTER FUEL\u001f\nTRANSFER\u001fPUMPFUEL FLOW THROUGH FILTER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck flow of fuel through fuel filter \n(Para 5-11).\n(a) If fuel flows freely without air, go to \nStep 5 of this fault.\n(b) If air in fuel lines, check for air \nleaks, shut down engine \n(TM 10-3930-673-10), and bleed \nsystem (Para 5-11). \nFILTER AND FUEL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck fuel consistenc y in fuel filter \n(Para 5-15).\n(a) If fuel is thin and flows freely, go to \nStep 6 of this fault.\n(b) If fuel is thick and jelling, shut down \nengine (TM 10-3930-673-10), clean fuel system (Para 5-15) and \nreplace fuel with climatized fuel (Para 5-9).TR01189", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-631FUEL FILTER FUEL\u001f\nTRANSFER\u001fPUMPFUEL FLOW THROUGH FILTER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck flow of fuel through fuel filter \n(Para 5-11).\n(a) If fuel flows freely without air, go to \nSte"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p684_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 684, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6323. ENGINE STARTS BUT DOES NOT CONTINUE TO RUN (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nFuel level OK.\nIdle speed OK.\nAir intake and exhaust OK.Fuel system OK.\nFuel consistency OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFuel contaminated.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf fuel contaminated, engine will \njerk and/or stall.6\nIs fuel clean and free from \ncontaminants?\nYESNODrain and flush \nfuel tank, and \nreplace fuel (Para 5-9).\nRefer to the Electrical Troubleshooting Section to solve possible electrical \nproblems.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6323. ENGINE STARTS BUT DOES NOT CONTINUE TO RUN (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nFuel level OK.\nIdle speed OK.\nAir intake and exhaust OK.Fuel system OK.\nFuel consistency OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p685_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 685, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-633CONTAMINATED FUEL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Check for contaminated fuel by \noperating engine with known clean fuel \nfrom temporary supply tank. (a) Disconnect fuel line at fuel/water \nseparator, where line leads to fuel \ntransfer pump. (b) Place disconnected end of fuel line \nin temp tank of known clean fuel. (c) Start engine and run it for \napproximately 5 minutes to \ndetermine if engine performance \nimproves with known clean fuel. (2) If fuel in fuel tank proves clean, refer to \nthe Electrical Troubleshooting Section to continue. (3) If fuel in fuel tank is contaminated, shut \ndown engine (TM 10-3930-673-10), drain and flush \ntank, and replace fuel with clean fuel (Para 5-9).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-633CONTAMINATED FUEL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Check for contaminated fuel by \noperating engine with known clean fuel \nfrom temporary supply tank. (a) Disconnect fuel line at fuel/wa"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p685_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 685, "text": "the Electrical Troubleshooting Section to continue. (3) If fuel in fuel tank is contaminated, shut \ndown engine (TM 10-3930-673-10), drain and flush \ntank, and replace fuel with clean fuel (Para 5-9).\nLINE TO FUEL/WATER\u001f\nSEPARATORTRANSFER\u001f\nPUMPFUEL/WATER\u001f\nSEPARATOR\nLINE TO FUEL\u001f\nTRANSFER PUMP\nTR01190", "snippet": "the Electrical Troubleshooting Section to continue. (3) If fuel in fuel tank is contaminated, shut \ndown engine (TM 10-3930-673-10), drain and flush \ntank, and replace fuel with clean fuel (Para 5-9)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p686_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 686, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6342\n4. ENGINE STARTS BUT IDLES ROUGHLY\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine running (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nIdle speed too low. High pressure fuel leak. Air in fuel system.Fuel contaminated. Fuel transfer pump faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nSTE/ICE-R.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6342\n4. ENGINE STARTS BUT IDLES ROUGHLY\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nEquip"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p686_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 686, "text": "0-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nIdle speed too low. High pressure fuel leak. Air in fuel system.Fuel contaminated. Fuel transfer pump faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nSTE/ICE-R.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where \ntroubleshooting continues.1\nIs idle speed adjusted \ncorrectly for accessories and \noperating environment (normally: 975 to 1075 RPM)?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOAdjust idle speed \nas necessary \n(Para 5-20).", "snippet": "0-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nIdle speed too low. High pressure fuel leak. Air in fuel system.Fuel contaminated. Fuel transfer pump faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nSTE/ICE-R."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p687_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 687, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-635ACCELERATOR\u001f\nCABLEWARNING\n• Fuel is very flammable and can explode easily. To avoid serious injury or death, keep fuel aw ay from open fire and keep fire \nextinguisher within easy reach when working with fuel. Do not work on fuel system when engine is hot. Fuel can be ignited by ho t \nengine. When working with fuel, post signs that  read NO SMOKING WITHIN 50 FEET (15 m). • Pressure test procedure results in fuel under high pressure. Be sure that pressure test device is connected properly and use \nsafety shield during test. Failure to do so may result in injury to personnel. • P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, sparks or open flame . Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F ( 59 degrees C).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-635ACCELERATOR\u001f\nCABLEWARNING\n• Fuel is very flammable and can explode easily. To avoid serious injury or death, keep fuel aw ay from open fire and keep fire \nextinguisher within "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p687_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 687, "text": "o personnel. • P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, sparks or open flame . Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F ( 59 degrees C).\nUse only in a well ventilated area. Inhaling vapors over a period of ti me \ncan cause headache and drowsiness. Use gloves  to prevent irritation or inflammation of the skin. Solvent absorbed through the \nskin can result in internal disorders. If contact occurs, wash the affected area with water for 15 minutes. For eyes, flush wit h water \nand then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment. Refer to \nTM9-247 for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous.", "snippet": "o personnel. • P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, sparks or open flame . Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F ( 59 degrees C)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p687_chunk_2", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 687, "text": " for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous.\nMake certain the air stream is directed away from user and other personnel in  \nthe area. To prevent injury, user must wear protec tive goggles or face shield when using compressed air. CAUTION\nWashing oil seals, electrical cables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. IDLE SPEED\nSTE/ICE-R RPM CHECK\nUse STE/ICE to check engine RPM at idle \n(Para 5-20). (a) If idle RPM is within 975 to 1075 \nrange, go to Step 2 of this fault. (b) If idle RPM is not within specified \nrange, adjust idle speed by \nadjusting accelerator cable as \nnecessary (Para 5-20). TR01191", "snippet": " for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p688_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 688, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6364. ENGINE STARTS BUT IDLES ROUGHLY (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nIdle speed OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHigh pressure fuel leak. Air in fuel system.Fuel contaminated. Fuel transfer pump faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf high pressure fuel leaks, engine \nwill not run smoothly. KNOWN INFO\nIdle speed OK. High pressure fuel lines OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir in fuel system.Fuel contaminated.Fuel transfer pump faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf air is in fuel system, engine will \njerk and/or stall.2\nAre high pressure fuel lines, \nfittings, sealing washers, \nand/or valve seals free from \nleaks? YESNOReplace lines, fittings, \nsealing washers, and/or valve seals as \nnecessary (Para 5-11). 3\nIs fuel system free from air in \nfuel lines?", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6364. ENGINE STARTS BUT IDLES ROUGHLY (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nIdle speed OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHigh pressure fuel leak. Air in fuel system.Fuel contaminated. Fuel transfer pump faulty"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p688_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 688, "text": "ngs, sealing washers, \nand/or valve seals free from \nleaks? YESNOReplace lines, fittings, \nsealing washers, and/or valve seals as \nnecessary (Para 5-11). 3\nIs fuel system free from air in \nfuel lines?\nYESNOCheck fuel flow and \nbleed system as necessary (Para 5-11).", "snippet": "ngs, sealing washers, \nand/or valve seals free from \nleaks? YESNOReplace lines, fittings, \nsealing washers, and/or valve seals as \nnecessary (Para 5-11). 3\nIs fuel system free from air in \nfuel lines?"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p689_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 689, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-637HIGH PRESSURE FUEL LINES\nFUEL FILTER TRANSFER \u001f\nPUMPHIGH PRESSURE FUEL LINES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect high pressure fuel lines and \nfittings for signs of leaks.\n(a) If lines, fittings, and/or seals free \nfrom leaks, go to Step 3 of this fault.\n(b) If lines, fittings, and/or seals show \nsigns of leaks, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace \ncomponents as necessary (Para 5-11).\nFUEL FLOW THROUGH FILTER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck flow of fuel through fuel filter (Para 5-11).\n(a) If fuel flows freely without air, go to \nStep 4 of this fault.\n(b) If air in fuel lines, check for air \nleaks, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and bleed system (Para 5-11).\nTR01192", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-637HIGH PRESSURE FUEL LINES\nFUEL FILTER TRANSFER \u001f\nPUMPHIGH PRESSURE FUEL LINES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect high pressure fuel lines and \nfittings for signs of leaks.\n(a) "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p690_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 690, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6384. ENGINE STARTS BUT IDLES ROUGHLY (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nIdle speed OK. High pressure fuel lines OK. Fuel system OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFuel contaminated. Fuel transfer pump faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf fuel contaminated, engine will not \nrun smoothly. KNOWN INFO\nIdle speed OK. High pressure fuel lines OK. Fuel system OK. Fuel OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTransfer pump and/or fuel filter \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf transfer pump and/or fuel filter \nfaulty, engine will not run smoothly.4\nIs fuel clean and free from \ncontaminants? YESNODrain and flush fuel \ntank, and replace fuel (Para 5-9). 5\nDoes fuel flow freely through \nthe transfer pump and fuel \nfilter?", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6384. ENGINE STARTS BUT IDLES ROUGHLY (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nIdle speed OK. High pressure fuel lines OK. Fuel system OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFuel contaminated. Fuel transfer pump fault"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p690_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 690, "text": "ne will not run smoothly.4\nIs fuel clean and free from \ncontaminants? YESNODrain and flush fuel \ntank, and replace fuel (Para 5-9). 5\nDoes fuel flow freely through \nthe transfer pump and fuel \nfilter?\nYESNOReplace faulty \ntransfer pump \n(Para 5-3) and/or filter elements \n(Para 5-15). Refer to the Electrical \nTroubleshooting Section \nto solve possible \nelectrical problems.", "snippet": "ne will not run smoothly.4\nIs fuel clean and free from \ncontaminants? YESNODrain and flush fuel \ntank, and replace fuel (Para 5-9). 5\nDoes fuel flow freely through \nthe transfer pump and fuel \nfilter?"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p691_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 691, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-639LINE TO FUEL/WATER\u001f\nSEPARATORTRANSFER\u001f\nPUMPFUEL/WATER\u001f\nSEPARATOR\nLINE TO FUEL\u001f\nTRANSFER PUMPCONTAMINATED FUEL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Check for contaminated fuel by \noperating engine with known clean fuel \nfrom temporary supply tank.(a) Disconnect fuel line at fuel/water \nseparator, where line leads to fuel transfer pump. (b) Place disconnected end of fuel line \nin temp tank of known clean fuel. (c) Start engine and run it for \napproximately 5 minutes to \ndetermine if engine performance \nimproves with known clean fuel. (2) If fuel in fuel tank proves clean, go to \nStep 5 of this fault. (3) If fuel in fuel tank is contaminated, shut \ndown engine (TM 10-3930-673-10), \ndrain and flush tank, and replace fuel \nwith clean fuel (Para 5-9).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-639LINE TO FUEL/WATER\u001f\nSEPARATORTRANSFER\u001f\nPUMPFUEL/WATER\u001f\nSEPARATOR\nLINE TO FUEL\u001f\nTRANSFER PUMPCONTAMINATED FUEL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Check for contaminated fuel by \noperating e"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p691_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 691, "text": "el tank proves clean, go to \nStep 5 of this fault. (3) If fuel in fuel tank is contaminated, shut \ndown engine (TM 10-3930-673-10), \ndrain and flush tank, and replace fuel \nwith clean fuel (Para 5-9).\nFUEL FLOW THROUGH FILTER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Loosen hose fitting at top of secondary \nfilter element (Para 5-15). (2) Crank engine for thirty seconds. (a) If fuel flows freely from fitting, \ntransfer pump and fuel filter OK. (b) If fuel does not flow freely, replace \ntransfer pump (Para 5-3) and/or filter elements (Para 5-15). TR01193", "snippet": "el tank proves clean, go to \nStep 5 of this fault. (3) If fuel in fuel tank is contaminated, shut \ndown engine (TM 10-3930-673-10), \ndrain and flush tank, and replace fuel \nwith clean fuel (Para 5-9)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p692_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 692, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6402\n5. ENGINE SURGES, CHANGES SPEED\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine running (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P.\nKNOWN INFO\nNone.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nIdle speed too low.\nHigh pressure fuel leak.TEST OPTIONS\nSTE/ICE-R.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where \ntroubleshooting continued.1\nIs idle speed adjusted \ncorrectly for accessories and \noperating environment (normally: 975 to 1075 RPM)?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOAdjust idle speed \nas necessary \n(Para 5-20).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6402\n5. ENGINE SURGES, CHANGES SPEED\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nEquipmen"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p693_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 693, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-641ACCELERATOR\u001f\nCABLEWARNING\n• Fuel is very flammable and can explode easily. To avoid serious injury or death, keep fuel aw ay from open fire and keep fire \nextinguisher within easy reach when working with fuel. Do not work on fuel system when engine is hot. Fuel can be ignited by ho t \nengine. When working with fuel, post signs that  read NO SMOKING WITHIN 50 FEET (15 m). • Pressure test procedure results in fuel under high pressure. Be sure that pressure test device is connected properly and use \nsafety shield during test. Failure to do so may result in injury to personnel. • P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, sparks or open flame . Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F ( 59 degrees C).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-641ACCELERATOR\u001f\nCABLEWARNING\n• Fuel is very flammable and can explode easily. To avoid serious injury or death, keep fuel aw ay from open fire and keep fire \nextinguisher within "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p693_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 693, "text": "o personnel. • P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, sparks or open flame . Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F ( 59 degrees C).\nUse only in a well ventilated area. Inhaling vapors over a period of ti me \ncan cause headache and drowsiness. Use gloves  to prevent irritation or inflammation of the skin. Solvent absorbed through the \nskin can result in internal disorders. If contact occurs, wash the affected area with water for 15 minutes. For eyes, flush wit h water \nand then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment. Refer to \nTM9-247 for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous.", "snippet": "o personnel. • P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, sparks or open flame . Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F ( 59 degrees C)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p693_chunk_2", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 693, "text": " for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous.\nMake certain the air stream is directed away from user and other personnel in  \nthe area. To prevent injury, user must wear protec tive goggles or face shield when using compressed air. CAUTION\nWashing oil seals, electrical cables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. IDLE SPEED\nSTE/ICE-R RPM CHECK\nUse STE/ICE to check engine RPM at idle \n(Para 5-20). (a) If idle RPM within 975 to 1075 \nrange, go to Step 2 of this fault. (b) If idle RPM is not within specified \nrange, adjust idle speed by \nadjusting accelerator cable as \nnecessary (Para 5-20). TR01197", "snippet": " for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p694_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 694, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6425. ENGINE SURGES, CHANGES SPEED (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nIdle speed OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHigh pressure fuel leak.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf high pressure fuel leaks, engine \nwill jerk and/or stall.2\nAre high pressure fuel lines, \nfittings, sealing washers, and/\nor valve seals free from leaks?\nYESNOReplace lines, fittings, \nsealing washers, \nand/or valve seals as necessary (Para 5-11). \nNotify Direct Support about this \nfault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6425. ENGINE SURGES, CHANGES SPEED (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nIdle speed OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHigh pressure fuel leak.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf high pressur"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p695_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 695, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-643HIGH PRESSURE FUEL LINESHIGH PRESSURE FUEL LINES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect high pressure fuel lines and \nfittings for signs of leaks.\n(a) If lines, fittings, and/or seals free \nfrom leaks, notify Direct Support about this fault.\n(b) If lines, fittings, and/or seals show \nsigns of leaks, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace \ncomponents as necessary \n(Para 5-11).\nTR01198", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-643HIGH PRESSURE FUEL LINESHIGH PRESSURE FUEL LINES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect high pressure fuel lines and \nfittings for signs of leaks.\n(a) If lines, fittings, and/or s"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p696_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 696, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6442\n6. ENGINE RUNS ROUGHLY, MISFIRES\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine running (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHigh pressure fuel leak. Air in fuel system. Fuel contaminated.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6442\n6. ENGINE RUNS ROUGHLY, MISFIRES\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine running (TM "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p696_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 696, "text": "ked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHigh pressure fuel leak. Air in fuel system. Fuel contaminated.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nAre high pressure fuel lines, \nfittings, sealing washers, \nand/or valve seals free from leaks?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOReplace lines, fittings, \nsealing washers, \nand/or valve seals as \nnecessary (Para 5-11).", "snippet": "ked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHigh pressure fuel leak. Air in fuel system. Fuel contaminated.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p697_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 697, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-645HIGH PRESSURE FUEL LINESWARNING\n• Fuel is very flammable and can explode easily. To avoid serious injury or death, keep fuel aw ay from open fire and keep fire \nextinguisher within easy reach when working with fuel. Do not work on fuel system when engine is hot. Fuel can be ignited by ho t \nengine. When working with fuel, post signs that  read NO SMOKING WITHIN 50 FEET (15 m). • Pressure test procedure results in fuel under high pressure. Be sure that pressure test device is connected properly and use \nsafety shield during test. Failure to do so may result in injury to personnel. • P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, sparks or open flame . Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F ( 59 degrees C).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-645HIGH PRESSURE FUEL LINESWARNING\n• Fuel is very flammable and can explode easily. To avoid serious injury or death, keep fuel aw ay from open fire and keep fire \nextinguisher w"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p697_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 697, "text": "o personnel. • P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, sparks or open flame . Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F ( 59 degrees C).\nUse only in a well ventilated area. Inhaling vapors over a period of ti me \ncan cause headache and drowsiness. Use gloves  to prevent irritation or inflammation of the skin. Solvent absorbed through the \nskin can result in internal disorders. If contact occurs, wash the affected area with water for 15 minutes. For eyes, flush wit h water \nand then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment. Refer to \nTM9-247 for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous.", "snippet": "o personnel. • P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, sparks or open flame . Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F ( 59 degrees C)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p697_chunk_2", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 697, "text": " for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous.\nMake certain the air stream is directed away from user and other personnel in  \nthe area. To prevent injury, user must wear protec tive goggles or face shield when using compressed air. CAUTION\nWashing oil seals, electrical cables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. HIGH PRESSURE FUEL LINES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect high pressure fuel lines and \nfittings for signs of leaks. (a) If lines, fittings, and/or seals free \nfrom leaks, go to Step 2 of this fault. (b) If lines, fittings, and/or seals show \nsigns of leaks, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace components as necessary \n(Para 5-11). TR01195", "snippet": " for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p698_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 698, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6466. ENGINE RUNS ROUGHLY, MISFIRES (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nHigh pressure fuel lines OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir in fuel system.\nFuel contaminated.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf air is in fuel system, engine will \nnot run smoothly.\nKNOWN INFO\nHigh pressure fuel lines OK.Fuel system OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFuel contaminated.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf fuel contaminated, engine will \njerk and/or stall.2\nIs fuel system free from air in \nfuel lines?\nYESNOCheck fuel flow and \nbleed system as necessary (Para 5-11). \n3\nIs fuel clean and free from \ncontaminants?\nYESNODrain and flush fuel \ntank and replace fuel (Para 5-9).\nRefer to the Electrical \nTroubleshooting Section \nto solve possible electrical problems.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6466. ENGINE RUNS ROUGHLY, MISFIRES (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nHigh pressure fuel lines OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir in fuel system.\nFuel contaminated.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p699_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 699, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-647LINE TO FUEL/WATER\u001f\nSEPARATORTRANSFER\u001f\nPUMPFUEL/WATER\u001f\nSEPARATOR\nLINE TO FUEL\u001f\nTRANSFER PUMPFUEL FLOW THROUGH FILTER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck flow of fuel through fuel filter \n(Para 5-11). (a) If fuel flows freely without air, go to \nStep 3 of this fault. (b) If air in fuel lines, check for air \nleaks, shut down engine \n(TM 10-3930-673-10) and bleed \nsystem (Para 5-11). CONTAMINATED FUEL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Check for contaminated fuel by \noperating engine with known clean fuel \nfrom temporary supply tank. (a) Disconnect fuel line at fuel/water \nseparator, where line leads to fuel \ntransfer pump. (b) Place disconnected end of fuel line \nin temp tank of known clean fuel.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-647LINE TO FUEL/WATER\u001f\nSEPARATORTRANSFER\u001f\nPUMPFUEL/WATER\u001f\nSEPARATOR\nLINE TO FUEL\u001f\nTRANSFER PUMPFUEL FLOW THROUGH FILTER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck flow of fuel through fuel filter \n"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p699_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 699, "text": "fuel \nfrom temporary supply tank. (a) Disconnect fuel line at fuel/water \nseparator, where line leads to fuel \ntransfer pump. (b) Place disconnected end of fuel line \nin temp tank of known clean fuel.\n(c) Start engine and run it for \napproximately 5 minutes to determine if engine performance improves with known clean fuel. (2) If fuel in fuel tank proves clean, use as \nis. (3) If fuel in fuel tank is contaminated, shut \ndown engine (TM 10-3930-673-10), drain and flush tank, and replace fuel with clean fuel \n(Para 5-9).TR01196", "snippet": "fuel \nfrom temporary supply tank. (a) Disconnect fuel line at fuel/water \nseparator, where line leads to fuel \ntransfer pump. (b) Place disconnected end of fuel line \nin temp tank of known clean fuel."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p700_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 700, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6482\n7. ENGINE RPM DOES NOT REACH RATED SPEED\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine running (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nEngine overloaded. Accelerator linkage incorrectly \nadjusted, worn.TEST OPTIONS\nSTE/ICE-R. REASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where \ntroubleshooting continues.1\nIs idle speed correct? Is \ncorrect gear range used for \noperation and load?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOUse STE/ICE to verify \nRPM at idle and full throttle.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6482\n7. ENGINE RPM DOES NOT REACH RATED SPEED\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p700_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 700, "text": "eshooting continues.1\nIs idle speed correct? Is \ncorrect gear range used for \noperation and load?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOUse STE/ICE to verify \nRPM at idle and full throttle.\nChange to appropriate \ngear range \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).", "snippet": "eshooting continues.1\nIs idle speed correct? Is \ncorrect gear range used for \noperation and load?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOUse STE/ICE to verify \nRPM at idle and full throttle."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p701_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 701, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-649ACCELERATOR \u001f\nCABLEWARNING\n• Fuel is very flammable and can explode easily. To avoid serious injury or death, keep fuel aw ay from open fire and keep fire \nextinguisher within easy reach when working with fuel. Do not work on fuel system when engine is hot. Fuel can be ignited by ho t \nengine. When working with fuel, post signs that  read NO SMOKING WITHIN 50 FEET (15 m). • Pressure test procedure results in fuel under high pressure. Be sure that pressure test device is connected properly and use \nsafety shield during test. Failure to do so may result in injury to personnel. • P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, sparks or open flame . Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F ( 59 degrees C).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-649ACCELERATOR \u001f\nCABLEWARNING\n• Fuel is very flammable and can explode easily. To avoid serious injury or death, keep fuel aw ay from open fire and keep fire \nextinguisher within"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p701_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 701, "text": "o personnel. • P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, sparks or open flame . Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F ( 59 degrees C).\nUse only in a well ventilated area. Inhaling vapors over a period of ti me \ncan cause headache and drowsiness. Use gloves  to prevent irritation or inflammation of the skin. Solvent absorbed through the \nskin can result in internal disorders. If contact occurs, wash the affected area with water for 15 minutes. For eyes, flush wit h water \nand then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment. Refer to \nTM9-247 for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous.", "snippet": "o personnel. • P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, sparks or open flame . Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F ( 59 degrees C)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p701_chunk_2", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 701, "text": " for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous.\nMake certain the air stream is directed away from user and other personnel in  \nthe area. To prevent injury, user must wear protec tive goggles or face shield when using compressed air. CAUTION\nWashing oil seals, electrical cables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. IDLE AND GEAR RANGE\nSTE/ICE-R RPM CHECK\nUse STE/ICE test to verify RPM within 975 to \n1075 at idle and 2650 to 2750 at full throttle. Check that gear range is correct for operation and load. (a) If RPM is within specified range \nand gear range is correct, go to Step 2 of this fault. (b) If RPM is not within specified range \nand/or gear range is incorrect, adjust as necessary (Para 5-20). TR01453", "snippet": " for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p702_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 702, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6507. ENGINE RPM DOES NOT REACH RATED SPEED (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nIdle and gear range OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAccelerator linkage incorrectly \nadjusted, worn.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nSTE/ICE (10).\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf accelerator linkage incorrectly \nadjusted or worn, engine will not reach rated speed.2\nIs accelerator linkage \nadjusted correctly and free \nfrom signs of wear?\nYESNOAdjust or replace \naccelerator cable \nlinkage as \nnecessary (Para 5-20).\nNotify Direct Support of this fault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6507. ENGINE RPM DOES NOT REACH RATED SPEED (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nIdle and gear range OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAccelerator linkage incorrectly \nadjusted, worn.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspe"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p703_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 703, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-651ACCELERATOR\u001f\nCABLE LINKAGEACCELERATOR CABLE LINKAGE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nShut down engine and check accelerator \ncable linkage for adjustment and signs of \nwear (Para 5-20).\n(a) If linkage is adjusted correctly and \nis free from signs of wear, notify Direct Support.\n(b) If linkage is not adjusted correctly \nor worn, adjust or replace linkage as necessary (Para 5-20).\nTR01454", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-651ACCELERATOR\u001f\nCABLE LINKAGEACCELERATOR CABLE LINKAGE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nShut down engine and check accelerator \ncable linkage for adjustment and signs of \nwear (Para 5-20).\n(a) "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p704_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 704, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6522\n8. ENGINE PRODUCES EXCESSIVE WHITE EXHAUST SMOKE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine running (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P.\nKNOWN INFO\nNone.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nStarting procedure incorrect.\nEngine water temperature below \nnormal.\nFuel quality poor.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where \ntroubleshooting continues.1\nWere proper procedures \nfollowed when starting \nengine?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOFollow proper starting \nprocedures \n(TM 10-3930-673-10).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6522\n8. ENGINE PRODUCES EXCESSIVE WHITE EXHAUST SMOKE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEng"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p705_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 705, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-653WARNING\n• Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment. Refer to \nTM 9-247 for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous. Make certain the air stream is directed away from user and other personnel in  \nthe area. To prevent injury, user must wear protec tive goggles or face shield when using compressed air. CAUTION\nWashing oil seals, electrical cables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. STARTING PROCEDURES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nReview proper procedures for starting \nengine.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-653WARNING\n• Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment. Refer to \nTM 9-247 for correct information. • Eye shi"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p705_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 705, "text": "and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. STARTING PROCEDURES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nReview proper procedures for starting \nengine.\n(a) If proper starting procedures were \nfollowed, go to Step 2 of this fault. (b) If proper procedures were not \nfollowed, follow proper procedures to restart engine (TM 10-3930-673-10).", "snippet": "and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. STARTING PROCEDURES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nReview proper procedures for starting \nengine."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p706_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 706, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6548. ENGINE PRODUCES EXCESSIVE WHITE EXHAUST SMOKE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nStarting procedure OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nEngine water temperature below \nnormal. Fuel quality poor.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf engine water temperature is \nbelow normal, engine produces \nexcessive white exhaust smoke. KNOWN INFO\nStarting procedures OK.Engine water temperature OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFuel quality poor.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf fuel quality is poor, engine \nproduces excessive white exhaust smoke.2\nIs indicated engine water \ntemperature within normal \n180 to 210 degrees F \noperating range? YESNORefer to Fault 12, \nEngine water \ntemperature below \nnormal to correct this fault. 3\nIs fuel in fuel tank known to \nbe of good quality?", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6548. ENGINE PRODUCES EXCESSIVE WHITE EXHAUST SMOKE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nStarting procedure OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nEngine water temperature below \nnormal. Fuel quality poor.TEST OPT"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p706_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 706, "text": "ature within normal \n180 to 210 degrees F \noperating range? YESNORefer to Fault 12, \nEngine water \ntemperature below \nnormal to correct this fault. 3\nIs fuel in fuel tank known to \nbe of good quality?\nYESNOCheck quality of \ncurrent fuel by \noperating engine \nfrom a temporary tank of known fuel \nquality. Notify Direct Support \nabout this fault.", "snippet": "ature within normal \n180 to 210 degrees F \noperating range? YESNORefer to Fault 12, \nEngine water \ntemperature below \nnormal to correct this fault. 3\nIs fuel in fuel tank known to \nbe of good quality?"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p707_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 707, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-655ENGINE WATER\u001f\nTEMPERATURE GAGE\nFUEL/WATER\u001f\nSEPARATOR\nLINE TO FUEL\u001f\nTRANSFER PUMP\nLINE TO FUEL/WATER\u001f\nSEPARATORTRANSFER\u001f\nPUMPENGINE WATER TEMPERATURE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Check indicated engine water \ntemperature to determine if it is within \nnormal 180 to 210 degrees F operating range. (a) If engine water temperature is \nwithin normal operating range, go \nto Step 3 of this fault. (b) If engine water temperature is not \nwithin normal operating range, refer to the Engine Water \nTemperature Below Normal \nTroubleshooting Section to correct this fault. (2) Start and run engine \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). If engine water temperature within normal range, go to \nStep 3 of this fault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-655ENGINE WATER\u001f\nTEMPERATURE GAGE\nFUEL/WATER\u001f\nSEPARATOR\nLINE TO FUEL\u001f\nTRANSFER PUMP\nLINE TO FUEL/WATER\u001f\nSEPARATORTRANSFER\u001f\nPUMPENGINE WATER TEMPERATURE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Chec"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p707_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 707, "text": "r \nTemperature Below Normal \nTroubleshooting Section to correct this fault. (2) Start and run engine \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). If engine water temperature within normal range, go to \nStep 3 of this fault.\nCURRENT FUEL QUALITY\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Check quality of fuel in fuel tank by \noperating engine from a temporary tank \nof known fuel quality. (a) Disconnect fuel line at fuel/water \nseparator, where line leads to fuel \ntransfer pump. (b) Place disconnected end of fuel line \nin temp tank of known clean fuel. (c) Start engine and run it for \napproximately 5 minutes to determine if engine performance improves with known clean fuel. (2) If fuel is of good quality, notify Direct \nSupport about this fault. (3) If fuel is of questionable quality, drain \nfuel tank and replace fuel (Para 5-9). Go to Step (4) below. (4) Start and run engine. If engine exhaust \nsmoke normal, fault is corrected. If not, notify Direct Support about this fault.TR01194", "snippet": "r \nTemperature Below Normal \nTroubleshooting Section to correct this fault. (2) Start and run engine \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). If engine water temperature within normal range, go to \nStep 3 of this fault."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p708_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 708, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6562\n9. ENGINE PRODUCES EXCESSIVE BLACK EXHAUST SMOKE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine running (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nGear range for operation or load \nincorrect. Air intake and/or exhaust blocked. Air leak between intake manifold \nand turbocharger. Air leak between turbocharger and \nexhaust manifold. Turbocharger malfunctioning, \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6562\n9. ENGINE PRODUCES EXCESSIVE BLACK EXHAUST SMOKE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEng"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p708_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 708, "text": "ke and/or exhaust blocked. Air leak between intake manifold \nand turbocharger. Air leak between turbocharger and \nexhaust manifold. Turbocharger malfunctioning, \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs gear range in use correct \nfor operation and load?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOIf gear range too \nhigh, shift to lower \ngear.", "snippet": "ke and/or exhaust blocked. Air leak between intake manifold \nand turbocharger. Air leak between turbocharger and \nexhaust manifold. Turbocharger malfunctioning, \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p709_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 709, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-657RANGE SELECT\u001f\nCONTROLWARNING\n• Drycleaning solvent P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, \nsparks, or open flame. Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F (59 degrees C). Use on ly in a well-ventilated area. Inhaling vap ors \nover a period of time can cause headache and drowsiness. Use gloves to prevent irrita tion or inflammation of the skin. Solvent \nabsorbed through the skin can result in internal disorders. If contact occurs, wash the affected area with water for 15 minutes . For \neyes, flush with water and then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-657RANGE SELECT\u001f\nCONTROLWARNING\n• Drycleaning solvent P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, \nsparks, or open f"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p709_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 709, "text": "or 15 minutes . For \neyes, flush with water and then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment.\nRefer to \nTM9-247 for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous. Make certain the air stream is directed away from user and other personnel in  \nthe area. To prevent injury, user must wear protec tive goggles or face shield when using compressed air. CAUTION\nWashing oil seals, electrical cables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. GEAR RANGE OPERATION AND LOAD\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck that gear range in use is correct for \noperation and load \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (a) If gear range is correct, go to \nStep 2 of this fault.", "snippet": "or 15 minutes . For \neyes, flush with water and then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p709_chunk_2", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 709, "text": "erials. GEAR RANGE OPERATION AND LOAD\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck that gear range in use is correct for \noperation and load \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (a) If gear range is correct, go to \nStep 2 of this fault.\n(b) If gear range is too high, shift to \nlower gear (TM 10-3930-673-10). TR01219", "snippet": "erials. GEAR RANGE OPERATION AND LOAD\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck that gear range in use is correct for \noperation and load \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (a) If gear range is correct, go to \nStep 2 of this fault."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p710_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 710, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6589. ENGINE PRODUCES EXCESSIVE BLACK EXHAUST SMOKE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nGear range for operation and load \nOK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir intake and/or exhaust blocked. Air leak between intake manifold \nand turbocharger. Air leak between turbocharger and \nexhaust manifold. Turbocharger malfunctioning, \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf air intake and/or exhaust \nblocked, engine will produce excessive black exhaust smoke. KNOWN INFO\nGear range for operation and load \nOK. Air intake and exhaust OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir leak between intake manifold \nand turbocharger. Air leak between turbocharger and \nexhaust manifold. Turbocharger malfunctioning, \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6589. ENGINE PRODUCES EXCESSIVE BLACK EXHAUST SMOKE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nGear range for operation and load \nOK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir intake and/or exhaust blocked. Air leak betwee"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p710_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 710, "text": "aust OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir leak between intake manifold \nand turbocharger. Air leak between turbocharger and \nexhaust manifold. Turbocharger malfunctioning, \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf air leaks in intake manifold and \ncrossovers, engine will run rich, produce excessive black exhaust smoke.2\nAre the air intake and/or \nexhaust free from obstruction \nand/or restrictions? YESNOClean air intake and/or \nexhaust as necessary \n(Para 5-5, Para 5-4, Para 6-3). 3\nAre the intake manifold and/or \ncrossovers free from signs of \nair leaks? YESNOReplace components \nas necessary \n(Para 4-9 and Para 5-7).", "snippet": "aust OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir leak between intake manifold \nand turbocharger. Air leak between turbocharger and \nexhaust manifold. Turbocharger malfunctioning, \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p711_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 711, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-659EXHAUST\nAIR INTAKE\nINTAKE\u001f\nMANIFOLDAIR INTAKE AND EXHAUST\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect air in take and exhaust for \nobstructions and/or restrictions.\n(a) If air intake and exhaust are clean, \ngo to Step 3 of this fault.\n(b) If air intake and exhaust are \nblocked, clean intake cap and \nexhaust pipe (Para 5-5, Para 5-4, \nPara 6-3).\nMANIFOLD AND CROSSOVERS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect manifold and crossover \nhoses for obstructions and/or restrictions.\n(a) If manifold and crossover hoses \nare clean, go to Step 4 of this fault.\n(b) If manifold and crossover hoses \nare blocked, clean manifold and \nhoses (Para 4-9 and Para 5-7).TR01220", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-659EXHAUST\nAIR INTAKE\nINTAKE\u001f\nMANIFOLDAIR INTAKE AND EXHAUST\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect air in take and exhaust for \nobstructions and/or restrictions.\n(a) If air intake a"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p712_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 712, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6609. ENGINE PRODUCES EXCESSIVE BLACK EXHAUST SMOKE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nGear range for operation and load \nOK. Air intake and exhaust OK. Intake manifold to turbocharger \nOK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir leaks between turbocharger \nand exhaust manifold. Turbocharger malfunctioning, \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf air leaks between turbocharger \nand exhaust manifold, engine will produce excessive black exhaust \nsmoke. KNOWN INFO\nGear range for operation and load \nOK. Air intake and exhaust OK. Intake manifold to turbocharger \nOK. Turbocharger to exhaust manifold \nOK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTurbocharger malfunctioning, \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6609. ENGINE PRODUCES EXCESSIVE BLACK EXHAUST SMOKE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nGear range for operation and load \nOK. Air intake and exhaust OK. Intake manifold to turbocharger \nOK. POSS"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p712_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 712, "text": "d load \nOK. Air intake and exhaust OK. Intake manifold to turbocharger \nOK. Turbocharger to exhaust manifold \nOK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTurbocharger malfunctioning, \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf turbocharger malfunctioning or \nfaulty, engine will produce excessive black exhaust smoke.4\nIs turbocharger gasket free \nfrom leaks? Is exhaust \nmanifold free from signs of \ncracks? YESNOReplace gasket \n(Para 5-6) and/or replace exhaust \nmanifold (Para 4-8). 5\nIs turbocharger functioning \ncorrectly? YESNOReplace malfunctioning \nor faulty turbocharger (Para 5-6). Notify Direct Support \nabout this fault.", "snippet": "d load \nOK. Air intake and exhaust OK. Intake manifold to turbocharger \nOK. Turbocharger to exhaust manifold \nOK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTurbocharger malfunctioning, \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p713_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 713, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-661EXHAUST\nAIR INTAKE\nTURBOCHARGERTURBOCHARGER AND \nEXHAUST MANIFOLD\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect turbocharger (gasket) and \nexhaust manifold for signs of cracks and air leaks.\n(a) If there are no signs of cracks and \nair leaks, go to Step 5 of this fault.\n(b) If there are signs of cracks and/or \nair leaks, replace turbocharger gasket (Para 5-6), and/or exhaust manifold (Para 4-8) as necessary. \nTURBOCHARGER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck turbocharger intake, air and oil lines \nfor debris. Check wastegate \n(Para 5-6).\n(a) If turbocharger is functioning \ncorrectly, notify Direct Support about this fault.\n(b) If turbocharger shows signs of \ndebris, malfunctioning assemblies, replace turbocharger (Para 5-6). TR01221", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-661EXHAUST\nAIR INTAKE\nTURBOCHARGERTURBOCHARGER AND \nEXHAUST MANIFOLD\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect turbocharger (gasket) and \nexhaust manifold for signs of cracks and air le"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p714_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 714, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6622\n10. ENGINE PRODUCES EXCESSIVE NOISE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine running (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nDrive belt worn, tensioner faulty. Air intake and/or exhaust blocked. Turbocharger malfunctioning, \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs drive belt free from signs of \nwear?", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6622\n10. ENGINE PRODUCES EXCESSIVE NOISE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine running ("}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p714_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 714, "text": "ioning, \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs drive belt free from signs of \nwear?\nIs tensioner \nfunctioning correctly?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOIf drive belt is worn \nand/or tensioner faulty, replace belt \nand/or tensioner \n(Para 7-9).", "snippet": "ioning, \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs drive belt free from signs of \nwear?"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p715_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 715, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-663\nWARNING\n• Drycleaning solvent P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, \nsparks, or open flame. Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F (59 degrees C). Use on ly in a well-ventilated area. Inhaling vap ors \nover a period of time can cause headache and drowsiness. Use gloves to prevent irrita tion or inflammation of the skin. Solvent \nabsorbed through the skin can result in internal disorders. If contact occurs, wash the affected area with water for 15 minutes . For \neyes, flush with water and then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment. Refer to \nTM9-247 for correct information.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-663\nWARNING\n• Drycleaning solvent P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, \nsparks, or open flame. Flash point of"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p715_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 715, "text": " and then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment. Refer to \nTM9-247 for correct information.\n• Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous. Make certain the air stream is directed away from user and other personnel in  \nthe area. To prevent injury, user must wear protec tive goggles or face shield when using compressed air. Washing oil seals, electrical cables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. DRIVE BELT AND TENSIONER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect drive belt for wear. Check \noperation of tensioner. Drive belt deflection \nshould be no greater than 1/2 in. (a) If belt is not worn and tensioner is \nfunctioning correctly, go to Step 2 of this fault.", "snippet": " and then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment. Refer to \nTM9-247 for correct information."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p715_chunk_2", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 715, "text": "rive belt for wear. Check \noperation of tensioner. Drive belt deflection \nshould be no greater than 1/2 in. (a) If belt is not worn and tensioner is \nfunctioning correctly, go to Step 2 of this fault.\n(b) If belt is worn and/or tensioner is \nnot deflecting belt correctly, replace belt and/or tensioner (Para 7-9). TR01206", "snippet": "rive belt for wear. Check \noperation of tensioner. Drive belt deflection \nshould be no greater than 1/2 in. (a) If belt is not worn and tensioner is \nfunctioning correctly, go to Step 2 of this fault."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p716_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 716, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-66410. ENGINE PRODUCES EXCESSIVE NOISE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nDrive belt and tensioner OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir intake and/or exhaust blocked. Turbocharger malfunctioning, \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf air intake and/or exhaust \nblocked, engine will produce excessive noise. KNOWN INFO\nDrive belt and tensioner OK.Air intake and exhaust OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTurbocharger malfunctioning, \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf turbocharger malfunctioning or \nfaulty, turbocharger will produce additional noise.2\nAre the air intake and/or \nexhaust free from \nobstructions and/or \nrestrictions? YESNOClean air intake \nand/or exhaust as \nnecessary (Para 5-5, Para 5-4, Para 6-3). 3\nIs turbocharger functioning \ncorrectly?", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-66410. ENGINE PRODUCES EXCESSIVE NOISE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nDrive belt and tensioner OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir intake and/or exhaust blocked. Turbocharger malfunctioning, \nfaulty.T"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p716_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 716, "text": "e air intake and/or \nexhaust free from \nobstructions and/or \nrestrictions? YESNOClean air intake \nand/or exhaust as \nnecessary (Para 5-5, Para 5-4, Para 6-3). 3\nIs turbocharger functioning \ncorrectly?\nYESNOReplace malfunctioning \nor faulty turbocharger (Para 5-6). Notify Direct Support of \nthis fault.", "snippet": "e air intake and/or \nexhaust free from \nobstructions and/or \nrestrictions? YESNOClean air intake \nand/or exhaust as \nnecessary (Para 5-5, Para 5-4, Para 6-3). 3\nIs turbocharger functioning \ncorrectly?"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p717_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 717, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-665AIR INTAKE TURBOCHARGER\nEXHAUSTAIR INTAKE AND EXHAUST\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect air in take and exhaust for \nobstructions and/or restrictions.\n(a) If air intake and exhaust are clean, \ngo to Step 3 of this fault.\n(b) If air intake and exhaust are \nblocked, shut down engine  and \nclean intake cap and exhaust pipe \n(Para 5-5, Para 5-4, Para 6-3).\nTURBOCHARGER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck turbocharger intake, air and oil lines \nfor obstructions. Check wastegate (Para 5-6).\n(a) If turbocharger is functioning \ncorrectly, notify Direct Support about this fault.\n(b) If turbocharger shows signs of \nobstructions, faulty assemblies, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace \nturbocharger (Para 5-6).TR01207", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-665AIR INTAKE TURBOCHARGER\nEXHAUSTAIR INTAKE AND EXHAUST\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect air in take and exhaust for \nobstructions and/or restrictions.\n(a) If air intake and e"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p718_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 718, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6662\n11. ENGINE OIL PRESSURE BELOW NORMAL\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine running (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine oil pressure is \ncontinuously below normal 30 to \n90 PSI operating range. LOW OIL PRESSURE light is ON. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nEngine oil level low.Engine coolant temperature above \nnormal. Engine oil filter blocked.Engine oil grade incorrect. Engine oil pressure switch faulty.Engine oil pressure gage faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6662\n11. ENGINE OIL PRESSURE BELOW NORMAL\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nEqu"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p718_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 718, "text": "el low.Engine coolant temperature above \nnormal. Engine oil filter blocked.Engine oil grade incorrect. Engine oil pressure switch faulty.Engine oil pressure gage faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs engine oil at FULL mark on \ndipstick?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOAdd engine oil as \nneeded (Para 4-2).", "snippet": "el low.Engine coolant temperature above \nnormal. Engine oil filter blocked.Engine oil grade incorrect. Engine oil pressure switch faulty.Engine oil pressure gage faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p719_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 719, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-667ENGINE OIL\u001f\nDIPSTICKWARNING\n• Drycleaning solvent P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, \nsparks, or open flame. Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F (59 degrees C). Use on ly in a well-ventilated area. Inhaling vap ors \nover a period of time can cause headache and drowsiness. Use gloves to prevent irrita tion or inflammation of the skin. Solvent \nabsorbed through the skin can result in internal disorders. If contact occurs, wash the affected area with water for 15 minutes . For \neyes, flush with water and then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-667ENGINE OIL\u001f\nDIPSTICKWARNING\n• Drycleaning solvent P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, \nsparks, or open fl"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p719_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 719, "text": "or 15 minutes . For \neyes, flush with water and then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment.\nRefer to \nTM9-247 for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous. Make certain the air stream is directed away from user and other personnel in  \nthe area. To prevent injury, user must wear protec tive goggles or face shield when using compressed air. CAUTION\nWashing oil seals, electrical cables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. ENGINE OIL LEVEL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Check engine oil level by means of \ndipstick to determine if engine oil is at \nproper level (Para 4-2).", "snippet": "or 15 minutes . For \neyes, flush with water and then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p719_chunk_2", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 719, "text": "spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. ENGINE OIL LEVEL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Check engine oil level by means of \ndipstick to determine if engine oil is at \nproper level (Para 4-2).\n(2) Engine oil should be near or at FULL \nmark on dipstick.(a) If engine oil is near or at FULL \nmark, go to Step 2 of this fault. (b) If engine oil is not at FULL mark, \nadd engine oil as needed \n(Para 4-2). TR01215", "snippet": "spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. ENGINE OIL LEVEL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Check engine oil level by means of \ndipstick to determine if engine oil is at \nproper level (Para 4-2)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p720_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 720, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-66811. ENGINE OIL PRESSURE BELOW NORMAL (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine oil pressure is \nbelow normal 30 to 90 PSI \noperating range. LOW OIL PRESSURE light is ON. Engine oil level OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nEngine coolant temperature is \nabove normal. Engine filter blocked. Engine oil grade incorrect.Oil pressure switch faulty. Oil pressure gage faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf engine coolant temperature is \nabove normal, see Fault 13, \nEngine water temperature above \nnormal Troubleshooting Section. KNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine oil pressure is \nbelow normal 30 to 90 PSI operating range. LOW OIL PRESSURE light is ON. Engine oil level OK. Engine coolant temperature OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nEngine filter blocked. Engine oil grade incorrect.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-66811. ENGINE OIL PRESSURE BELOW NORMAL (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine oil pressure is \nbelow normal 30 to 90 PSI \noperating range. LOW OIL PRESSURE light is ON. Engine oil "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p720_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 720, "text": "re is \nbelow normal 30 to 90 PSI operating range. LOW OIL PRESSURE light is ON. Engine oil level OK. Engine coolant temperature OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nEngine filter blocked. Engine oil grade incorrect.\nOil pressure switch faulty. Oil pressure gage faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf engine oil filter is dirty or blocked, \nengine oil pressure will be low.2\nIs engine coolant temperature \nwithin normal 180 to 210 \ndegrees F operating range? YESNOSee Fault 13, Engine \nwater temperature \nabove normal Troubleshooting \nSection. 3\nDoes engine oil flow freely \nthrough engine oil filter? YESNOReplace engine oil \nfilter (Para 4-2).", "snippet": "re is \nbelow normal 30 to 90 PSI operating range. LOW OIL PRESSURE light is ON. Engine oil level OK. Engine coolant temperature OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nEngine filter blocked. Engine oil grade incorrect."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p721_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 721, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-669OIL FILTERENGINE WATER\u001f\nTEMPERATURE GAGEENGINE COOLANT TEMPERATURE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck engine coolant temperature gage to \ndetermine if coolant temperature is within \nnormal operating range.\n(a) If temperature is within normal \noperating range (180 to 210 degrees F), go to Step 3 of this fault.\n(b) If temperature is above normal \noperating range, go to Fault 13, Engine water temperature above \nnormal Troubleshooting Section.\nENGINE OIL FILTER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck last date of oil filter replacement. \nVisually inspect oil filter for damage.\n(a) If recent replacement and no \ndamage, go to Step 4 of this fault.\n(b) If dirty, blocked, or damaged, shut \ndown engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) \nand replace engine oil filter \n(Para 4-2).\nTR01216", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-669OIL FILTERENGINE WATER\u001f\nTEMPERATURE GAGEENGINE COOLANT TEMPERATURE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck engine coolant temperature gage to \ndetermine if coolant temperature is within \nnorm"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p722_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 722, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-67011. ENGINE OIL PRESSURE BELOW NORMAL (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine oil pressure is \nbelow normal 30 to 90 PSI \noperating range. LOW OIL PRESSURE light is ON. Engine oil level OK. Engine coolant temperature OK.Engine oil filter OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nEngine oil grade incorrect.Oil pressure switch faulty.Oil pressure gage faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nSample, analysis. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf engine oil grade is incorrect, \nengine oil pressure will be low. KNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine oil pressure is \nbelow normal 30 to 90 PSI \noperating range. LOW OIL PRESSURE light is ON. Engine oil OK. Engine coolant temperature OK. Engine oil filter OK.Engine oil grade OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nOil pressure switch faulty.Oil pressure gage faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nSTE/ICE Test #50.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-67011. ENGINE OIL PRESSURE BELOW NORMAL (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine oil pressure is \nbelow normal 30 to 90 PSI \noperating range. LOW OIL PRESSURE light is ON. Engine oil "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p722_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 722, "text": "ight is ON. Engine oil OK. Engine coolant temperature OK. Engine oil filter OK.Engine oil grade OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nOil pressure switch faulty.Oil pressure gage faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nSTE/ICE Test #50.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf engine oil pressure sensor/gage \nfaulty, engine oil pressure will be low.4\nIs engine oil of correct grade \nfor working environment \n(normally: OE/HDO-15/40)? YESNOReplace engine oil \nwith oil of correct grade (Para 4-2). 5\nIs engine oil pressure within \nrange of 30 to 90 PSI (at \nnormal engine coolant \ntemperature of 140 to 210 degrees F)? YESNONotify Direct \nSupport about this \nfault. Refer to Electrical System Troubleshooting \nFault 39, Oil pressure gage does not operate.", "snippet": "ight is ON. Engine oil OK. Engine coolant temperature OK. Engine oil filter OK.Engine oil grade OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nOil pressure switch faulty.Oil pressure gage faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nSTE/ICE Test #50."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p723_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 723, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-671ENGINE OIL\u001f\nSAMPLING\u001fVALVE\nPRESSURE\u001f\nTEST\u001f\nPORT\nOIL FILTERENGINE OIL GRADE\nSAMPLE AND ANALYSIS\n(1) Take engine oil sample from engine oil \nsampling valve. (2) Have engine oil sample analyzed. (a) If engine oil sample is of correct \ngrade, go to Step 5 of this fault. (b) If engine oil sample is of incorrect \ngrade, replace the current oil with \noil of correct grade (Para 4-2). PRESSURE AND \nENGINE OIL PRESSURE SWITCH\nSTE/ICE TEST\n(1) Remove plug from oil filter base and \ninstall STE/ICE pressure transducer. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #50 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P). (3) Read output pressure on test device. (a) If engine oil pressure is within 30 to \n90 PSI, refer to Electrical System \nTroubleshooting Fault 39, Oil \npressure gage does not operate.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-671ENGINE OIL\u001f\nSAMPLING\u001fVALVE\nPRESSURE\u001f\nTEST\u001f\nPORT\nOIL FILTERENGINE OIL GRADE\nSAMPLE AND ANALYSIS\n(1) Take engine oil sample from engine oil \nsampling valve. (2) Have engine oil "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p723_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 723, "text": "-4910-571-12&P). (3) Read output pressure on test device. (a) If engine oil pressure is within 30 to \n90 PSI, refer to Electrical System \nTroubleshooting Fault 39, Oil \npressure gage does not operate.\n(b) If engine oil pressure is not within \n30 to 90 PSI, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10). Notify Direct \nSupport about this fault. TR01217", "snippet": "-4910-571-12&P). (3) Read output pressure on test device. (a) If engine oil pressure is within 30 to \n90 PSI, refer to Electrical System \nTroubleshooting Fault 39, Oil \npressure gage does not operate."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p724_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 724, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6722\n12. ENGINE OIL PRESSURE ABOVE NORMAL\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine running (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine oil pressure is \ncontinuously above normal (30 to \n90 PSI) operating range. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nEngine oil pressure switch faulty. Engine oil pressure gage faulty. Engine coolant temperature below \nnormal. Engine oil grade incorrect (too \nheavy).TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. STE/ICE Test #50.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6722\n12. ENGINE OIL PRESSURE ABOVE NORMAL\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nEqu"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p724_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 724, "text": "\nEngine oil pressure switch faulty. Engine oil pressure gage faulty. Engine coolant temperature below \nnormal. Engine oil grade incorrect (too \nheavy).TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. STE/ICE Test #50.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs the engine oil pressure \nsensor functioning?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOGo to Step 2 of this \nfault. Go to Electrical System Trouble-\nshooting Fault 39, Oil pressure gage \ndoes not operate.", "snippet": "\nEngine oil pressure switch faulty. Engine oil pressure gage faulty. Engine coolant temperature below \nnormal. Engine oil grade incorrect (too \nheavy).TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. STE/ICE Test #50."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p725_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 725, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-673PRESSURE\u001f\nTEST\u001f\nPORT\nOIL FILTERWARNING\n• Drycleaning solvent P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, \nsparks, or open flame. Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F (59 degrees C). Use on ly in a well-ventilated area. Inhaling vap ors \nover a period of time can cause headache and drowsiness. Use gloves to prevent irrita tion or inflammation of the skin. Solvent \nabsorbed through the skin can result in internal disorders. If contact occurs, wash the affected area with water for 15 minutes . For \neyes, flush with water and then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-673PRESSURE\u001f\nTEST\u001f\nPORT\nOIL FILTERWARNING\n• Drycleaning solvent P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, \nsparks,"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p725_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 725, "text": "or 15 minutes . For \neyes, flush with water and then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment.\nRefer to \nTM9-247 for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous. Make certain the air stream is directed away from user and other personnel in  \nthe area. To prevent injury, user must wear protec tive goggles or face shield when using compressed air. CAUTION\nWashing oil seals, electrical cables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. PRESSURE AND ENGINE OIL PRESSURE \nSWITCH VISUAL INSPECTION\nAND STE/ICE TEST\n(1) Remove plug from oil filter base and \ninstall STE/ICE pressure transducer. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #50 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P).", "snippet": "or 15 minutes . For \neyes, flush with water and then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p725_chunk_2", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 725, "text": "RE AND ENGINE OIL PRESSURE \nSWITCH VISUAL INSPECTION\nAND STE/ICE TEST\n(1) Remove plug from oil filter base and \ninstall STE/ICE pressure transducer. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #50 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P).\n(3) Read output pressure on test device. (a) If engine oil pressure is within 30 to \n90 PSI, go to Electrical System \nTroubleshooting Fault 39, Oil pressure gage does not operate. (b) If engine oil pressure is not within \n30 to 90 PSI, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10). Go to \nStep 2 of this fault. TR01212", "snippet": "RE AND ENGINE OIL PRESSURE \nSWITCH VISUAL INSPECTION\nAND STE/ICE TEST\n(1) Remove plug from oil filter base and \ninstall STE/ICE pressure transducer. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #50 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p726_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 726, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-67412. ENGINE OIL PRESSURE ABOVE NORMAL (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine oil pressure is \ncontinuously above normal 30 to \n90 PSI operating range.\nEngine oil pressure switch OK.\nEngine oil pressure gage OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nEngine coolant temperature below \nnormal.\nEngine oil grade incorrect (too \nheavy).TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf coolant temperature is below \nnormal, engine oil pressure will \nread above normal.2\nIs engine coolant temperature \nwithin normal (180 to \n210 degrees F) operating range?\nYESNOSee Fault 12, Engine \nwater temperature \nbelow normal \nTroubleshooting Section.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-67412. ENGINE OIL PRESSURE ABOVE NORMAL (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine oil pressure is \ncontinuously above normal 30 to \n90 PSI operating range.\nEngine oil pressure switch O"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p727_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 727, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-675ENGINE OIL\u001f\nPRESSURE GAGEENGINE WATER\u001f\nTEMPERATURE\u001f\nGAGEENGINE COOLANT TEMPERATURE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck engine coolant temperature gage to \ndetermine if coolant temperature is within \nnormal operating range.\n(a) If temperature is within normal \noperating range (180 - 210 degrees F), go to Step 3 of this \nfault.\n(b) If temperature is below normal \noperating range, go to Fault 12, Engine water temperature below normal Troubleshooting Section.\nTR01213", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-675ENGINE OIL\u001f\nPRESSURE GAGEENGINE WATER\u001f\nTEMPERATURE\u001f\nGAGEENGINE COOLANT TEMPERATURE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck engine coolant temperature gage to \ndetermine if coolant temperature"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p728_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 728, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-67612. ENGINE OIL PRESSURE ABOVE NORMAL (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine oil pressure is \ncontinuously above normal 30 to \n90 PSI operating range.\nEngine oil pressure switch OK.\nEngine oil pressure gage OK.\nEngine coolant temperature OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nEngine oil grade incorrect.TEST OPTIONS\nOil sample, analysis.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf engine oil grade is incorrect, oil \npressure will read above normal.3\nIs engine oil of correct grade \nfor working environment \n(normally: OE/HDO-15/40)?\nYESNOReplace engine oil \nwith oil of correct \nweight (Para 4-2). \nNotify Direct Support of \nthis fault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-67612. ENGINE OIL PRESSURE ABOVE NORMAL (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine oil pressure is \ncontinuously above normal 30 to \n90 PSI operating range.\nEngine oil pressure switch O"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p729_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 729, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-677ENGINE OIL\u001f\nSAMPLING\u001fVALVEENGINE OIL GRADE\nSAMPLE AND ANALYSIS\n(1) Take engine oil sample from engine oil \nsampling valve (TM 10-3930-673-10).\n(2) Have engine oil sample analyzed.\n(a) If engine oil sample is of correct \ngrade, notify Direct Support about \nthis fault.\n(b) If engine oil sample is of incorrect \ngrade, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace \nthe current oil with oil of correct grade (Para 4-2).\nTR01214", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-677ENGINE OIL\u001f\nSAMPLING\u001fVALVEENGINE OIL GRADE\nSAMPLE AND ANALYSIS\n(1) Take engine oil sample from engine oil \nsampling valve (TM 10-3930-673-10).\n(2) Have engine oil sample analy"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p730_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 730, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6782\n13. ENGINE WATER TEMPERATURE BELOW NORMAL\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine running (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine water \ntemperature is continuously \nbelow normal 180 to \n210 degrees F operating range. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nThermostat incorrect or faulty. Coolant not flowing by temperature \nsensor.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6782\n13. ENGINE WATER TEMPERATURE BELOW NORMAL\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine run"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p730_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 730, "text": "rature is continuously \nbelow normal 180 to \n210 degrees F operating range. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nThermostat incorrect or faulty. Coolant not flowing by temperature \nsensor.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where \ntroubleshooting continues.1\nIs thermostat functioning?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOReplace faulty \nthermostat \n(Para 7-3).", "snippet": "rature is continuously \nbelow normal 180 to \n210 degrees F operating range. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nThermostat incorrect or faulty. Coolant not flowing by temperature \nsensor.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p731_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 731, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-679THERMOSTATWARNING\n• Drycleaning solvent P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, \nsparks, or open flame. Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F (59 degrees C). Use on ly in a well-ventilated area. Inhaling vap ors \nover a period of time can cause headache and drowsiness. Use gloves to prevent irrita tion or inflammation of the skin. Solvent \nabsorbed through the skin can result in internal disorders. If contact occurs, wash the affected area with water for 15 minutes . For \neyes, flush with water and then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment. Refer to \nTM9-247 for correct information.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-679THERMOSTATWARNING\n• Drycleaning solvent P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, \nsparks, or open flame. Flash"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p731_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 731, "text": " and then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment. Refer to \nTM9-247 for correct information.\n• Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous. Make certain the air stream is directed away from user and other personnel in  \nthe area. To prevent injury, user must wear protec tive goggles or face shield when using compressed air. CAUTION\nWashing oil seals, electrical cables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. THERMOSTAT\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Shut down engine and let it cool to \nambient temperature. (2) Relieve pressure on cooling system. (3) Remove thermostat.", "snippet": " and then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment. Refer to \nTM9-247 for correct information."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p731_chunk_2", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 731, "text": "l cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. THERMOSTAT\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Shut down engine and let it cool to \nambient temperature. (2) Relieve pressure on cooling system. (3) Remove thermostat.\nInspect and test \nthermostat to determine if it is functioning (Para 7-3).(a) If thermostat is functioning, \nreinstall thermostat and go to Step 2 of this fault. (b) If thermostat is faulty, replace \nthermostat (Para 7-3). TR01457", "snippet": "l cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. THERMOSTAT\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Shut down engine and let it cool to \nambient temperature. (2) Relieve pressure on cooling system. (3) Remove thermostat."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p732_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 732, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-68013. ENGINE WATER TEMPERAT URE BELOW NORMAL (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine water \ntemperature is continuously \nbelow normal 180 to 210 degrees F operating range.\nThermostat OK.Engine water temperature switch \nOK.\nEngine water temperature gage \nOK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nCoolant not flowing by temperature \nsensor.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf coolant is not flowing by sensor, \nwater temperature will read below normal.2\nIs engine coolant flowing by \ntemperature sensor?\nYESNOFlush and refill \ncoolant system \n(Para 7-2).\nGo to Electrical System Troubleshooting Fault \n38, Temperature gage \ndoes not operate.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-68013. ENGINE WATER TEMPERAT URE BELOW NORMAL (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine water \ntemperature is continuously \nbelow normal 180 to 210 degrees F operating range.\nThermosta"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p733_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 733, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-681RADIATOR\u001f\nCAP\nRADIATOR\nDRAIN\u001f\nCOCKDRAIN\u001fCOCKCOOLANT\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Remove radiator cap.\n(2) Run engine for approximately 10 \nminutes.\n(3) Visually check that  coolant is flowing \nfreely.\n(a) If coolant is flowing freely, go to \nElectrical System Troubleshooting \nFault 38, Temperature gage does \nnot operate.\n(b) If coolant is not flowing freely, shut \ndown engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and flush and refill coolant system \n(Para 7-2).\nTR01459", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-681RADIATOR\u001f\nCAP\nRADIATOR\nDRAIN\u001f\nCOCKDRAIN\u001fCOCKCOOLANT\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Remove radiator cap.\n(2) Run engine for approximately 10 \nminutes.\n(3) Visually check that  coolant i"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p734_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 734, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6822\n14. ENGINE WATER TEMPERATURE ABOVE NORMAL\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine running (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine water \ntemperature is continuously \nabove normal 180 to \n210 degrees F operating range. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nEngine coolant level low. Air flow across radiator \ninadequate. Fan drive belt loose or faulty.Radiator cap malfunctioning. Radiator hose(s) collapsed, \nruptured, leaking. Thermostat faulty.Water pump faulty.Cooling passages in cooling \nsystem blocked.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6822\n14. ENGINE WATER TEMPERATURE ABOVE NORMAL\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine run"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p734_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 734, "text": "e or faulty.Radiator cap malfunctioning. Radiator hose(s) collapsed, \nruptured, leaking. Thermostat faulty.Water pump faulty.Cooling passages in cooling \nsystem blocked.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs engine coolant at proper \nlevel (1/3 to 2/3 full) in \noverflow tank?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOAdd coolant as \nneeded (Para 7-2).", "snippet": "e or faulty.Radiator cap malfunctioning. Radiator hose(s) collapsed, \nruptured, leaking. Thermostat faulty.Water pump faulty.Cooling passages in cooling \nsystem blocked.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p735_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 735, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-683OVERFLOW\u001f\nTANKWARNING\n• Drycleaning solvent P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, \nsparks, or open flame. Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F (59 degrees C). Use on ly in a well-ventilated area. Inhaling vap ors \nover a period of time can cause headache and drowsiness. Use gloves to prevent irrita tion or inflammation of the skin. Solvent \nabsorbed through the skin can result in internal disorders. If contact occurs, wash the affected area with water for 15 minutes . For \neyes, flush with water and then seek immediate medical attention. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-683OVERFLOW\u001f\nTANKWARNING\n• Drycleaning solvent P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, \nsparks, or open flame. F"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p735_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 735, "text": "tes . For \neyes, flush with water and then seek immediate medical attention. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel.\n• Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous. Make certain the air stream is directed away from user and other personnel in  \nthe area. To prevent injury, user must wear protec tive goggles or face shield when using compressed air. • Cooling system is pressurized. Remove radiator cap slow ly and only when engine is cool or  painful burns could result. • The engine and radiator can be extremely hot. Contacting expos ed skin to these areas could result in severe burns. CAUTION\nWashing oil seals, electrical cables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. ENGINE COOLANT\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually check engine coolant level in \noverflow tank. Tank should be 1/3 to 2/3 full (Para 7-2).", "snippet": "tes . For \neyes, flush with water and then seek immediate medical attention. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p735_chunk_2", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 735, "text": " or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. ENGINE COOLANT\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually check engine coolant level in \noverflow tank. Tank should be 1/3 to 2/3 full (Para 7-2).\n(a) If tank is 1/3 to 2/3 full, go to Step 2 \nof this fault. (b) If tank is not filled to proper level, \nadd coolant as needed (Para 7-2). TR01448", "snippet": " or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. ENGINE COOLANT\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually check engine coolant level in \noverflow tank. Tank should be 1/3 to 2/3 full (Para 7-2)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p736_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 736, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-68414. ENGINE WATER TEMPERATURE ABOVE NORMAL (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine water \ntemperature is continuously \nabove normal 180 to 210 degrees F operating range. Engine coolant level OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir flow across radiator \ninadequate. Fan drive belt loose or faulty.Radiator cap malfunctioning.Radiator hose(s) collapsed, \nruptured, leaking. Thermostat faulty.Water pump faulty. Cooling passages in cooling \nsystem blocked.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf air flow restricted, engine will \noverheat. KNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine water \ntemperature is continuously above normal 180 to \n210 degrees F operating range. Engine coolant level OK. Air flow across radiator adequate. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFan drive belt loose or faulty.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-68414. ENGINE WATER TEMPERATURE ABOVE NORMAL (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine water \ntemperature is continuously \nabove normal 180 to 210 degrees F operating range. Engine coo"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p736_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 736, "text": "ine water \ntemperature is continuously above normal 180 to \n210 degrees F operating range. Engine coolant level OK. Air flow across radiator adequate. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFan drive belt loose or faulty.\nRadiator cap malfunctioning. Radiator hose(s) collapsed, \nruptured, leaking. Thermostat faulty.Water pump faulty. Cooling passages in cooling \nsystem blocked.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf fan drive belt is loose or faulty, \nengine will run hot.2\nIs air flow across radiator free \nfrom obstructions/\nrestrictions? YESNORemove obstructions/\nrestrictions. 3\nIs fan drive belt tightened \nproperly and free from signs \nof wear? YESNOTighten loose belt \nor replace worn belt (Para 7-9).", "snippet": "ine water \ntemperature is continuously above normal 180 to \n210 degrees F operating range. Engine coolant level OK. Air flow across radiator adequate. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFan drive belt loose or faulty."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p737_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 737, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-685DRIVE\u001f\nBELT\nRADIATORRADIATOR FINS AND FAN\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect radiator fins and fan for \nobstructions and/or restrictions that might \nrestrict air flow.\n(a) If there are no obstructions and/or \nrestrictions, go to Step 3 of this fault.\n(b) If there are obstructions and/or \nrestrictions, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and remove obstructions and/or restrictions.\nFAN DRIVE BELT\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect fan drive belt for tension and wear (cracks, frays, worn areas).\n(a) If belt is properly adjusted and not \nseverely worn, go to Step 4 of this fault.\n(b) If belt is loose, shut down engine \n(TM 10-3930-673-10) and tighten belt. If belt is severely worn, \nreplace belt (Para 7-9).\nTR01449", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-685DRIVE\u001f\nBELT\nRADIATORRADIATOR FINS AND FAN\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect radiator fins and fan for \nobstructions and/or restrictions that might \nrestrict air flow.\n(a) If "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p738_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 738, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-68614. ENGINE WATER TEMPERATURE ABOVE NORMAL (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine water \ntemperature is continuously \nabove normal 180 to 210 degrees F operating range. Engine coolant level OK.Air flow across radiator adequate.Fan drive belt OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nRadiator cap malfunctioning.Radiator hose(s) collapsed, \nruptured, leaking. Thermostat faulty.Water pump faulty.Cooling passages in cooling \nsystem blocked.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf radiator cap is malfunctioning, \nengine will run hot. KNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine water \ntemperature is continuously \nabove normal 180 to \n210 degrees F operating range. Engine coolant level OK. Air flow across radiator adequate. Fan drive belt OK.Radiator cap OK.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-68614. ENGINE WATER TEMPERATURE ABOVE NORMAL (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine water \ntemperature is continuously \nabove normal 180 to 210 degrees F operating range. Engine coo"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p738_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 738, "text": "\nIndicated engine water \ntemperature is continuously \nabove normal 180 to \n210 degrees F operating range. Engine coolant level OK. Air flow across radiator adequate. Fan drive belt OK.Radiator cap OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nRadiator hose(s) collapsed, \nruptured, leaking. Thermostat faulty. Water pump faulty. Cooling passages in cooling \nsystem blocked.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf radiator hose(s) collapsed, \nruptured, leaking, engine will overheat.4\nIs radiator cap \n(normally: 15 PSI) functioning \nproperly? YESNOTest, clean and/or \nreplace radiator cap (Para 7-2). 5\nAre upper and lower radiator \nhoses free from collapsed \nsections, ruptures, and/or \nleaks? YESNOReplace faulty \nradiator hose(s) (Para 7-4).", "snippet": "\nIndicated engine water \ntemperature is continuously \nabove normal 180 to \n210 degrees F operating range. Engine coolant level OK. Air flow across radiator adequate. Fan drive belt OK.Radiator cap OK."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p739_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 739, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-687RADIATOR\u001f\nCAP\nRADIATOR\nRADIATOR\u001f\nHOSERADIATOR\u001f\nHOSERADIATOR CAP\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nUse a known functioning radiator cap on the \nradiator. (a) If engine water temperature \ncontinues above normal operating temperature, clean and reinstall \noriginal cap. Go to Step 5 of this fault. (b) If engine water temperature returns \nto normal temperature range, original cap faulty. Replace original \ncap (Para 7-2). RADIATOR HOSES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect upper and lower radiator hoses for soft or collapsed sections, cracks \nor ruptures, and leaks. (a) If radiator hoses are free from soft \nor collapsed sections, cracks or ruptures, and leaks, go to Step 6 of this fault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-687RADIATOR\u001f\nCAP\nRADIATOR\nRADIATOR\u001f\nHOSERADIATOR\u001f\nHOSERADIATOR CAP\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nUse a known functioning radiator cap on the \nradiator. (a) If engine water temperature \nconti"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p739_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 739, "text": "diator hoses for soft or collapsed sections, cracks \nor ruptures, and leaks. (a) If radiator hoses are free from soft \nor collapsed sections, cracks or ruptures, and leaks, go to Step 6 of this fault.\n(b) If hoses have soft sections, cracks \nor ruptures, and/or leaks, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) \nand replace the hoses (Para 7-4). TR01450", "snippet": "diator hoses for soft or collapsed sections, cracks \nor ruptures, and leaks. (a) If radiator hoses are free from soft \nor collapsed sections, cracks or ruptures, and leaks, go to Step 6 of this fault."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p740_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 740, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-68814. ENGINE WATER TEMPERATURE ABOVE NORMAL (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine water \ntemperature is continuously \nabove normal 180 to 210 degrees F operating range. Engine coolant level OK.Air flow across radiator adequate.Fan drive belt OK. Radiator cap OK. Radiator hose(s) OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nThermostat faulty. Water pump faulty. Cooling passages in cooling \nsystem blocked.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf thermostat faulty, engine will \noverheat. KNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine water \ntemperature is continuously \nabove normal 180 to \n210 degrees F operating range. Engine coolant level OK. Air flow across radiator adequate. Fan drive belt OK.Radiator cap OK. Radiator hose(s) OK. Thermostat OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWater pump faulty.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-68814. ENGINE WATER TEMPERATURE ABOVE NORMAL (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine water \ntemperature is continuously \nabove normal 180 to 210 degrees F operating range. Engine coo"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p740_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 740, "text": " \n210 degrees F operating range. Engine coolant level OK. Air flow across radiator adequate. Fan drive belt OK.Radiator cap OK. Radiator hose(s) OK. Thermostat OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWater pump faulty.\nCooling passages in cooling \nsystem blocked.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf water pump faulty, engine will run \nhot.6\nIs thermostat functioning? YESNOReplace faulty \nthermostat (Para 7-3). 7\nIs water pump functioning \nproperly? YESNOReplace worn and/\nor faulty pump (Para 7-5).", "snippet": " \n210 degrees F operating range. Engine coolant level OK. Air flow across radiator adequate. Fan drive belt OK.Radiator cap OK. Radiator hose(s) OK. Thermostat OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWater pump faulty."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p741_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 741, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-689WATER \u001f\nPUMPTHERMOSTATTHERMOSTAT\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-\n10) and let it cool to ambient \ntemperature.\n(2) Relieve pressure on cooling system.\n(3) Remove, inspect, and test thermostat \nto determine if it is functioning \n(Para 7-3).\n(a) If thermostat is functioning, \nreinstall thermostat and go to \nStep 7 of this fault.\n(b) If thermostat is faulty, replace \nthermostat (Para 7-3).\nWATER PUMP\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nListen for rattles and/or squeals from water pump. Visually inspect water pump for leaks.\n(a) If pump is free from unusual noise \nand leaks, go to Step 8 of this fault.\n(b) If pump makes unusual noise and/\nor leaks, pump worn and/or faulty. \nShut down engine \n(TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace pump (Para 7-5).\nTR01451", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-689WATER \u001f\nPUMPTHERMOSTATTHERMOSTAT\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-\n10) and let it cool to ambient \ntemperature.\n(2) Relieve pressure on cooling system.\n("}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p742_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 742, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-69014. ENGINE WATER TEMPERATURE ABOVE NORMAL (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine water \ntemperature is continuously \nabove normal 180 to 210 degrees F operating range.\nEngine coolant level OK.Air flow across radiator adequate.Fan drive belt OK.\nRadiator cap OK.\nRadiator hose(s) OK.Thermostat OK.\nWater pump OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nCooling passages in cooling \nsystem blocked.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf cooling passages blocked, \nengine will overheat.8\nDoes coolant flow freely \nthrough the cooling passages \nof the system?\nYESNOFlush and refill \ncoolant system \n(Para 7-2).\nNotify Direct \nSupport about this \nfault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-69014. ENGINE WATER TEMPERATURE ABOVE NORMAL (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nIndicated engine water \ntemperature is continuously \nabove normal 180 to 210 degrees F operating range.\nEngine coo"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p743_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 743, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-691RADIATOR\u001f\nCAP\nRADIATOR\nDRAIN\u001f\nCOCKCOOLANT FLOW IN SYSTEM\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Remove radiator cap.\n(2) Run engine to operating temperature.\n(3) Visually check c oolant flow through \nradiator.\n(a) If coolant flows freely through \nradiator, but water temperature \nremains above normal, notify \nDirect Support about this fault.\n(b) If coolant does not flow freely, shut \ndown engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and flush and refill coolant system \n(Para 7-2).\nTR01452", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-691RADIATOR\u001f\nCAP\nRADIATOR\nDRAIN\u001f\nCOCKCOOLANT FLOW IN SYSTEM\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Remove radiator cap.\n(2) Run engine to operating temperature.\n(3) Visually check c oolant flow t"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p744_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 744, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6922\n15. TRANSMISSION OIL TEMPERATURE ABOVE NORMAL\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nIndicated transmission oil \ntemperature is continuously \nabove 250 degrees F. High transmission oil temperature \nwarning light is ON. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTransmission oil level low/high. Engine coolant level low.Main hydraulic pressure low. Transmission oil lines/cooler \nobstructed, leaking. Transmission oil filter dirty, \nobstructed. Vehicle brakes dragging.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-6922\n15. TRANSMISSION OIL TEMPERATURE ABOVE NORMAL\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p744_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 744, "text": "e coolant level low.Main hydraulic pressure low. Transmission oil lines/cooler \nobstructed, leaking. Transmission oil filter dirty, \nobstructed. Vehicle brakes dragging.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where \ntroubleshooting continues.1\nIs transmission oil at FULL \nmark on the dipstick?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOAdd or drain \ntransmission oil as \nnecessary (Para 9-6).", "snippet": "e coolant level low.Main hydraulic pressure low. Transmission oil lines/cooler \nobstructed, leaking. Transmission oil filter dirty, \nobstructed. Vehicle brakes dragging.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p745_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 745, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-693\nWARNING\n• Drycleaning solvent P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, \nsparks, or open flame. Flash point of solvent is 138 degrees F (59 degrees C). Use on ly in a well-ventilated area. Inhaling vap ors \nover a period of time can cause headache and drowsiness. Use gloves to prevent irrita tion or inflammation of the skin. Solvent \nabsorbed through the skin can result in internal disorders. If contact occurs, wash the affected area with water for 15 minutes . For \neyes, flush with water and then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment. Refer to \nTM9-247 for correct information.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-693\nWARNING\n• Drycleaning solvent P-D-680 (Type II) is a flammable solvent that is potentially dangerous to personnel. Keep away from heat, \nsparks, or open flame. Flash point of"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p745_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 745, "text": " and then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment. Refer to \nTM9-247 for correct information.\n• Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous. Make certain the air stream is directed away from user and other personnel in  \nthe area. To prevent injury, user must wear protec tive goggles or face shield when using compressed air. CAUTION\nWashing oil seals, electrical cables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. TRANSMISSION OIL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck transmission oil level with dipstick. Transmission oil should be at or near FULL \nmark. (a) If transmission oil at FULL mark, \ngo to Step 2 of this fault.", "snippet": " and then seek immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment. Refer to \nTM9-247 for correct information."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p745_chunk_2", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 745, "text": "als. TRANSMISSION OIL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck transmission oil level with dipstick. Transmission oil should be at or near FULL \nmark. (a) If transmission oil at FULL mark, \ngo to Step 2 of this fault.\n(b) If transmission oil is at or near ADD \nmark, add transmission oil as \nnecessary (Para 9-6). (c) If transmission oil is above FULL \nmark, drain transmission oil to proper level (Para 9-6). TR01208", "snippet": "als. TRANSMISSION OIL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck transmission oil level with dipstick. Transmission oil should be at or near FULL \nmark. (a) If transmission oil at FULL mark, \ngo to Step 2 of this fault."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p746_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 746, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-69415. TRANSMISSION OIL TEMPERATURE ABOVE NORMAL (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nIndicated transmission oil \ntemperature is continuously \nabove 250 degrees F. High transmission oil temperature \nwarning light is ON. Transmission oil level OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nEngine coolant level low. Main hydraulic pressure low. Transmission oil lines/cooler \nobstructed, leaking. Transmission oil filter dirty, \nobstructed. Vehicle brakes dragging.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf engine coolant level low, engine \nwill run hot. KNOWN INFO\nIndicated transmission oil \ntemperature is continuously \nabove 250 degrees F. High transmission oil temperature \nwarning light is ON. Transmission oil level OK. Engine coolant level OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nMain hydraulic pressure low.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-69415. TRANSMISSION OIL TEMPERATURE ABOVE NORMAL (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nIndicated transmission oil \ntemperature is continuously \nabove 250 degrees F. High transmission oil temperatur"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p746_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 746, "text": "erature is continuously \nabove 250 degrees F. High transmission oil temperature \nwarning light is ON. Transmission oil level OK. Engine coolant level OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nMain hydraulic pressure low.\nTransmission oil lines/cooler \nobstructed, leaking. Transmission oil filter dirty, \nobstructed. Vehicle brakes dragging.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic functions operate \nslowly and/or jerk, hydraulic \npressure is low.2\nIs engine coolant at proper \nlevel (1/3 to 2/3 full) in \noverflow tank? YESNOAdd coolant as \nneeded (Para 7-2). 3\nDo hydraulic functions (boom \ncylinder and attachment \ncylinders, for example) \noperate smoothly, without hesitation? YESNOPossible low hydraulic \npressure. Refer to the \nHydraulic Troubleshooting \nSection to correct \nfault.", "snippet": "erature is continuously \nabove 250 degrees F. High transmission oil temperature \nwarning light is ON. Transmission oil level OK. Engine coolant level OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nMain hydraulic pressure low."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p747_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 747, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-695OVERFLOW TANKENGINE COOLANT\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually check engine coolant level in \noverflow tank. Tank should be 1/3 to 2/3 full \n(Para 7-2).\n(a) If tank is 1/3 to 2/3 full, go to Step 3 \nof this fault.\n(b) If tank is not filled to proper level, \nadd coolant as needed (Para 7-2). \nHYDRAULIC PRESSURE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nOperate hydraulic functions (boom cylinder, attachment cylinders, for example) to check hydraulic pressure.\n(a) If functions operate smoothly, \nwithout hesitation, go to Step 4 of this fault.\n(b) If functions are sluggish, hesitate, \nor jerk, hydraulic pressure may be low. Refer to the Hydraulic \nTroubleshooting Section to correct \nfault.\nTR01209", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-695OVERFLOW TANKENGINE COOLANT\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually check engine coolant level in \noverflow tank. Tank should be 1/3 to 2/3 full \n(Para 7-2).\n(a) If tank is 1/3 to 2/3 full"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p748_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 748, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-69615. TRANSMISSION OIL TEMPERATURE ABOVE NORMAL (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nIndicated transmission oil \ntemperature is continuously \nabove 250 degrees F. High transmission oil temperature \nwarning light is ON. Transmission oil level OK.Engine coolant level OK. Main hydraulic pressure OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTransmission oil lines/cooler \nobstructed, leaking. Transmission oil filter dirty, \nobstructed. Vehicle brakes dragging.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf transmission oil lines and/or \ncooler obstructed or leaking, \ntransmission will run hot. KNOWN INFO\nIndicated transmission oil \ntemperature is continuously \nabove 250 degrees F. High transmission oil temperature \nwarning light is ON. Transmission oil level OK.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-69615. TRANSMISSION OIL TEMPERATURE ABOVE NORMAL (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nIndicated transmission oil \ntemperature is continuously \nabove 250 degrees F. High transmission oil temperatur"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p748_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 748, "text": " \ntransmission will run hot. KNOWN INFO\nIndicated transmission oil \ntemperature is continuously \nabove 250 degrees F. High transmission oil temperature \nwarning light is ON. Transmission oil level OK.\nEngine coolant level OK.Main hydraulic pressure OK. Transmission oil lines, cooler OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTransmission oil filter dirty, \nobstructed. Vehicle brakes dragging.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf transmission oil filter is dirty or \nobstructed, transmission oil will \noverheat.4\nAre transmission  oil lines and \ncooler free from signs of \nleaks or obstructions? YESNOReplace \ntransmission oil \nlines as necessary (Para 7-2  and Para 9-7). 5\nIs transmission oil filter clean \nand free from obstructions? YESNOReplace transmission \noil filter (Para 9-8).", "snippet": " \ntransmission will run hot. KNOWN INFO\nIndicated transmission oil \ntemperature is continuously \nabove 250 degrees F. High transmission oil temperature \nwarning light is ON. Transmission oil level OK."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p749_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 749, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-697TRANSMISSION\u001f\nOIL FILTERTRANSMISSION OIL LINES AND COOLER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect tr ansmission oil lines and \ncooler for signs of damage and leaks or \nobstructions.\n(a) If lines and cooler are tight, clean, \nand undamaged, go to Step 5 of this fault.\n(b) If lines and/or cooler show signs of \ndamage and/or leaks, replace components as necessary (Para 9-7).\nTRANSMISSION OIL FILTER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck transmission oil filter for dirt, damage, \nand/or obstructions.\n(a) If oil flows freely through filter, go to \nStep 6 of this fault.\n(b) If oil does not flow freely through \nfilter (filter obstructed and/or \ndamaged), replace transmission oil \nfilter (Para 9-8).TR01210", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-697TRANSMISSION\u001f\nOIL FILTERTRANSMISSION OIL LINES AND COOLER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect tr ansmission oil lines and \ncooler for signs of damage and leaks or \nobstructions"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p750_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 750, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-69815. TRANSMISSION OIL TEMPERATURE ABOVE NORMAL (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nIndicated transmission oil \ntemperature is continuously \nabove 250 degrees F.\nHigh transmission oil temperature \nwarning light is ON.\nTransmission oil level OK.Engine coolant level OK.\nMain hydraulic pressure OK.\nTransmission oil lines, cooler OK.Transmission oil filter OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nVehicle brakes dragging.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf vehicle brakes dragging, refer to \nthe Brakes Troubleshooting Sections to correct this fault.6\nAre vehicle brakes \nfunctioning correctly?\nYESNORefer to the Service \nand Parking Brakes \nTroubleshooting \nSections to correct this fault.\nReplace transmission oil \nfilter. If fault not \ncorrected, notify Direct Support about this fault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-69815. TRANSMISSION OIL TEMPERATURE ABOVE NORMAL (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nIndicated transmission oil \ntemperature is continuously \nabove 250 degrees F.\nHigh transmission oil temperatur"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p751_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 751, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-699BRAKE\u001f\nPEDAL\nLINKAGEVEHICLE BRAKES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck that vehicle brake pedal returns to full \nup position, and that park brake linkage is \nadjusted correctly.\n(a) If vehicle brakes are functioning \ncorrectly, replace transmission oil filter. If fault not corrected, notify Direct Support.\n(b) If vehicle brakes are not \nfunctioning correctly, refer to the Service and Parking Brakes \nTroubleshooting Sections to \ncorrect this fault.\nTR01211", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-699BRAKE\u001f\nPEDAL\nLINKAGEVEHICLE BRAKES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck that vehicle brake pedal returns to full \nup position, and that park brake linkage is \nadjusted correctly.\n(a) If ve"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p752_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 752, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7002\n16. TRANSMISSION DISCONNECT SLOW OR MALFUNCTIONING\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine running (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTransmission oil level low. Transmission oil pressure low. Transmission disconnect master \ncylinder fluid low. Transmission disconnect pedal \nlinkage faulty. Transmission disconnect cable \nassemblies faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7002\n16. TRANSMISSION DISCONNECT SLOW OR MALFUNCTIONING\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, A"}
