{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p753_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 753, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-701\nWARNING\n• Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment. Refer to \nTM9-247 for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous. Make certain the air stream is directed away from user and other personnel in  \nthe area. To prevent injury, user must wear protec tive goggles or face shield when using compressed air. CAUTION\nWashing oil seals, electrical cables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. TRANSMISSION OIL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck transmission oil level with dipstick.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-701\nWARNING\n• Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment. Refer to \nTM9-247 for correct information. • Eye shi"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p753_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 753, "text": "ables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. TRANSMISSION OIL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck transmission oil level with dipstick.\nTransmission oil should be at or near FULL \nmark. (a) If transmission oil at FULL mark, \ngo to Step 2 of this fault. (b) If transmission oil is at or near ADD \nmark, add transmission oil as \nnecessary (Para 9-6). TR01201", "snippet": "ables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. TRANSMISSION OIL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck transmission oil level with dipstick."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p754_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 754, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-70216. TRANSMISSION DISCONNECT SLOW OR MALFUNCTIONING (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nTransmission oil level OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTransmission oil pressure low. Transmission disconnect master \ncylinder fluid low. Transmission disconnect pedal \nlinkage faulty. Transmission disconnect cable \nassemblies faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nSTE/ICE-R #50. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf transmission oil pressure low, \nline leaks possible and/or faulty control valve. KNOWN INFO\nTransmission oil level OK.Transmission oil pressure OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTransmission disconnect master \ncylinder fluid low. Transmission disconnect pedal \nlinkage faulty. Transmission disconnect cable \nassemblies faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-70216. TRANSMISSION DISCONNECT SLOW OR MALFUNCTIONING (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nTransmission oil level OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTransmission oil pressure low. Transmission disconnect maste"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p754_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 754, "text": "K. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTransmission disconnect master \ncylinder fluid low. Transmission disconnect pedal \nlinkage faulty. Transmission disconnect cable \nassemblies faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf transmission disconnect master \ncylinder fluid level is  low, fault will \noccur.2\nIs transmission oil pressure \nwithin normal 120 to 220 PSI \noperating range? YESNOCheck for leaks along \ntransmission line and for faulty control valve. Replace components \nas necessary (Para 9-6 and \nPara 9-10). 3\nIs transmission disconnect \nmaster cylinder fluid at \ncorrect level? YESNOService transmission \ndisconnect master \ncylinder fluid level \n(LO 10-3930-673-12).", "snippet": "K. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTransmission disconnect master \ncylinder fluid low. Transmission disconnect pedal \nlinkage faulty. Transmission disconnect cable \nassemblies faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p755_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 755, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-703\nTRANSMISSION OIL PRESSURE\nSTE/ICE PRESSURE TEST\n(1) Install STE/ICE test device on port \nnumber 1 of transmission control valve. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #50 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P). (3) Read output pressure on test device. (a) If pressure is within 120 to 220 PSI \nat 2000 RPM, go to Step 3 of this \nfault. (b) If pressure is below specified \nrange, check for leaks along transmission lines, or for faulty \ncontrol valve. Shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace \ncomponents as necessary \n(Para 9-6 and Para 9-10). TRANSMISSION DISCONNECT \nMASTER CYLINDER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nLoosen retainer and remove cover from transmission disc onnect master cylinder. (a) If fluid level is below 1/4 in.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-703\nTRANSMISSION OIL PRESSURE\nSTE/ICE PRESSURE TEST\n(1) Install STE/ICE test device on port \nnumber 1 of transmission control valve. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #50 \n(TM 9-4910-571-"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p755_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 755, "text": "(Para 9-6 and Para 9-10). TRANSMISSION DISCONNECT \nMASTER CYLINDER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nLoosen retainer and remove cover from transmission disc onnect master cylinder. (a) If fluid level is below 1/4 in.\n(6.35 mm) of top, service transmission disconnect \nmaster cylinder fluid level \n(LO 10-3930-673-12). (b) If fluid level is within 1/4 in. (6.35 mm) of top, install cover with retainer. Go to Step 4 of this fault. TR01202", "snippet": "(Para 9-6 and Para 9-10). TRANSMISSION DISCONNECT \nMASTER CYLINDER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nLoosen retainer and remove cover from transmission disc onnect master cylinder. (a) If fluid level is below 1/4 in."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p756_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 756, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-70416. TRANSMISSION DISCONNECT SLOW OR MALFUNCTIONING (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nTransmission oil level OK. Transmission oil pressure OK. Transmission disconnect master \ncylinder fluid level OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTransmission disconnect pedal \nlinkage faulty. Transmission disconnect cable \nassemblies faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf transmission disconnect pedal \nlinkage worn or faulty, transission disconnect will malfunction. KNOWN INFO\nTransmission oil level OK.Transmission oil pressure OK. Transmission disconnect master \ncylinder fluid level OK. Transmission disconnect pedal \nlinkage OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTransmission disconnect cable \nassemblies faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-70416. TRANSMISSION DISCONNECT SLOW OR MALFUNCTIONING (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nTransmission oil level OK. Transmission oil pressure OK. Transmission disconnect master \ncylinder fluid l"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p756_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 756, "text": ". Transmission disconnect master \ncylinder fluid level OK. Transmission disconnect pedal \nlinkage OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTransmission disconnect cable \nassemblies faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf transmission disconnect cable \nand connections worn or \ndamaged, transmission disconnect will malfunction.4\nIs transmission disconnect \npedal linkage adjusted \ncorrectly and free from signs \nof wear? YESNOReplace worn or \nfaulty transmission \ndisconnect pedal \nlinkage as necessary (Para 9-4). 5\nAre transmission disconnect \ncable and connections free \nfrom signs of wear and/or \ndamage? YESNOReplace worn or \ndamaged cable and \nconnections (Para 9-3). Notify Direct \nSupport about this \nfault.", "snippet": ". Transmission disconnect master \ncylinder fluid level OK. Transmission disconnect pedal \nlinkage OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTransmission disconnect cable \nassemblies faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p757_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 757, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-705TRANSMISSION\u001f\nDISCONNECT\u001fPEDALBRAKE\u001fPEDAL\nLINKAGE\nLINKAGE\nTRANSMISSION DISCONNECT PEDAL \nLINKAGE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect tr ansmission disconnect \npedal linkage for incorrect adjustment and/or \nsigns of wear. (a) If linkage is correctly adjusted and \nfree from signs of wear, go to Step 5 of this fault. (b) If linkage is incorrectly adjusted, \nreadjust linkage (Para 9-4). If linkage shows signs of wear, \nreplace worn components \n(Para 9-4). TRANSMISSION DISCONNECT CABLE \nAND CONNECTIONS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect tr ansmission disconnect \ncable and connections for wear and/or damage. (a) If cable and/or connections show \nno signs of wear or damage, notify Direct Support about this fault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-705TRANSMISSION\u001f\nDISCONNECT\u001fPEDALBRAKE\u001fPEDAL\nLINKAGE\nLINKAGE\nTRANSMISSION DISCONNECT PEDAL \nLINKAGE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect tr ansmission disconnect \npedal linkage for"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p757_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 757, "text": "CTION\nVisually inspect tr ansmission disconnect \ncable and connections for wear and/or damage. (a) If cable and/or connections show \nno signs of wear or damage, notify Direct Support about this fault.\n(b) If cable and/or connections show \nsigns of wear and/or damage, replace worn or damaged cable \nand/or connections (Para 9-3).TR01203\nTR01299", "snippet": "CTION\nVisually inspect tr ansmission disconnect \ncable and connections for wear and/or damage. (a) If cable and/or connections show \nno signs of wear or damage, notify Direct Support about this fault."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p758_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 758, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7062\n17. STEERING PULLS TO RIGHT OR LEFT\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nMultimeter, Digital (Item 9, Appendix F)\nPressure Gage, 0 to 5000 PSI \n(Item 6, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (TM 10-3930-673-10)Parking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)Personnel Required\nTwo\nReferences\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nSteering pulls to left or right when \nvehicle is driven straight ahead. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nWheels out of alignment. One or more steering cylinders \nfaulty. Steering control valve faulty.Steering select valve faulty.Service brake caliper and/or rotor \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7062\n17. STEERING PULLS TO RIGHT OR LEFT\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nMultimeter, Digital (Item 9, Appendi"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p758_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 758, "text": "S\nWheels out of alignment. One or more steering cylinders \nfaulty. Steering control valve faulty.Steering select valve faulty.Service brake caliper and/or rotor \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nAre wheels correctly aligned?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNORealign front and/or \nrear wheels as necessary \n(Para 14-4).", "snippet": "S\nWheels out of alignment. One or more steering cylinders \nfaulty. Steering control valve faulty.Steering select valve faulty.Service brake caliper and/or rotor \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p759_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 759, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-707\nWARNING\n• Hydraulic oil in the system can be under pressure over 3000 psi with engine OFF. Always reliev e pressure in hydraulic lines \nbefore attempting to remove any component in the hydraulic system. With engine OFF, starter switch in RUN position, and MLRS \nattachment on the ground, move control levers through all operati ng positions several times to relieve levers through all opera ting \npositions several times to relieve live pres sure. Relieve pressure in hydraulic oil tank by live pressure. Relieve pressure in \nhydraulic oil tank by loos ening filler cap very slowly. Fail ure to follow these precautions c ould result in serious personal in jury. • At operating temperatures,  hydraulic oi l is hot and under pressure. Hot oil is hot and under pressure.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-707\nWARNING\n• Hydraulic oil in the system can be under pressure over 3000 psi with engine OFF. Always reliev e pressure in hydraulic lines \nbefore attempting to remove any compon"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p759_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 759, "text": " very slowly. Fail ure to follow these precautions c ould result in serious personal in jury. • At operating temperatures,  hydraulic oi l is hot and under pressure. Hot oil is hot and under pressure.\nHot oil can cause inju ries. Allow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines. CAUTION\n• Wipe the area clean around all hydraulic connections to be opened  during removal and disassembly. Cap oil lines and plug holes  \nafter removing lines. • Contamination of the hydraulic system  could result in premature failure. FRONT AND/OR REAR WHEEL \nALIGNMENT\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nMeasure distances between front tread surfaces of front and/or rear wheels, and \ndistances between rear tread surfaces of \nwheels to determine if front and/or rear wheels are correctly aligned (Para 14-4). (a) If distances between the front tread \nsurfaces and rear tread surfaces of wheels are within 1/8 in., go to \nStep 2 of this fault.", "snippet": " very slowly. Fail ure to follow these precautions c ould result in serious personal in jury. • At operating temperatures,  hydraulic oi l is hot and under pressure. Hot oil is hot and under pressure."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p759_chunk_2", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 759, "text": "ine if front and/or rear wheels are correctly aligned (Para 14-4). (a) If distances between the front tread \nsurfaces and rear tread surfaces of wheels are within 1/8 in., go to \nStep 2 of this fault.\n(b) If distances between the front tread \nsurfaces and rear tread surfaces of wheels are greater than 1/8 in., \nadjust tie rods to realign wheels (Para 14-4). TR01460", "snippet": "ine if front and/or rear wheels are correctly aligned (Para 14-4). (a) If distances between the front tread \nsurfaces and rear tread surfaces of wheels are within 1/8 in., go to \nStep 2 of this fault."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p760_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 760, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-70817. STEERING PULLS TO RIGHT OR LEFT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nSteering pulls to left or right when \nvehicle is driven straight ahead. Wheel alignment OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nOne or more steering cylinders \nfaulty. Steering control valve faulty. Steering select valve faulty. Service brake caliper and/or rotor \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf one or more steering cylinders \nfaulty, steering will pull to right or \nleft. KNOWN INFO\nSteering pulls to left or right when \nvehicle is driven straight ahead. Wheel alignment OK.Hydraulic steeri ng cylinders OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nSteering control valve faulty. Steering select valve faulty. Service brake caliper and/or rotor \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.Pressure test.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-70817. STEERING PULLS TO RIGHT OR LEFT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nSteering pulls to left or right when \nvehicle is driven straight ahead. Wheel alignment OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nOne or mor"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p760_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 760, "text": "ydraulic steeri ng cylinders OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nSteering control valve faulty. Steering select valve faulty. Service brake caliper and/or rotor \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.Pressure test.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil pressure is not at \n2500 PSI, priority valve is faulty.2\nAre hydraulic steering \ncylinders free from signs of \ndamage and/or leaks? YESNOReplace damaged or \nleaking hydraulic \nsteering cylinders (Para 14-7). 3\nIs hydraulic oil pressure at \nsteering control valve at \n2500 PSI? YESNOReplace faulty \nsteering control valve (Para 14-9).", "snippet": "ydraulic steeri ng cylinders OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nSteering control valve faulty. Steering select valve faulty. Service brake caliper and/or rotor \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.Pressure test."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p761_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 761, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-709\nHYDRAULIC STEERING CYLINDERS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydraul ic steering cylinders \nfor signs of damage and/or leaks.\n(a) If there are no signs of damage or \nleaks, go to Step 3 of this fault.\n(b) If there are signs of damage and/or \nleaks replace cylinders as \nnecessary (Para 14-7).\nSTEERING CONTROL VALVE\nVISUAL INSPECTION AND\nHYDRAULIC PRESSURE TEST\n(1) Use a test tee and a 0 to 5000 PSI \npressure gage to test hydraulic pressure at the steering control valve output line.\n(2) Read output pressure on test device.\n(a) If the hydraulic oil pressure is at \n2500 PSI, go to Step 4 of this fault.\n(b) If the hydraulic oil pressure is not \nat 2500 PSI, remove and replace the priority valve (Para 14-9).\nTR01461", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-709\nHYDRAULIC STEERING CYLINDERS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydraul ic steering cylinders \nfor signs of damage and/or leaks.\n(a) If there are no signs of damage or \nleaks"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p762_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 762, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-71017. STEERING PULLS TO RIGHT OR LEFT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nSteering pulls to left or right when \nvehicle is driven straight ahead. Wheel alignment OK. Hydraulic steeri ng cylinders OK. Steering control valve OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nSteering select valve faulty.Service brake caliper and/or rotor \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. Continuity test. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf continuity is not indicated at \nsolenoids, faulty steering select valve. KNOWN INFO\nSteering pulls to left or right when \nvehicle is driven straight ahead. Wheel alignment OK. Hydraulic steeri ng cylinders OK. Steering control valve OK. Steering select valve OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nService brake caliper and/or rotor \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspections.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-71017. STEERING PULLS TO RIGHT OR LEFT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nSteering pulls to left or right when \nvehicle is driven straight ahead. Wheel alignment OK. Hydraulic steeri ng cylinder"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p762_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 762, "text": "Wheel alignment OK. Hydraulic steeri ng cylinders OK. Steering control valve OK. Steering select valve OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nService brake caliper and/or rotor \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspections.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf brake calipers or rotors binding \nor faulty, notify Direct Support to \nreplace calipers.4\nIs continuity indicated at \nsolenoids on the steering \nselect valve? YESNOReplace faulty \nsteering select valve (Para 14-10). 5\nAre vehicle brakes \nfunctioning co rrectly (not \nbinding or dragging)? YESNONotify Direct Support to \nreplace calipers. Notify Direct Support \nabout this fault.", "snippet": "Wheel alignment OK. Hydraulic steeri ng cylinders OK. Steering control valve OK. Steering select valve OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nService brake caliper and/or rotor \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspections."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p763_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 763, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-711VEHICLE\u001f\nBRAKE\nCONNECTORSSOLENOID\nSOLENOIDSOLENOIDS AT STEERING SELECT \nVALVE\nCONTINUITY TEST\n(1) Set multimeter to ohms.\n(2) Perform a continuity test on solenoids \nat the steering select valve.\n(3) Read output of multimeter.\n(a) If multimeter indicates continuity, \ngo to Step 5 of this fault.\n(b) If continuity is not indicated, \nreplace steering select valve (Para 14-10).\nBRAKES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nOperate brakes to test brake calipers, rotors \nfor binding and/or dragging.\n(a) If brakes functioning correctly, \nnotify Direct Support about this fault.\n(b) If one or more service brakes \nbinding, dragging, notify Direct Support to replace calipers and/or \nrotors.\nTR01462", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-711VEHICLE\u001f\nBRAKE\nCONNECTORSSOLENOID\nSOLENOIDSOLENOIDS AT STEERING SELECT \nVALVE\nCONTINUITY TEST\n(1) Set multimeter to ohms.\n(2) Perform a continuity test on solenoids \nat the st"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p764_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 764, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7122\n18. SERVICE BRAKES CHATTER, ARE NOISY\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10.\nKNOWN INFO\nNone.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir in hydraulic brake lines.\nService brake pads faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where \ntroubleshooting continues.1\nBleed one or more brake \ncalipers. Is hydraulic brake oil \nclear and bubble free?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOContinue to bleed \nbrake calipers until hydraulic brake oil is \nclear (Para 12-5).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7122\n18. SERVICE BRAKES CHATTER, ARE NOISY\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut do"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p765_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 765, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-713\nWARNING\n• Be sure wheels are chocked before removing parking brake assembly. • Springs are under tension. Always wear  protective glasses when working on spri ngs under tension. Use care when removing \nsprings from brake pads. Failure to follow thes e precautions could result in personal injury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries. Allow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines. CAUTION\n• Wipe the area clean around all hydraulic connections to be opened  during removal and disassembly. Cap oil lines and plug holes  \nafter removing lines. • Contamination of the hydraulic system  could result in premature failure.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-713\nWARNING\n• Be sure wheels are chocked before removing parking brake assembly. • Springs are under tension. Always wear  protective glasses when working on spri ngs under tensi"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p765_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 765, "text": "all hydraulic connections to be opened  during removal and disassembly. Cap oil lines and plug holes  \nafter removing lines. • Contamination of the hydraulic system  could result in premature failure.\n• Washing oil seals, electrical cables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damag e or \ndestroy materials. BRAKE CALIPERS \nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Bleed one or more brake calipers to \ndetermine if there is air in brake lines \n(Para 12-5).(a) If hydraulic brake oil is clear and \nbubble free, go to Step 2 of this fault. (b) If hydraulic brake oil is cloudy or \ncontains bubbles, continue to bleed lines until air is removed from lines (Para 12-5). Go to \nStep (2) below. (2) Test service brakes. If fault is not \ncorrected, go to Step 2 of this fault. TR01199", "snippet": "all hydraulic connections to be opened  during removal and disassembly. Cap oil lines and plug holes  \nafter removing lines. • Contamination of the hydraulic system  could result in premature failure."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p766_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 766, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-71418. SERVICE BRAKES CHATTER, ARE NOISY (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nBrake lines free of air.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nService brake pads faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf service brake pads worn, service \nbrakes will chatter.2\nAre service brake pads free \nfrom signs of excessive \nwear?\nYESNOReplace service \nbrake pads as \nnecessary \n(Para 0-1).\nNotify Direct Support of \nthis fault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-71418. SERVICE BRAKES CHATTER, ARE NOISY (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nBrake lines free of air.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nService brake pads faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTI"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p767_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 767, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-715\nBRAKE PADS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Visually inspect service brake pads for \nuneven and/or excessive wear.\n(a) If brake pads appear OK, notify \nDirect Support of this fault.\n(b) If brake pads show uneven and/or \nexcessive wear, replace pads as necessary (Para 0-1). Go to Step (2) below.\n(2) Test service brakes. If fault is not \ncorrected, notify Direct Support of this fault.\nTR01200", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-715\nBRAKE PADS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Visually inspect service brake pads for \nuneven and/or excessive wear.\n(a) If brake pads appear OK, notify \nDirect Support of this fault.\n(b)"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p768_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 768, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7162\n19. BRAKES DRAG\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10.\nKNOWN INFO\nBrakes drag.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nService brake calipers faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf brake calipers dirty, oily, faulty, \ncalipers will need to be replaced.1\nAre service brake calipers \nfree from dirt and oil?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNONotify Direct \nSupport to correct \nthis fault.\nNotify Direct Support of \nthis fault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7162\n19. BRAKES DRAG\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (TM 10-3930-673-10)"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p769_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 769, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-717WARNING\n• Be sure wheels are chocked before removing parking brake assembly. • Springs are under tension. Always wear  protective glasses when working on spri ngs under tension. Use care when removing \nsprings from brake pads. Failure to follow thes e precautions could result in personal injury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries. Allow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines. CAUTION\n• Wipe the area clean around all hydraulic connections to be opened  during removal and disassembly. Cap oil lines and plug holes  \nafter removing lines. • Contamination of the hydraulic system  could result in premature failure.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-717WARNING\n• Be sure wheels are chocked before removing parking brake assembly. • Springs are under tension. Always wear  protective glasses when working on spri ngs under tensio"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p769_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 769, "text": "all hydraulic connections to be opened  during removal and disassembly. Cap oil lines and plug holes  \nafter removing lines. • Contamination of the hydraulic system  could result in premature failure.\n• Washing oil seals, electrical cables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damag e or \ndestroy materials. BRAKE CALIPERS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nInspect service brake calipers for dirt and oil. (a) If brake calipers are clean, notify \nDirect Support of this fault. (b) If brake calipers are dirty and show \nsigns of oil, notify Direct Support to \nreplace calipers.", "snippet": "all hydraulic connections to be opened  during removal and disassembly. Cap oil lines and plug holes  \nafter removing lines. • Contamination of the hydraulic system  could result in premature failure."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p770_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 770, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7182\n20. SERVICE BRAKES DO NOT STOP VEHICLE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10. KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nBrake lines leaking, damaged, \nobstructed. Air in hydraulic brake lines. Accumulator faulty. Service brake calipers faulty. Service brake pads faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7182\n20. SERVICE BRAKES DO NOT STOP VEHICLE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut d"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p770_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 770, "text": " POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nBrake lines leaking, damaged, \nobstructed. Air in hydraulic brake lines. Accumulator faulty. Service brake calipers faulty. Service brake pads faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nAre hydraulic brake lines and \nfittings free from signs of \nleaks and/or damage?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOReplace damaged \nand/or leaking lines and fittings as \nnecessary \n(Para 12-9).", "snippet": " POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nBrake lines leaking, damaged, \nobstructed. Air in hydraulic brake lines. Accumulator faulty. Service brake calipers faulty. Service brake pads faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p771_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 771, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-719\nWARNING\n• Be sure wheels are chocked before removing parking brake assembly. • Springs are under tension. Always wear  protective glasses when working on spri ngs under tension. Use care when removing \nsprings from brake pads. Failure to follow thes e precautions could result in personal injury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries. Allow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines. CAUTION\n• Wipe the area clean around all hydraulic connections to be opened  during removal and disassembly. Cap oil lines and plug holes  \nafter removing lines. • Contamination of the hydraulic system  could result in premature failure.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-719\nWARNING\n• Be sure wheels are chocked before removing parking brake assembly. • Springs are under tension. Always wear  protective glasses when working on spri ngs under tensi"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p771_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 771, "text": "all hydraulic connections to be opened  during removal and disassembly. Cap oil lines and plug holes  \nafter removing lines. • Contamination of the hydraulic system  could result in premature failure.\n• Washing oil seals, electrical cables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damag e or \ndestroy materials. BRAKE LINES AND FITTINGS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydraulic brake lines and \nfittings for breaks and leaks. (a) If lines and fittings are free from \nsigns of damage and/or leaks, go to Step 2 of this fault. (b) If lines and fittings show signs of \ndamage and/or leaks, replace lines and fittings as necessary (Para 12-9). TR01470", "snippet": "all hydraulic connections to be opened  during removal and disassembly. Cap oil lines and plug holes  \nafter removing lines. • Contamination of the hydraulic system  could result in premature failure."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p772_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 772, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-72020. SERVICE BRAKES DO NOT STOP VEHICLE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nBrake lines and fittings OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir in hydraulic brake lines. Accumulator faulty.Service brake calipers faulty. Service brake pads faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf air is in hydraulic brake lines, \nlines will need to be bled. KNOWN INFO\nBrake lines and fittings OK. Brake lines free of air. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAccumulator faulty.Service brake calipers faulty.Service brake pads faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf accumulator fails to charge, \nnitrogen precharge is low, accumulator is faulty.2\nBleed one or more brake \ncalipers. Is hydraulic brake oil \nclear and bubble free?", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-72020. SERVICE BRAKES DO NOT STOP VEHICLE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nBrake lines and fittings OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir in hydraulic brake lines. Accumulator faulty.Service brake caliper"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p772_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 772, "text": "al inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf accumulator fails to charge, \nnitrogen precharge is low, accumulator is faulty.2\nBleed one or more brake \ncalipers. Is hydraulic brake oil \nclear and bubble free?\nYESNOContinue to bleed \nbrake calipers until hydraulic brake oil is \nclear (Para 12-5). 3\nDoes accumulator charge \ncorrectly? Is nitrogen \nprecharge at specified \n900 PSI? YESNORecharge and/or \nreplace brake accumulator \n(Para 12-8).", "snippet": "al inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf accumulator fails to charge, \nnitrogen precharge is low, accumulator is faulty.2\nBleed one or more brake \ncalipers. Is hydraulic brake oil \nclear and bubble free?"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p773_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 773, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-721\nBRAKE CALIPERS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nBleed one or more brake calipers to \ndetermine if there is air in the brake lines \n(Para 12-5).\n(a) If hydraulic brake oil is clear and \nbubble free, go to Step 3 of this fault.\n(b) If hydraulic brake oil is cloudy or \ncontains bubbles, continue to bleed lines until air is removed from lines (Para 12-5).\nACCUMULATOR\nVISUAL INSPECTION AND\nPRECHARGE PRESSURE TEST\nVisually inspect brake accumulator for signs of damage. Remove accumulator and test precharge pressure (Para 12-8).\n(a) If accumulator shows no signs of \ndamage, and precharge is at specified pressure, go to Step 4 of \nthis fault.\n(b) If accumulator is damaged, replace \naccumulator (Para 12-8).\n(c) If precharge is not at 900 PSI, add \nnitrogen charge (Para 12-8).\nTR01471", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-721\nBRAKE CALIPERS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nBleed one or more brake calipers to \ndetermine if there is air in the brake lines \n(Para 12-5).\n(a) If hydraulic brake oil is clear and \nbubb"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p774_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 774, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-72220. SERVICE BRAKES DO NOT STOP VEHICLE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nBrake lines and fittings OK. Brake lines free of air. Brake accumulator OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nService brake calipers faulty. Service brake pads faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf service brake calipers dirty or \nshow signs of oil, service bakes \nwill not stop vehicle. KNOWN INFO\nBrake lines and fittings OK.Brake lines free of air. Brake accumulator OK. Service brake calipers OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nService brake pads faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf service brake pads worn, pads \nare faulty.4\nAre service brake calipers \nfree from dirt and oil? YESNONotify Direct Support to \ncorrect this fault. 5\nAre service brake pads free \nfrom signs of excessive \nwear?", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-72220. SERVICE BRAKES DO NOT STOP VEHICLE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nBrake lines and fittings OK. Brake lines free of air. Brake accumulator OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nService brake calipers "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p774_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 774, "text": "brake pads worn, pads \nare faulty.4\nAre service brake calipers \nfree from dirt and oil? YESNONotify Direct Support to \ncorrect this fault. 5\nAre service brake pads free \nfrom signs of excessive \nwear?\nYESNOReplace service \nbrake pads as \nnecessary (Para 0-1). Notify Direct Support \nabout this fault.", "snippet": "brake pads worn, pads \nare faulty.4\nAre service brake calipers \nfree from dirt and oil? YESNONotify Direct Support to \ncorrect this fault. 5\nAre service brake pads free \nfrom signs of excessive \nwear?"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p775_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 775, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-723\nBRAKE CALIPERS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nInspect service brake calipers for dirt and oil.\n(a) If brake calipers are clean, go to \nStep 5 of this fault.\n(b) If brake calipers are dirty and show \nsigns of oil, notify Direct Support to \nreplace calipers.\nBRAKE PADS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect service brake pads for uneven and/or excessive wear.\n(a) If brake pads appear OK, notify \nDirect Support about this fault.\n(b) If brake pads show uneven and/or \nexcessive wear, replace pads as \nnecessary (Para 0-1).TR01472", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-723\nBRAKE CALIPERS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nInspect service brake calipers for dirt and oil.\n(a) If brake calipers are clean, go to \nStep 5 of this fault.\n(b) If brake calipers are dirt"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p776_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 776, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7242\n21. PARKING BRAKE DOES NOT ENGAGE/DISENGAGE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nWheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)Personnel Required\nTwo\nReferences\nTM 10-3930-673-10. KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHydraulic hoses/connections \nfaulty. Parking brake valve faulty. Accumulator faulty. Parking brake faulty. Parking brake pads faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7242\n21. PARKING BRAKE DOES NOT ENGAGE/DISENGAGE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine s"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p776_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 776, "text": " KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHydraulic hoses/connections \nfaulty. Parking brake valve faulty. Accumulator faulty. Parking brake faulty. Parking brake pads faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nDoes parking brake light \nilluminate when parking brake \nswitch is turned to ON position?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOGo to Electrical \nTroubleshooting Fault 43, Parking \nbrake switch does \nnot operate.", "snippet": " KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHydraulic hoses/connections \nfaulty. Parking brake valve faulty. Accumulator faulty. Parking brake faulty. Parking brake pads faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p777_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 777, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-725PARKING BRAKE\u001f\nLIGHT\nPARKING BRAKE\u001f\nSWITCHWARNING\n• Be sure wheels are chocked before removing parking brake. • Springs are under tension. Always wear  protective glasses when working on spri ngs under tension. Use care when removing \nsprings from brake pads. Failure to follow thes e precautions could result in personal injury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries. Allow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines. CAUTION\n• Wipe the area clean around hydraulic connections to be opened duri ng removal and disassembly. Cap oil lines and plug holes \nafter removing lines.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-725PARKING BRAKE\u001f\nLIGHT\nPARKING BRAKE\u001f\nSWITCHWARNING\n• Be sure wheels are chocked before removing parking brake. • Springs are under tension. Always wear  protective glasses when"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p777_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 777, "text": " befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines. CAUTION\n• Wipe the area clean around hydraulic connections to be opened duri ng removal and disassembly. Cap oil lines and plug holes \nafter removing lines.\n• Washing oil seals, electrical cables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damag e or \ndestroy materials. VISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Turn engine start switch to ON position \n(TM 10-3930-673-10). (2) Turn parking brake switch to ON \nposition. (3) Check to determine if parking brake \nlight illuminates. (a) If light illuminates, go to Step 2 of \nthis fault. (b) If light does not illuminate, go to \nElectrical Troubleshooting Fault \n43, Parking brake switch does not operate. (4) Turn parking brake switch to OFF \nposition. (5) Turn engine start switch to OFF \nposition (TM 10-3930-673-10).", "snippet": " befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines. CAUTION\n• Wipe the area clean around hydraulic connections to be opened duri ng removal and disassembly. Cap oil lines and plug holes \nafter removing lines."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p778_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 778, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-72621. PARKING BRAKE DOES NOT ENGAGE/DISENGAGE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nParking brake switch OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHydraulic hoses/connections \nfaulty.\nParking brake valve faulty.\nAccumulator faulty.\nParking brake faulty.Parking brake pads faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hoses and connections are faulty \nor loose, parking brake will not engage/disengage.2\nAre hydraulic hoses and \nconnections between the \nparking brake valve, brake accumulator, and parking \nbrake free from signs of leaks \nand damage?\nYESNOReplace hoses and \ntighten connections \nas necessary \n(Para 12-2 and Para 12-9).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-72621. PARKING BRAKE DOES NOT ENGAGE/DISENGAGE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nParking brake switch OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHydraulic hoses/connections \nfaulty.\nParking brake valve faulty.\nAccu"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p779_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 779, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-727TO PARKING \nBRAKE VALVE\nPARKING \nBRAKE\nVALVETO\nPARKING \nBRAKE\nTO\nBRAKE\nVALVE BRAKE\nACCUMULATOR TO BRAKE\nACCUMULATORPARKING\nBRAKEVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect the hoses and connections \nfrom and to the parking brake valve, brake accumulator, and parking brake.\n(a) If hoses and connections are free \nfrom signs of leaks and damage, go to Step 3 of this fault.\n(b) If hoses and connections show \nsigns of leaks and damage, replace hoses and tighten \nconnections as necessary \n(Para 12-2 and Para 12-9).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-727TO PARKING \nBRAKE VALVE\nPARKING \nBRAKE\nVALVETO\nPARKING \nBRAKE\nTO\nBRAKE\nVALVE BRAKE\nACCUMULATOR TO BRAKE\nACCUMULATORPARKING\nBRAKEVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect the hoses an"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p780_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 780, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-72821. PARKING BRAKE DOES NOT ENGAGE/DISENGAGE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nParking brake switch OK.\nHoses and connections OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nParking brake valve faulty.\nAccumulator faulty.\nParking brake faulty.\nParking brake pads faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf parking brake valve is damaged \nor leaks, brake will not engage/disengage.3\nIs parking brake valve free \nfrom signs of leaks and \ndamage?\nYESNOReplace parking \nbrake valve \n(Para 12-4).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-72821. PARKING BRAKE DOES NOT ENGAGE/DISENGAGE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nParking brake switch OK.\nHoses and connections OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nParking brake valve faulty.\nAccumulator fau"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p781_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 781, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-729PARKING \u001f\nBRAKE VALVEVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect the parking brake valve for \nleaks and damage.\n(a) If valve is free from signs of leaks \nand damage, go to Step 4 of this \nfault.\n(b) If valve shows signs of leaks and \ndamage, replace parking brake valve (Para 12-4).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-729PARKING \u001f\nBRAKE VALVEVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect the parking brake valve for \nleaks and damage.\n(a) If valve is free from signs of leaks \nand damage, go to Step 4 of th"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p782_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 782, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-73021. PARKING BRAKE DOES NOT ENGAGE/DISENGAGE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nParking brake switch OK.\nHoses and connections OK.\nParking brake valve OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAccumulator faulty.\nParking brake faulty.\nParking brake pads faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf accumulator charging pressure is \nnot within specified psi, parking brake will not engage/disengage.4\nIs the brake accumulator \ncharging pressure properly \nadjusted?\nYESNOAdjust charging \npressure as \nnecessary (Para 12-8).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-73021. PARKING BRAKE DOES NOT ENGAGE/DISENGAGE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nParking brake switch OK.\nHoses and connections OK.\nParking brake valve OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAccumulator faulty."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p783_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 783, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-731ACCUMULATORPRESSURE TEST\nPerform a pressure test at the brake \naccumulator (Para 12-8).\n(a) If charging pressure is adjusted to \n2250 (±25) psi, go to Step 5 of this fault.\n(b) If charging pressure is not adjusted \nto 2250 (±25) psi, adjust charging pressure as necessary \n(Para 12-8).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-731ACCUMULATORPRESSURE TEST\nPerform a pressure test at the brake \naccumulator (Para 12-8).\n(a) If charging pressure is adjusted to \n2250 (±25) psi, go to Step 5 of this fault.\n(b"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p784_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 784, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-73221. PARKING BRAKE DOES NOT ENGAGE/DISENGAGE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nParking brake switch OK.\nHoses and connections OK.\nParking brake valve OK.Accumulator OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nParking brake faulty.Parking brake pads faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf parking brake is damaged or \nleaks, brake will not engage/disengage.5\nIs parking brake free from \nsigns of leaks and damage?\nYESNOReplace parking \nbrake assembly \n(Para 12-2).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-73221. PARKING BRAKE DOES NOT ENGAGE/DISENGAGE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nParking brake switch OK.\nHoses and connections OK.\nParking brake valve OK.Accumulator OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nPark"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p785_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 785, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-733FRONT\nDIFFERENTIAL\nHOUSINGPARKING\nBRAKEROTORVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect parki ng brake for leaks and \ndamage.\n(a) If parking brake is free from signs \nof leaks and damage, go to Step 6 \nof this fault.\n(b) If brake shows signs of leaks and \ndamage, replace parking brake assembly (Para 12-2).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-733FRONT\nDIFFERENTIAL\nHOUSINGPARKING\nBRAKEROTORVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect parki ng brake for leaks and \ndamage.\n(a) If parking brake is free from signs \nof leaks and dama"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p786_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 786, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-73421. PARKING BRAKE DOES NOT ENGAGE/DISENGAGE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nParking brake switch OK.\nHoses and connections OK.\nParking brake valve OK.Accumulator OK.\nParking brake OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nParking brake adjustment faulty.\nParking brake pads faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nBrake adjustment check.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf parking brake is not properly \nadjusted, brake will not  engage/disengage.6\nIs parking brake properly \nadjusted?\nYESNOAdjust parking brake \nas necessary \n(Para 12-2).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-73421. PARKING BRAKE DOES NOT ENGAGE/DISENGAGE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nParking brake switch OK.\nHoses and connections OK.\nParking brake valve OK.Accumulator OK.\nParking brake OK.\nPOSS"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p787_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 787, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-735PARKING\u001f\nBRAKEBRAKE ADJUSTMENT CHECK\n(1) Remove parking brake assembly from \nvehicle (Para 12-2).\n(2) Check brake for proper adjustment.\n(a) If brake is properly adjusted, go to \nStep 7 of this fault.\n(b) If brake is not properly adjusted, \nadjust the parking brake as \nnecessary (Para 12-2).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-735PARKING\u001f\nBRAKEBRAKE ADJUSTMENT CHECK\n(1) Remove parking brake assembly from \nvehicle (Para 12-2).\n(2) Check brake for proper adjustment.\n(a) If brake is properly adjusted, go "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p788_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 788, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-73621. PARKING BRAKE DOES NOT ENGAGE/DISENGAGE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nParking brake switch OK.\nHoses and connections OK.\nParking brake valve OK.Accumulator OK.\nParking brake OK.\nBrake adjustment OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nParking brake pads faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nBrake pads check.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf parking brake pads worn or \nfaulty, brake will not engage/disengage.7\nAre parking brake pads free \nfrom signs of wear and \ndamage?\nYESNONotify Direct \nSupport to replace parking brake pads.\nNotify Direct Support about this \nfault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-73621. PARKING BRAKE DOES NOT ENGAGE/DISENGAGE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nParking brake switch OK.\nHoses and connections OK.\nParking brake valve OK.Accumulator OK.\nParking brake OK.\nBrak"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p789_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 789, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-737PARKING\nBRAKEPARKING\nBRAKE\nPADSBRAKE PADS CHECK\n(1) With brake removed from vehicle, \ncheck parking brake pads (Para 12-2).\n(a) If brake pads are OK, notify Direct \nSupport about this fault.\n(b) If brake pads show signs of wear \nor are faulty, notify Direct Support to replace brake pads.\n(2) Install parking brake assembly on \nvehicle (Para 12-2).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-737PARKING\nBRAKEPARKING\nBRAKE\nPADSBRAKE PADS CHECK\n(1) With brake removed from vehicle, \ncheck parking brake pads (Para 12-2).\n(a) If brake pads are OK, notify Direct \nSupport ab"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p790_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 790, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7382\n22. COMPRESSION KNOCKS\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10.\nKNOWN INFO\nNone.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir in fuel system.\nFuel quality poor.\nEngine overloaded.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual Inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs fuel system free from air in \nfuel lines?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOCheck fuel flow and \nbleed system(Para 5-9).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7382\n22. COMPRESSION KNOCKS\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (TM 10-3930-6"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p791_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 791, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-739FUEL\u001f\nHOSEFUEL TRANSFER\u001f\nPUMP\nFUEL FILTERWARNING\n• Fuel is very flammable and can explode easily. To avoid serious injury or death, keep fuel aw ay from open fire and keep fire \nextinguisher within easy reach when working with fuel. Do not work on fuel system when engine is hot. Fuel can be ignited by ho t \nengine. When working with fuel, post signs that  read NO SMOKING WITHIN 50 FEET (15 m). • Pressure test procedure results in fuel under high pressure. Be sure that pressure test device is connected properly and use \nsafety shield during test. Failure to do so may result in injury to personnel. • Drycleaning Solvent (P-D-680) is TOXIC and flammable.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-739FUEL\u001f\nHOSEFUEL TRANSFER\u001f\nPUMP\nFUEL FILTERWARNING\n• Fuel is very flammable and can explode easily. To avoid serious injury or death, keep fuel aw ay from open fire and keep fir"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p791_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 791, "text": " Be sure that pressure test device is connected properly and use \nsafety shield during test. Failure to do so may result in injury to personnel. • Drycleaning Solvent (P-D-680) is TOXIC and flammable.\nWear protective goggles and gl oves; use only in a well-ventilated area; \navoid contact with skin, eyes, and clothes; and do not breathe vapors. Keep away from heat or flame. Never smoke when using \nsolvent; the flashpoint for type I drycl eaning solvent is 100°F (38°C), type II is 138°F (50°C), and type III is 200°F (93.3°C) . Failure \nto do so may result in injury or death to personnel. If perso nnel become dizzy while using cl eaning solvent, immediately get fresh \nair and medical help. If solvent contacts sk in or clothes, flush with cold water. If solvent contacts eyes, immediately flush eyes \nwith water and get immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment.", "snippet": " Be sure that pressure test device is connected properly and use \nsafety shield during test. Failure to do so may result in injury to personnel. • Drycleaning Solvent (P-D-680) is TOXIC and flammable."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p791_chunk_2", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 791, "text": "contacts eyes, immediately flush eyes \nwith water and get immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment.\nRefer to \nTM 9-247 for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous. Make certain the air stream is directed away from user and other personnel in  \nthe area. To prevent injury, user must wear protec tive goggles or face shield when using compressed air. CAUTION\nWashing oil seals, electrical cables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. FUEL FLOW\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck flow of fuel through fuel filter \n(Para 5-9). (a) If fuel flows freely without air \nbubbles, go to Step 2 of this fault. (b) If air is in fuel lines, check for air \nleaks then bleed system(Para 5-9).", "snippet": "contacts eyes, immediately flush eyes \nwith water and get immediate medical attention. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p791_chunk_3", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 791, "text": " flow of fuel through fuel filter \n(Para 5-9). (a) If fuel flows freely without air \nbubbles, go to Step 2 of this fault. (b) If air is in fuel lines, check for air \nleaks then bleed system(Para 5-9).\nTR01444", "snippet": " flow of fuel through fuel filter \n(Para 5-9). (a) If fuel flows freely without air \nbubbles, go to Step 2 of this fault. (b) If air is in fuel lines, check for air \nleaks then bleed system(Para 5-9)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p792_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 792, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-74022. COMPRESSION KNOCKS (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nFuel system fuel OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFuel quality poor. Engine overloaded.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual Inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf fuel quality is poor, engine will \nproduce audible compression knocks. KNOWN INFO\nFuel system fuel OK.Fuel quality OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nEngine overloaded.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual Inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf machine is overloaded, or gear \nrange incorrect, engine will produce audible compression knocks.2\nIs fuel in fuel tank known to \nbe of good quality? YESNODrain and flush fuel \ntank and replace \nfuel \n(Para 5-9). 3\nIs payload within specified \nload range? Is gear range in \nuse correct for operation and load? YESNOIf overloaded, \nreduce load. If gear \nrange too high, shift \nto lower gear.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-74022. COMPRESSION KNOCKS (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nFuel system fuel OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFuel quality poor. Engine overloaded.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual Inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf fu"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p792_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 792, "text": "ace \nfuel \n(Para 5-9). 3\nIs payload within specified \nload range? Is gear range in \nuse correct for operation and load? YESNOIf overloaded, \nreduce load. If gear \nrange too high, shift \nto lower gear.\nNotify Direct Support about this fault.", "snippet": "ace \nfuel \n(Para 5-9). 3\nIs payload within specified \nload range? Is gear range in \nuse correct for operation and load? YESNOIf overloaded, \nreduce load. If gear \nrange too high, shift \nto lower gear."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p793_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 793, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-741FUEL TANK\nRANGE SELECT\nCONTROLFUEL QUALITY\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Check quality of fuel in fuel tank by \noperating engine from a temporary tank \nof known fuel quality.(a) Disconnect fuel line at fuel/water \nseparator, where line leads to fuel transfer pump. (b) Place disconnected end of fuel line \nin temp tank of known clean fuel. (c) Start engine and run it for \napproximately 5 minutes to \ndetermine if engine performance \nimproves with known clean fuel. (2) If fuel is of good quality, go to Step 3 of \nthis fault. (3) If fuel is of questionable quality, drain \nfuel tank and replace fuel (Para 5-9). LOAD AND GEAR RANGE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck that payload is within specified load \nrange for machine.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-741FUEL TANK\nRANGE SELECT\nCONTROLFUEL QUALITY\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Check quality of fuel in fuel tank by \noperating engine from a temporary tank \nof known fuel quality.(a) Disco"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p793_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 793, "text": "\nthis fault. (3) If fuel is of questionable quality, drain \nfuel tank and replace fuel (Para 5-9). LOAD AND GEAR RANGE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck that payload is within specified load \nrange for machine.\nCheck that gear range in use is correct for operation and load (TM 10-3930-673-10). (a) If load is within specified range and \ngear range is correct, notify Direct Support about this fault. (b) If load is above specified range, \nreduce load. If gear range too high, shift to lower gear\n(TM 10-3930-673-10). TR01445", "snippet": "\nthis fault. (3) If fuel is of questionable quality, drain \nfuel tank and replace fuel (Para 5-9). LOAD AND GEAR RANGE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck that payload is within specified load \nrange for machine."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p794_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 794, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7422\n23. LOSS OF POWER\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10. KNOWN INFO\nLoss of vehicle power. (Hydraulic system OK.)\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTransmission oil level low.Transmission oil temperature \nabove normal. Air intake leaks.Turbocharger, exhaust leaks.High pressure fuel leak. Air in fuel lines. Accelerator pedal or cable linkage \nfaulty. Turbocharger malfunctioning.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7422\n23. LOSS OF POWER\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (TM 10-3930-673-1"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p794_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 794, "text": "normal. Air intake leaks.Turbocharger, exhaust leaks.High pressure fuel leak. Air in fuel lines. Accelerator pedal or cable linkage \nfaulty. Turbocharger malfunctioning.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs transmission oil at FULL \nmark on dipstick?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOAdd transmission \noil as necessary \n(Para 9-6).", "snippet": "normal. Air intake leaks.Turbocharger, exhaust leaks.High pressure fuel leak. Air in fuel lines. Accelerator pedal or cable linkage \nfaulty. Turbocharger malfunctioning.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p795_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 795, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-743\nWARNING\n• Do not smoke or allow open flame or sparks in  the vicinity while working on any part of  the fuel system. Diesel fuel is highly  \nflammable and can cause injury or death if accidentally ignited. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment. Refer to \nTM9-247 for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous. Make certain the air stream is directed away from user and other personnel in  \nthe area. To prevent injury, user must wear protec tive goggles or face shield when using compressed air.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-743\nWARNING\n• Do not smoke or allow open flame or sparks in  the vicinity while working on any part of  the fuel system. Diesel fuel is highly  \nflammable and can cause injury or"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p795_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 795, "text": "are hazardous. Make certain the air stream is directed away from user and other personnel in  \nthe area. To prevent injury, user must wear protec tive goggles or face shield when using compressed air.\nCAUTION\nWashing oil seals, electrical cables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. TRANSMISSION OIL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck transmission oil level with dipstick. Transmission oil should be at or near FULL mark. (a) If transmission oil is at FULL mark, \ngo to Step 2 of this fault. (b) If transmission oil is at or near ADD \nmark, add transmission oil as necessary (Para 9-6). TR01463", "snippet": "are hazardous. Make certain the air stream is directed away from user and other personnel in  \nthe area. To prevent injury, user must wear protec tive goggles or face shield when using compressed air."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p796_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 796, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-74423. LOSS OF POWER (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nLoss of vehicle power. (Hydraulic system OK.)\nTransmission oil level OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTransmission oil temperature \nabove normal. Air intake leaks.Turbocharger, exhaust leaks. High pressure fuel leak. Air in fuel lines.Accelerator pedal or cable linkage \nfaulty. Turbocharger malfunctioning.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf transmission oil temperature at \nor above 250 degrees F, refer to Fault 15, Transmission oil \ntemperature above normal on \nPage 2-692 to correct fault. KNOWN INFO\nLoss of vehicle power.(Hydraulic system OK.)\nTransmission oil level OK. Transmission oil temperature OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir intake leaks. Turbocharger, exhaust leaks. High pressure fuel leak.Air in fuel lines.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-74423. LOSS OF POWER (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nLoss of vehicle power. (Hydraulic system OK.)\nTransmission oil level OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTransmission oil temperature \nabove normal. Air"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p796_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 796, "text": "cle power.(Hydraulic system OK.)\nTransmission oil level OK. Transmission oil temperature OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir intake leaks. Turbocharger, exhaust leaks. High pressure fuel leak.Air in fuel lines.\nAccelerator pedal or cable linkage \nfaulty. Turbocharger malfunctioning.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf air intake and crossovers to \nturbocharger leaki ng, engine will \nlose power.2\nIs indicated transmission oil \ntemperature within normal \n(180 to 210 degrees F) \noperating range? YESNORefer to Fault 15, \nTransmission oil \ntemperature above \nnormal Troubleshooting Section to correct fault. 3\nAre the intake manifold and/or \ncrossovers free from signs of \nair leaks? YESNOReplace components \nas necessary\n(Para 4-9 and Para 5-7).", "snippet": "cle power.(Hydraulic system OK.)\nTransmission oil level OK. Transmission oil temperature OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir intake leaks. Turbocharger, exhaust leaks. High pressure fuel leak.Air in fuel lines."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p797_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 797, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-745AIR INTAKE TURBOCHARGERTRANSMISSION OIL\u001f\nTEMPERATURE GAGETRANSMISSION OIL TEMPERATURE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck transmission oil temperature gage to \ndetermine oil temperature.\n(a) If transmission oil temperature is \nwithin normal 180 to 210 degrees F operating range, go to Step 3 of \nthis fault.\n(b) If indicated temperature is at or \nnear 250 degrees F, refer to Fault 15, Transmission oil temperature above normal to \ncorrect fault.\nINTAKE MANIFOLD AND CROSSOVERS \nAIR LEAK\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect air inta ke and air crossovers \nto turbocharger for air leaks.\n(a) If there are no signs of air leaks, go \nto Step 4 of this fault.\n(b) If there are signs of air leaks, \nreplace components as necessary (Para 4-9 and Para 5-7).\nTR01464", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-745AIR INTAKE TURBOCHARGERTRANSMISSION OIL\u001f\nTEMPERATURE GAGETRANSMISSION OIL TEMPERATURE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck transmission oil temperature gage to \ndetermine oil temperature.\n"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p798_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 798, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-74623. LOSS OF POWER (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nLoss of vehicle power. (Hydraulic system OK.)\nTransmission oil level OK.Transmission oil temperature OK. Air intake manifold OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTurbocharger, exhaust leaks. High pressure fuel leak. Air in fuel lines. Accelerator pedal or cable linkage \nfaulty. Turbocharger malfunctioning.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf air leaks in turbocharger gasket \nand/or exhaust manifold, engine will lose power. KNOWN INFO\nLoss of vehicle power.(Hydraulic system OK.)\nTransmission oil level OK. Transmission oil temperature OK.Air intake manifold OK. Turbocharger, exhaust manifold \nOK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHigh pressure fuel leak.Air in fuel lines. Accelerator pedal or cable linkage \nfaulty.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-74623. LOSS OF POWER (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nLoss of vehicle power. (Hydraulic system OK.)\nTransmission oil level OK.Transmission oil temperature OK. Air intake manifold OK. POSSIBLE "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p798_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 798, "text": "OK. Transmission oil temperature OK.Air intake manifold OK. Turbocharger, exhaust manifold \nOK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHigh pressure fuel leak.Air in fuel lines. Accelerator pedal or cable linkage \nfaulty.\nTurbocharger malfunctioning.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf leak in high pressure fuel lines \nand/or fittings, li nes, engine will \nlose power.4\nIs turbocharger gasket free \nfrom leaks? Is exhaust \nmanifold free from signs of \ncracks? YESNOReplace gasket \n(Para 5-6) and/or replace exhaust \nmanifold (Para 4-8). 5\nAre high pressure fuel lines, \nfittings, sealing washers, \nand/or valve seals free from \nleaks? YESNOReplace lines, fittings, \nsealing washers, and/\nor valve seals as necessary \n(Para 5-11).", "snippet": "OK. Transmission oil temperature OK.Air intake manifold OK. Turbocharger, exhaust manifold \nOK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHigh pressure fuel leak.Air in fuel lines. Accelerator pedal or cable linkage \nfaulty."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p799_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 799, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-747TURBOCHARGERHIGH PRESSURE\u001f\nFUEL LINES\nEXHAUST\u001f\nMANIFOLD\nGASKETTURBOCHARGER AND EXHAUST \nMANIFOLD AIR LEAK\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect turbocharger gasket and \nexhaust manifold for signs of cracks and air leaks.\n(a) If there are no signs of cracks and \nair leaks, go to Step 5 of this fault.\n(b) If there are signs of cracks and/or \nair leaks, replace turbocharger gasket (Para 5-6), and/or exhaust manifold (Para 4-8) as necessary. \nHIGH PRESSURE FUEL LINES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect high pressure fuel lines and fittings for signs of leaks.\n(a) If lines, fittings, and/or seals free \nfrom signs of leaks, go to Step 6 of this fault.\n(b) If lines, fittings, and/or seals show \nsigns of leaks, replace components as necessary (Para 5-11).\nTR01465", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-747TURBOCHARGERHIGH PRESSURE\u001f\nFUEL LINES\nEXHAUST\u001f\nMANIFOLD\nGASKETTURBOCHARGER AND EXHAUST \nMANIFOLD AIR LEAK\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect turbocharger gasket and \nexhaust m"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p800_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 800, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-74823. LOSS OF POWER (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nLoss of vehicle power. (Hydraulic system OK.)\nTransmission oil level OK.Transmission oil temperature OK. Air intake manifold OK. Turbocharger, exhaust manifold \nOK. High pressure fuel lines OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir in fuel lines.Accelerator pedal or cable linkage \nfaulty. Turbocharger malfunctioning.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf air in fuel lines, engine will not \nrun smoothly. KNOWN INFO\nLoss of vehicle power.(Hydraulic system OK.)\nTransmission oil level OK. Transmission oil temperature OK.Air intake manifold OK. Turbocharger, exhaust manifold \nOK. High pressure fuel lines OK.Fuel lines OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAccelerator pedal or cable linkage \nfaulty.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-74823. LOSS OF POWER (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nLoss of vehicle power. (Hydraulic system OK.)\nTransmission oil level OK.Transmission oil temperature OK. Air intake manifold OK. Turbochar"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p800_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 800, "text": "OK. Transmission oil temperature OK.Air intake manifold OK. Turbocharger, exhaust manifold \nOK. High pressure fuel lines OK.Fuel lines OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAccelerator pedal or cable linkage \nfaulty.\nTurbocharger malfunctioning.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf accelerator pedal and/or linkage \nincorrectly adjusted or worn, \nengine will not run smoothly.6\nIs fuel system free from air in \nfuel lines? YESNOCheck fuel flow and \nbleed system as \nnecessary (Para 5-11). 7\nAre accelerator pedal and \nlinkage adjusted correctly and \nfree from signs of wear? YESNOAdjust or replace \naccelerator cable \nlinkage as necessary \n(Para 5-20 and \nPara 5-21).", "snippet": "OK. Transmission oil temperature OK.Air intake manifold OK. Turbocharger, exhaust manifold \nOK. High pressure fuel lines OK.Fuel lines OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAccelerator pedal or cable linkage \nfaulty."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p801_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 801, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-749FUEL\u001f\nHOSEFUEL TRANSFER\u001f\nPUMP\nFUEL FILTER\nACCELERATOR\u001f\nCABLE LINKAGEACCELERATOR\u001f\nCABLEACCELERATOR\u001f\nPEDALFUEL FLOW THROUGH FILTER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck flow of fuel through fuel filter \n(Para 5-11).\n(a) If fuel flows freely without air, go to \nStep 7 of this fault.\n(b) If air in fuel lines, check for air \nleaks, then bleed system \n(Para 5-11).\nACCELERATOR PEDAL AND/OR LINKAGE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck accelerator pedal and cable linkage \nfor adjustment and signs of wear (Para 5-20 \nand Para 5-21).\n(a) If pedal and linkage are adjusted \ncorrectly and are free from signs of wear, go to Step 8 of this fault.\n(b) If pedal and linkage are not \nadjusted correctly or are worn, adjust or replace pedal and/or linkage as necessary  (Para 5-20 \nand Para 5-21).\nTR01466", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-749FUEL\u001f\nHOSEFUEL TRANSFER\u001f\nPUMP\nFUEL FILTER\nACCELERATOR\u001f\nCABLE LINKAGEACCELERATOR\u001f\nCABLEACCELERATOR\u001f\nPEDALFUEL FLOW THROUGH FILTER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck flow of fuel through f"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p802_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 802, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-75023. LOSS OF POWER (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nLoss of vehicle power.\n(Hydraulic system OK.)\nTransmission oil level OK.Transmission oil temperature OK.\nAir intake manifold OK.\nTurbocharger, exhaust manifold \nOK.\nHigh pressure fuel lines OK.Accelerator pedal or cable linkage \nOK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTurbocharger malfunctioning.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf turbocharger malfunctioning or \nfaulty, engine will lose power.8\nIs turbocharger functioning \ncorrectly?\nYESNOReplace \nmalfunctioning or \nfaulty turbocharger \n(Para 5-6).\nNotify Direct Support of \nthis fault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-75023. LOSS OF POWER (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nLoss of vehicle power.\n(Hydraulic system OK.)\nTransmission oil level OK.Transmission oil temperature OK.\nAir intake manifold OK.\nTurbochar"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p803_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 803, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-751TURBOCHARGER\nEXHAUST\u001f\nMANIFOLD\nGASKETTURBOCHARGER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck turbocharger intake, air and oil lines \nfor debris. Check wastegate. Check exhaust \nmanifold for cracks(Para 5-6).\n(a) If turbocharger is functioning \ncorrectly, notify Direct Support about this fault. \n(b) If turbocharger shows signs of \ndebris, malfunctioning assemblies, or cracks, replace turbocharger \n(Para 5-6).\nTR01467", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-751TURBOCHARGER\nEXHAUST\u001f\nMANIFOLD\nGASKETTURBOCHARGER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck turbocharger intake, air and oil lines \nfor debris. Check wastegate. Check exhaust \nmanifold for crac"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p804_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 804, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7522\n24. ENGINE DOES NOT SHUT OFF\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nMultimeter (Item 9, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10. KNOWN INFO\nEngine does not shut off. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFuel shutoff solenoid faulty. Fumes in air intake.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. Resistance test. REASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs resistance of fuel shutoff \nsolenoid within specified 29.5 \n(±2.5) ohms?", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7522\n24. ENGINE DOES NOT SHUT OFF\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nMultimeter (Item 9, Appendix F)\nEquipment C"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p804_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 804, "text": "tance test. REASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs resistance of fuel shutoff \nsolenoid within specified 29.5 \n(±2.5) ohms?\nIs draw approximately one ampere?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNONotify Direct \nSupport to replace fuel shutoff \nsolenoid.", "snippet": "tance test. REASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs resistance of fuel shutoff \nsolenoid within specified 29.5 \n(±2.5) ohms?"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p805_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 805, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-753FUEL SHUTOFF SOLENOIDWARNING\n• Do not smoke or allow open flame or sparks in  the vicinity while working on any part of  the fuel system. Diesel fuel is highly  \nflammable and can cause injury or death if accidentally ignited. • Improper cleaning methods and use of unauthorized cleaning so lvents may injure personnel and damage equipment. Refer to \nTM9-247 for correct information. • Eye shields must be worn when cleaning with a wire brush. Fl ying rust and metal particles may cause injury to personnel. • Particles blown by compressed air are hazardous. Make certain the air stream is directed away from user and other personnel in  \nthe area. To prevent injury, user must wear protec tive goggles or face shield when using compressed air.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-753FUEL SHUTOFF SOLENOIDWARNING\n• Do not smoke or allow open flame or sparks in  the vicinity while working on any part of  the fuel system. Diesel fuel is highly  \nflammable and"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p805_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 805, "text": "are hazardous. Make certain the air stream is directed away from user and other personnel in  \nthe area. To prevent injury, user must wear protec tive goggles or face shield when using compressed air.\nCAUTION\nWashing oil seals, electrical cables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. RESISTANCE TEST AND \nDRAW OF SOLENOID\nSet multimeter to ohms to test resistance of \nfuel shutoff solenoid. Set multimeter to amps to test draw of solenoid (Para 5-2). (a) If resistance and draw are within \nspecified ohms and amperes, go to Step 2 of this fault. (b) If resistance and/or draw are not \nwithin specified ohms and amperes, notify Direct Support to \nreplace solenoid. TR01204", "snippet": "are hazardous. Make certain the air stream is directed away from user and other personnel in  \nthe area. To prevent injury, user must wear protec tive goggles or face shield when using compressed air."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p806_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 806, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-75424. ENGINE DOES NOT SHUT OFF (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nEngine does not shut off.\nFuel shutoff solenoid OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFumes in air intake.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf fumes in air intake assemblies, \nassemblies will need to be \ncleared.2\nAre air intake assemblies free \nof fumes which cause \ncontinued dieseling?\nYESNOCheck air intake \nassemblies to \ndetermine source \nof fumes. Replace faulty assemblies \nas necessary.\nNotify Direct Support about this fault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-75424. ENGINE DOES NOT SHUT OFF (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nEngine does not shut off.\nFuel shutoff solenoid OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFumes in air intake.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASO"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p807_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 807, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-755\nAIR INTAKE ASSEMBLIES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Check air intake assemblies to \ndetermine source of fumes which \ncause continued dieseling.(a) If air intake assemblies are clear \nand functioning correctly, notify Direct Support of this fault.\n(b) If source of fumes is determined, \nreplace air intake assemblies as necessary.\n(2) Start engine, then shut off engine. If \nengine shuts off, fault corrected. If not, notify Direct Support of this fault.\nTR01205", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-755\nAIR INTAKE ASSEMBLIES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Check air intake assemblies to \ndetermine source of fumes which \ncause continued dieseling.(a) If air intake assemblies are clear "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p808_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 808, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7562\n25. CAB HEATER DOES NOT HEAT CAB\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nTemperature control adjusted to \nheat cab. Cab heater does not heat cab. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nLight OFF, switch faulty. Control valve cable assembly \nfaulty. Engine water temperature low.Control valve, hoses, or \nconnections faulty. Heater obstructed or damaged.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7562\n25. CAB HEATER DOES NOT HEAT CAB\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (T"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p808_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 808, "text": "\nLight OFF, switch faulty. Control valve cable assembly \nfaulty. Engine water temperature low.Control valve, hoses, or \nconnections faulty. Heater obstructed or damaged.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs cab heater/air conditioner \nswitch functioning (green \nswitch light ON, blower operating)?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOGo to Electrical System \nTroubleshooting Fault 29, Heater does not operate and/or \nFault 31, Cab blower \ndoes not operate.", "snippet": "\nLight OFF, switch faulty. Control valve cable assembly \nfaulty. Engine water temperature low.Control valve, hoses, or \nconnections faulty. Heater obstructed or damaged.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p809_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 809, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-757HEATER/AIR\u001f\nCONDITIONER\u001fSELECTOR SWITCHHEATER/AIR\u001fCONDITIONER\u001fBLOWER SWITCHWARNING\nAlways wear eye and hand protection when wo rking on an air conditioning system or compressor. Liquid refrigerant can cause \nfrostbite and/or blindness. CAUTION\nWashing oil seals, electrical cables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damage  or \ndestroy materials. HEATER/AIR CONDITIONER SWITCH\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nPress cab heater/air conditioner switch to ON \nposition. (a) If green switch light is ON and \nblower functions, go to Step 2 of this fault. (b) If green switch light is not ON and \nblower does not function, go to Electrical System Trouble-shooting Fault 29, Heater does not operate \nand/or Fault 31, Cab blower does \nnot operate.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-757HEATER/AIR\u001f\nCONDITIONER\u001fSELECTOR SWITCHHEATER/AIR\u001fCONDITIONER\u001fBLOWER SWITCHWARNING\nAlways wear eye and hand protection when wo rking on an air conditioning system or compresso"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p809_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 809, "text": "his fault. (b) If green switch light is not ON and \nblower does not function, go to Electrical System Trouble-shooting Fault 29, Heater does not operate \nand/or Fault 31, Cab blower does \nnot operate.\nTR01480", "snippet": "his fault. (b) If green switch light is not ON and \nblower does not function, go to Electrical System Trouble-shooting Fault 29, Heater does not operate \nand/or Fault 31, Cab blower does \nnot operate."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p810_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 810, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-75825. CAB HEATER DOES NOT HEAT CAB (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nTemperature control adjusted to \nheat cab. Cab heater does not heat cab. Light ON, switch OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nControl valve cable assembly \nfaulty. Engine water temperature low. Control valve, hoses, or \nconnections faulty. Heater obstructed or damaged.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf control valve cable assembly \nfaulty, valve will not operate \nproperly. KNOWN INFO\nTemperature control adjusted to \nheat cab. Cab heater does not heat cab.Light ON, switch OK.Control valve cable assembly OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nEngine water temperature low.Control valve, hoses, or \nconnections faulty. Heater obstructed or damaged.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-75825. CAB HEATER DOES NOT HEAT CAB (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nTemperature control adjusted to \nheat cab. Cab heater does not heat cab. Light ON, switch OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nControl val"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p810_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 810, "text": "ON, switch OK.Control valve cable assembly OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nEngine water temperature low.Control valve, hoses, or \nconnections faulty. Heater obstructed or damaged.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf engine water temperature low, \nrefer to Fault 12, Engine water temperature below normal on Page 2-678.2\nIs heater control valve cable \nassembly correctly connected \nto control valve and \nfunctioning? YESNOReplace faulty \ncontrol valve cable assembly. 3\nIs engine water temperature \nwithin the normal (180 to \n210 degrees F) operating \nrange? YESNORefer to Fault 12, \nEngine water \ntemperature below normal on \nPage 2-678 to \ncorrect fault.", "snippet": "ON, switch OK.Control valve cable assembly OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nEngine water temperature low.Control valve, hoses, or \nconnections faulty. Heater obstructed or damaged.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p811_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 811, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-759HEATER\nCONTROL\nVALVE\nENGINE WATER\nTEMPERATURE\nGAGECONTROL VALVE CABLE ASSEMBLY \nAND CONNECTION TO CONTROL VALVE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nInspect heater control valve cable assembly \nand its connection to heater control valve (Para 17-8). (a) If control valve cable assembly is \ncorrectly connected to control valve and functioning, go to Step 3 \nof this fault. (b) If cable assembly is disconnected \nfrom control valve, connect assembly to valve (Para 17-8). (c) If cable assembly is damaged, \nreplace assembly (Para 17-8). ENGINE WATER TEMPERATURE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck engine water temperature gage to determine if water temperature is within the \nnormal (180 to 210 degrees F) operating range.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-759HEATER\nCONTROL\nVALVE\nENGINE WATER\nTEMPERATURE\nGAGECONTROL VALVE CABLE ASSEMBLY \nAND CONNECTION TO CONTROL VALVE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nInspect heater control valve cable assembly \n"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p811_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 811, "text": "ace assembly (Para 17-8). ENGINE WATER TEMPERATURE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck engine water temperature gage to determine if water temperature is within the \nnormal (180 to 210 degrees F) operating range.\n(a) If engine water temperature is \nwithin normal operating range, go to Step 4 of this fault. (b) If engine water temperature below \nnormal, refer to Fault 12, Engine water temperature below normal \non Page 2-678 to correct fault. TR01481", "snippet": "ace assembly (Para 17-8). ENGINE WATER TEMPERATURE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nCheck engine water temperature gage to determine if water temperature is within the \nnormal (180 to 210 degrees F) operating range."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p812_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 812, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-76025. CAB HEATER DOES NOT HEAT CAB (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nTemperature control adjusted to \nheat cab. Cab heater does not heat cab. Light ON, switch OK. Control valve cable assembly OK. Engine water temperature OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nControl valve, hoses, or \nconnections faulty. Heater obstructed or damaged.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nDamaged or leaking valve, hoses, \nor connections will affect heater operation. KNOWN INFO\nTemperature control adjusted to \nheat cab. Cab heater does not heat cab.Light ON, switch OK.Control valve cable assembly OK. Engine water temperature OK. Control valve, hoses, connections \nOK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHeater obstructed or damaged.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-76025. CAB HEATER DOES NOT HEAT CAB (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nTemperature control adjusted to \nheat cab. Cab heater does not heat cab. Light ON, switch OK. Control valve cable assembly "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p812_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 812, "text": ".Light ON, switch OK.Control valve cable assembly OK. Engine water temperature OK. Control valve, hoses, connections \nOK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHeater obstructed or damaged.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf heater obstructed or damaged, \nheater will not operate properly.4\nAre the control valve, heater \nhoses, and connections free \nfrom signs of damage and/or \nleaks? YESNOCheck valve, \nhoses, and connections for \ndamage and leaks. Replace as necessary \n(Para 17-8). 5\nAre heater fins free to radiate \nheat? Is heater free from \nsigns of damage? YESNOClean dirt and \ndebris from heater fins, or replace \ndamaged heater. Notify Direct Support about this fault.", "snippet": ".Light ON, switch OK.Control valve cable assembly OK. Engine water temperature OK. Control valve, hoses, connections \nOK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHeater obstructed or damaged.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p813_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 813, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-761HOSE\nELBOW\u001f\nCONNECTION\nHEATER\nHEATER\u001f\nFINSHEATER\u001f\nCONTROL\u001f\nVALVEVALVE, HOSES, CONNECTIONS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect heater control valve, heater \nhoses, and connections for damage and \nleaks. (a) If valve, hoses, and connections \nare clean, tight, and free from damage and leaks, go to Step 5 of this fault. (b) If valve, hoses, and/or connections \nshow signs of damage or leaks, replace as necessary (Para 17-7). HEATER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect heater fins for dirt and debris \nor signs of damage. (a) If heater fins are free to radiate \nheat and heater is free from signs of damage, notify Direct Support \nabout this fault. (b) If heater fins are blocked by dirt \nand debris, clean heater fins.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-761HOSE\nELBOW\u001f\nCONNECTION\nHEATER\nHEATER\u001f\nFINSHEATER\u001f\nCONTROL\u001f\nVALVEVALVE, HOSES, CONNECTIONS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect heater control valve, heater \nhoses, and connectio"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p813_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 813, "text": "e. (a) If heater fins are free to radiate \nheat and heater is free from signs of damage, notify Direct Support \nabout this fault. (b) If heater fins are blocked by dirt \nand debris, clean heater fins.\n(c) If heater shows signs of damage, \nrepair or replace heater \n(Para 17-7). TR01482", "snippet": "e. (a) If heater fins are free to radiate \nheat and heater is free from signs of damage, notify Direct Support \nabout this fault. (b) If heater fins are blocked by dirt \nand debris, clean heater fins."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p814_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 814, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7622\n26. AIR CONDITIONER DOES NOT COOL CAB\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nRefrigerant Leak Detect or (Item 13, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)References\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nAir conditioner does  not cool cab. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nLight OFF, switch faulty. Air conditioner belt loose, faulty. Lines, fittings leaking, faulty.Condenser obstructed, damaged. Filter-drier blocked, faulty. Evaporator obstructed, damaged.Compressor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7622\n26. AIR CONDITIONER DOES NOT COOL CAB\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanic’s: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nRefrigerant Leak Detect or (Item 1"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p814_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 814, "text": "tioner belt loose, faulty. Lines, fittings leaking, faulty.Condenser obstructed, damaged. Filter-drier blocked, faulty. Evaporator obstructed, damaged.Compressor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs cab heater/air conditioner \nswitch functioning (green \nswitch light ON, blower operating)?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOGo to Electrical System \nTroubleshooting, Fault 30, Air conditioner \ndoes not operate.", "snippet": "tioner belt loose, faulty. Lines, fittings leaking, faulty.Condenser obstructed, damaged. Filter-drier blocked, faulty. Evaporator obstructed, damaged.Compressor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p815_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 815, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-763HEATER/AIR\u001f\nCONDITIONER\u001fSELECTOR SWITCHHEATER/AIR\u001fCONDITIONER\u001fBLOWER SWITCHWARNING\nAlways wear eye and hand protection when wo rking on an air conditioning system or compressor. Liquid refrigerant can cause \nfrostbite and/or blindness. CAUTION\n• Before disconnecting lines, make sure refrigerant has been remove d from air conditioning system  by recovering it with \nappropriate recovery equipment. Do not mix refrigerant in same recovery equipment. • When working on compressors, always relieve internal pressure first. Internal compressor pressure can be relieved by removing \noil plug, or by removing sh ipping caps/pads from ports. • Keep refrigerants and oils away from open flames. Refrigerants can produce poisonous gases in open flames. Work in well-\nventilated area.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-763HEATER/AIR\u001f\nCONDITIONER\u001fSELECTOR SWITCHHEATER/AIR\u001fCONDITIONER\u001fBLOWER SWITCHWARNING\nAlways wear eye and hand protection when wo rking on an air conditioning system or compresso"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p815_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 815, "text": "ng \noil plug, or by removing sh ipping caps/pads from ports. • Keep refrigerants and oils away from open flames. Refrigerants can produce poisonous gases in open flames. Work in well-\nventilated area.\n• Do not introduce compressed air into an air c onditioning system due to danger of contamination. • Washing oil seals, electrical cables and fl exible hoses with dry cleaning solvents or mineral spirits will cause serious damag e or \ndestroy materials. HEATER/AIR CONDITIONER SWITCH\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nPress cab heater/air conditioner switch to ON \nposition. (a) If green switch light is ON and \nblower functions, go to Step 2 of this fault. (b) If green switch light is not ON and \nblower does not function, go to Electrical System Trouble-shooting \nFault 30, Air conditioner does not \noperate. TR01475", "snippet": "ng \noil plug, or by removing sh ipping caps/pads from ports. • Keep refrigerants and oils away from open flames. Refrigerants can produce poisonous gases in open flames. Work in well-\nventilated area."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p816_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 816, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-76426. AIR CONDITIONER DOES  NOT COOL CAB (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nAir conditioner does  not cool cab. Light ON, switch OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir conditioner belt loose, faulty. Lines, fittings leaking, faulty. Condenser obstructed, damaged. Filter-drier blocked, faulty.Evaporator obstructed, damaged. Compressor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf air conditioner belt loose or \nfaulty, air conditioner will not \noperate properly. KNOWN INFO\nAir conditioner does  not cool cab. Light ON, switch OK. Air conditioner belt OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nLines, fittings leaking, faulty. Condenser obstructed, damaged. Filter-drier blocked, faulty. Evaporator obstructed, damaged.Compressor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-76426. AIR CONDITIONER DOES  NOT COOL CAB (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nAir conditioner does  not cool cab. Light ON, switch OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAir conditioner belt loose, faulty. Lines,"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p816_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 816, "text": "elt OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nLines, fittings leaking, faulty. Condenser obstructed, damaged. Filter-drier blocked, faulty. Evaporator obstructed, damaged.Compressor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf lines and/or fittings are leaking, \nair conditioner will not operate properly.2\nIs air conditioner belt \nadjusted properly and free \nfrom signs of wear? YESNOAdjust tension or \nreplace belt. 3\nAre air conditioning lines and/\nor fittings clean, tight, and \nfree from leaks? YESNOCheck lines and \nfittings for leaks, \nand replace lines and fittings as \nnecessary.", "snippet": "elt OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nLines, fittings leaking, faulty. Condenser obstructed, damaged. Filter-drier blocked, faulty. Evaporator obstructed, damaged.Compressor faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p817_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 817, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-765RADIATORCONDENSOR\nCONNECTIONSAIR CONDITIONER\u001f\nBELT\nAIR\u001f\nCONDITIONER\u001f\nCOMPRESSORPULLEYAIR CONDITIONER BELT\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nInspect air conditioner belt for proper tension \nand/or signs of wear. (a) If tension correct and belt free from \nsigns of wear, go to Step 3 of this fault. (b) If tension is incorrect, tighten or \nloosen the belt as necessary (Para 7-8). (c) If belt shows signs of wear (cracks, \ndeteriorated sections), replace belt (Para 7-8). LINES AND FITTINGS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nUse a leak detector (which is sensitive to refrigerant in use) to  check air conditioning \nlines and fittings for leaks. (a) If lines and fittings are clean, tight, \nand free from leaks, go to Step 4 of \nthis fault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-765RADIATORCONDENSOR\nCONNECTIONSAIR CONDITIONER\u001f\nBELT\nAIR\u001f\nCONDITIONER\u001f\nCOMPRESSORPULLEYAIR CONDITIONER BELT\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nInspect air conditioner belt for proper tension \nan"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p817_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 817, "text": "ctor (which is sensitive to refrigerant in use) to  check air conditioning \nlines and fittings for leaks. (a) If lines and fittings are clean, tight, \nand free from leaks, go to Step 4 of \nthis fault.\n(b) If leaks are detected, evacuate \nsystem, replace faulty lines and/or fittings, and recharge system. TR01476", "snippet": "ctor (which is sensitive to refrigerant in use) to  check air conditioning \nlines and fittings for leaks. (a) If lines and fittings are clean, tight, \nand free from leaks, go to Step 4 of \nthis fault."}
