{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p839_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 839, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-781HYDRAULIC OIL TANKHYDRAULIC \u001f\nOIL LEVEL GAGEWARNING\n• Hydraulic oil in system can be under pressures over 3000 psi With engine OFF. Always relieve pres sure in hydraulic lines befor e \nattempting to remove any component in hydraulic system. Wi th engine OFF, starter switch in RUN position, and MLRS \nattachment on ground, move control levers through all operating pos itions several times to relieve live pressure. Relieve pressure \nin hydraulic oil tank by loosening filler cap very slowly. Failure to follow these precautions could result in serious personal  injury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries. Allow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-781HYDRAULIC OIL TANKHYDRAULIC \u001f\nOIL LEVEL GAGEWARNING\n• Hydraulic oil in system can be under pressures over 3000 psi With engine OFF. Always relieve pres sure in hydraulic lines"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p839_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 839, "text": " operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries. Allow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines.\nCAUTION\nWipe area clean around all hydrau lic connections to be opened during removal and disa ssembly. Cap oil lines and plug holes afte r \nremoving lines. Contamination of hydraulic s ystem could result in premature failure. HYDRAULIC OIL LEVEL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Visually inspect hydraulic oil level \ngauge to determine if tank is filled to \nproper level. (2) Hydraulic oil should be visible between \nADD and FULL marks.(a) If hydraulic oil is visible between \nADD and FULL marks, go to \nStep 2 of this fault. (b) If hydraulic oil is not visible, inspect \ntank for leaks. Then fill tank to FULL mark with hydraulic oil, \n(TM 10-3930-673-20). Go to Step 2 of this fault. TR01243", "snippet": " operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries. Allow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p840_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 840, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7822. RIGHT HAND FORK SIDESHIFT CYLINDER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nLeft hand fork cylinder extends and \nretracts. Hydraulic oil OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAttachment hydraulic hoses faulty. Attachment hose connections \nfaulty. Piston pump faulty.Attachment control valve faulty. Right hand fork cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf any hoses are cracked, kinked, \nor pinched, fork cylinder will not operate. KNOWN INFO\nLeft hand fork cylinder extends and \nretracts. Hydraulic oil OK. Hydraulic hoses OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAttachment hose connections \nfaulty. Piston pump faulty.Attachment control valve faulty. Right hand fork cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. STE/ICE-R #51.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7822. RIGHT HAND FORK SIDESHIFT CYLINDER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nLeft hand fork cylinder extends and \nretracts. Hydraulic oil OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAttachment hydraul"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p840_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 840, "text": "ulic hoses OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAttachment hose connections \nfaulty. Piston pump faulty.Attachment control valve faulty. Right hand fork cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. STE/ICE-R #51.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf any hydraulic hose connections \nare leaking, fork cylinder will not operate.2\nAre hydraulic hoses leading \nto right hand fork sideshift \ncylinder free of cracked, kinked, or pinched sections? YESNOReplace faulty \nhoses (Para 18-25). 3\nAre hydraulic hose \nconnections leading to right \nhand fork sideshift cylinder tight and free of leaks? YESNOTighten or replace \nfaulty fittings \n(Para 18-25).", "snippet": "ulic hoses OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAttachment hose connections \nfaulty. Piston pump faulty.Attachment control valve faulty. Right hand fork cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. STE/ICE-R #51."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p841_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 841, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-783HYDRAULIC \nHOSES\nHYDRAULIC FITTINGSHYDRAULIC \nCONNECTIONSHYDRAULIC HOSES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hoses leading to \nright hand fork sideshift cylinder for cracks, \nkinks, or pinches. (a) If there are no cracks, kinks, or \npinches in hydraulic hoses, go to Step 3 of this fault. (b) If there are cracks, kinks, or \npinches in one or more of hydraulic hoses, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace \nhose(s) (Para 18-25). HYDRAULIC HOSE CONNECTIONS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hose connections \nleading to right hand fork  sideshift cylinder for \nleaks. (a) If there are no leaks at hydraulic \nhose connections, go to Step 4 of \nthis fault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-783HYDRAULIC \nHOSES\nHYDRAULIC FITTINGSHYDRAULIC \nCONNECTIONSHYDRAULIC HOSES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hoses leading to \nright hand fork sideshift cylinder for"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p841_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 841, "text": "SPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hose connections \nleading to right hand fork  sideshift cylinder for \nleaks. (a) If there are no leaks at hydraulic \nhose connections, go to Step 4 of \nthis fault.\n(b) If there are leaks at one or more of \nconnections, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and tighten or replace connection(s) \n(Para 18-25). TR01244", "snippet": "SPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hose connections \nleading to right hand fork  sideshift cylinder for \nleaks. (a) If there are no leaks at hydraulic \nhose connections, go to Step 4 of \nthis fault."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p842_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 842, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7842. RIGHT HAND FORK SIDESHIFT CYLINDER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nLeft hand fork cylinder extends and \nretracts. Hydraulic oil OK. Hydraulic hoses OK. Hose connections OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nPiston pump faulty. Attachment control valve faulty. Right hand fork cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test.STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil pressure is not \n160 psi, piston pump is faulty. KNOWN INFO\nLeft hand fork cylinder extends and \nretracts. Hydraulic oil OK. Hydraulic hoses OK.Hose connections OK. Piston pump OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAttachment control valve faulty. Right hand fork cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test.STE/ICE-R #51.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7842. RIGHT HAND FORK SIDESHIFT CYLINDER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nLeft hand fork cylinder extends and \nretracts. Hydraulic oil OK. Hydraulic hoses OK. Hose connections"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p842_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 842, "text": ". Hydraulic oil OK. Hydraulic hoses OK.Hose connections OK. Piston pump OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAttachment control valve faulty. Right hand fork cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test.STE/ICE-R #51.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil pressure is not \nbetween 160 and 3625 psi, attachment control valve is faulty.4\nIs hydraulic oil pressure at \npiston pump 160 psi \n(1103.2 kPa)? YESNOTest/replace faulty \npiston pump(Para 18-4). 5\nIs hydraulic oil pressure at \nattachment control valve \nbetween 160 psi (minimum stand-by pressure) and \n3625 psi (maximum operating \npressure) (1103.2 and \n24994.4 kPa)? YESNOReplace faulty \nattachment control \nvalve (Para 18-6).", "snippet": ". Hydraulic oil OK. Hydraulic hoses OK.Hose connections OK. Piston pump OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAttachment control valve faulty. Right hand fork cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test.STE/ICE-R #51."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p843_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 843, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-785COVER ATTACHMENT CONTROL VALVE\nNOTE\nHydraulic oil pressure should be chec ked only while engine is idling with no controls operated. If a function is \noperated, the pressure will rise up to 3625 psi. PISTON PUMP\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non piston pump. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P). (3) Read output pressure on test device. (a) If hydraulic oil pressure is 160 psi \n(1103.2 kPa), go to Step 5 of this \nfault. (b) If hydraulic oil pressure is not \n160 psi (1103.2 kPa), shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and test and replace piston pump \n(Para 18-4). ATTACHMENT CONTROL VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non attachment control valve.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-785COVER ATTACHMENT CONTROL VALVE\nNOTE\nHydraulic oil pressure should be chec ked only while engine is idling with no controls operated. If a function is \noperated, the pressure w"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p843_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 843, "text": "kPa), shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and test and replace piston pump \n(Para 18-4). ATTACHMENT CONTROL VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non attachment control valve.\n(2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P)\n(3) Read output pressure on test device. (a) If hydraulic oil pressure is between \n160 and 3625 psi (1103.2 and \n24994.4 kPa), go to Step 6 of this fault. (b) If hydraulic oil pressure is not \nbetween 160 and 3625 psi (1103.2 and 24994.4 kPa), shut down \nengine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and \nreplace attachment control valve (Para 18-6).TR01808\nTR01245", "snippet": "kPa), shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and test and replace piston pump \n(Para 18-4). ATTACHMENT CONTROL VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non attachment control valve."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p844_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 844, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7862. RIGHT HAND FORK SIDESHIFT CYLINDER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nLeft hand fork cylinder extends and \nretracts.\nHydraulic oil OK.\nHydraulic hoses OK.\nHose connections OK.\nPiston pump OK.Attachment control valve OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nRight hand fork cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nSTE/ICE-R #51.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf right hand fork cylinder or \nhousing is damaged, fork cylinder will not operate.6\nIs right hand fork sideshift \ncylinder or its housing free \nfrom signs of damage?\nYESNOReplace damaged \ncylinder and \nhousing \n(Para 18-28).\nGo to Electrical System Troubleshooting, \nFault 33, Electric joystick control does \nnot operate.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7862. RIGHT HAND FORK SIDESHIFT CYLINDER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nLeft hand fork cylinder extends and \nretracts.\nHydraulic oil OK.\nHydraulic hoses OK.\nHose connections"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p845_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 845, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-787HAND FORK CYLINDER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect right hand fork sideshift \ncylinder and its housing for signs of damage.\n(a) If right hand fork cylinder and/or its \nhousing are not damaged, see Electrical System Troubleshooting, \nFault 33, Electric joystick control does not operate.\n(b) If right hand fork cylinder and/or its \nhousing are damaged, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and \nreplace cylinder and its housing \n(Para 18-28).HYDRAULIC\u001f\nFORK CYLINDER\nTR01246", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-787HAND FORK CYLINDER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect right hand fork sideshift \ncylinder and its housing for signs of damage.\n(a) If right hand fork cylinder and/or its \nhous"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p846_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 846, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-788 Change 12\n3. FORK TILT CYLINDER DOES NOT OPERATE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nGage, 0-5000 psi\nEquipment Condition\nEngine running (TM 10-3930-673-10)Parking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)Personnel Required\nTwo\nReferences\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nLow hydraulic oil level. Attachment hydraulic hoses faulty. Attachment hose connections/\ntubes faulty. Piston pump faulty.Attachment control valve faulty.Fork tilt cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-788 Change 12\n3. FORK TILT CYLINDER DOES NOT OPERATE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appen"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p846_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 846, "text": "Attachment hydraulic hoses faulty. Attachment hose connections/\ntubes faulty. Piston pump faulty.Attachment control valve faulty.Fork tilt cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs hydraulic oil tank filled to \nproper level?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOFill hydr aulic oil \ntank to proper level \n(Para 18-2).", "snippet": "Attachment hydraulic hoses faulty. Attachment hose connections/\ntubes faulty. Piston pump faulty.Attachment control valve faulty.Fork tilt cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p847_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 847, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-789HYDRAULIC OIL TANKHYDRAULIC \u001f\nOIL LEVEL GAGEWARNING\n• Hydraulic oil in system can be under pressures over 3000 psi With engine OFF. Always relieve pres sure in hydraulic lines befor e \nattempting to remove any component in hydraulic system. Wi th engine OFF, starter switch in RUN position, and MLRS \nattachment on ground, move control levers through all operating pos itions several times to relieve live pressure. Relieve pressure \nin hydraulic oil tank by loosening filler cap very slowly. Failure to follow these precautions could result in serious personal  injury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries. Allow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-789HYDRAULIC OIL TANKHYDRAULIC \u001f\nOIL LEVEL GAGEWARNING\n• Hydraulic oil in system can be under pressures over 3000 psi With engine OFF. Always relieve pres sure in hydraulic lines"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p847_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 847, "text": " operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries. Allow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines.\nCAUTION\nWipe area clean around all hydrau lic connections to be opened during removal and disa ssembly. Cap oil lines and plug holes afte r \nremoving lines. Contamination of hydraulic s ystem could result in premature failure. HYDRAULIC OIL LEVEL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Visually inspect hydraulic oil level \ngauge to determine if tank is filled to \nproper level. (2) Hydraulic oil should be visible between \nADD and FULL marks.(a) If hydraulic oil is visible between \nADD and FULL marks, go to \nStep 2 of this fault. (b) If hydraulic oil is not visible, inspect \ntank for leaks. Then fill tank to FULL mark with hydraulic oil \n(TM 10-3930-673-20) and go to Step 2 of this fault. TR01247", "snippet": " operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries. Allow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p848_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 848, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7903. FORK TILT CYLINDER DO ES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAttachment hydraulic hoses faulty. Attachment hose connections/\ntubes faulty. Piston pump faulty.Attachment control valve faulty.Fork tilt cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf any hoses are cracked, kinked, \nor pinched, tilt cylinder will not operate. KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK. Hydraulic hoses OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAttachment hose connections/\ntubes faulty. Piston pump faulty. Attachment control valve faulty. Fork tilt cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7903. FORK TILT CYLINDER DO ES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAttachment hydraulic hoses faulty. Attachment hose connections/\ntubes faulty. Pi"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p848_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 848, "text": "ulic hoses OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAttachment hose connections/\ntubes faulty. Piston pump faulty. Attachment control valve faulty. Fork tilt cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf any hydraulic hose connections \nare leaking, tilt cylinder will not operate.2\nAre hydraulic hoses leading \nto fork tilt cylinder free of \ncracked, kinked, or pinched sections? YESNOReplace faulty \nhoses (Para 18-25). 3\nAre hydraulic hose \nconnections leading to fork \ntilt cylinder tight and free of leaks? Are hydraulic tubes \nundamaged and intact? YESNOTighten or replace \nfaulty fittings \n(Para 18-25).Replace damaged \ntube (Para 18-26).", "snippet": "ulic hoses OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAttachment hose connections/\ntubes faulty. Piston pump faulty. Attachment control valve faulty. Fork tilt cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p849_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 849, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-791HYDRAULIC \u001f\nHOSES\nHYDRAULIC \u001f\nCONNECTIONSHYDRAULIC HOSES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hoses leading to \nfork tilt cylinder for cr acks, kinks, or pinches. (a) If there are no cracks, kinks, or \npinches in hydraulic hoses, go to \nStep 3 of this fault. (b) If there are cracks, kinks, or \npinches in one or more of hydraulic hoses, shut down engine \n(TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace hose(s) (Para 18-25). HYDRAULIC HOSE CONNECTIONS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hose connections \nleading to fork tilt cylin der for leaks. Visually \ninspect hydraulic tubes  on cylinder housing. (a) If there are no leaks at hydraulic \nhose connections and tubes are not damaged, go to Step 4 of this fault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-791HYDRAULIC \u001f\nHOSES\nHYDRAULIC \u001f\nCONNECTIONSHYDRAULIC HOSES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hoses leading to \nfork tilt cylinder for cr acks, kinks, or pinches. (a)"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p849_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 849, "text": "o fork tilt cylin der for leaks. Visually \ninspect hydraulic tubes  on cylinder housing. (a) If there are no leaks at hydraulic \nhose connections and tubes are not damaged, go to Step 4 of this fault.\n(b) If there are leaks at one or more of \nconnections, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and tighten \nor replace connection(s) (Para \n18-25). If tube(s) are damaged, replace tube(s) (Para 18-26). TR01248", "snippet": "o fork tilt cylin der for leaks. Visually \ninspect hydraulic tubes  on cylinder housing. (a) If there are no leaks at hydraulic \nhose connections and tubes are not damaged, go to Step 4 of this fault."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p850_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 850, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7923. FORK TILT CYLINDER DO ES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK. Hydraulic hoses OK. hose/tube connections OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nPiston pump faulty. Attachment control valve faulty. Fork tilt cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil pressure is not \n160 psi, piston pump is faulty. KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK. Hydraulic hoses OK. Hose/tube connections OK. Piston pump OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAttachment control valve faulty. Fork tilt cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil pressure is not \nbetween 160 and 3625 psi, attachment control valve is faulty.4\nIs hydraulic oil pressure at \npiston pump 160 psi \n(1103.2 kPa)?", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7923. FORK TILT CYLINDER DO ES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK. Hydraulic hoses OK. hose/tube connections OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nPiston pump faulty. Attachment con"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p850_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 850, "text": "st. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil pressure is not \nbetween 160 and 3625 psi, attachment control valve is faulty.4\nIs hydraulic oil pressure at \npiston pump 160 psi \n(1103.2 kPa)?\nYESNOTest/replace faulty \npiston pump (Para 18-4). 5\nIs hydraulic oil pressure at \nattachment control valve \nbetween 160 psi (minimum stand-by pressure) and \n3625 psi (maximum operating \npressure) (1103.2 and \n24994.4 kPa)? YESNOReplace faulty \nattachment control \nvalve (Para 18-6).", "snippet": "st. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil pressure is not \nbetween 160 and 3625 psi, attachment control valve is faulty.4\nIs hydraulic oil pressure at \npiston pump 160 psi \n(1103.2 kPa)?"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p851_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 851, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-793COVER ATTACHMENT CONTROL VALVE\nNOTE\nHydraulic oil pressure should be chec ked only while engine is idling with no controls operated. If a function is \noperated, the pressure will rise up to 3625 psi. PISTON PUMP\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non piston pump. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P). (3) Read output pressure on test device. (a) If hydraulic oil pressure is 160 psi \n(1103.2 kPa), go to Step 5 of this \nfault. (b) If hydraulic oil pressure is not \n160 psi (1103.2 kPa), shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and test and replace piston pump \n(Para 18-4). ATTACHMENT CONTROL VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non attachment control valve.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-793COVER ATTACHMENT CONTROL VALVE\nNOTE\nHydraulic oil pressure should be chec ked only while engine is idling with no controls operated. If a function is \noperated, the pressure w"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p851_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 851, "text": "kPa), shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and test and replace piston pump \n(Para 18-4). ATTACHMENT CONTROL VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non attachment control valve.\n(2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P)\n(3) Read output pressure on test device. (a) If hydraulic oil pressure is between \n160 and 3625 psi (1103.2 and 24994.4 kPa), go to Step 6 of this \nfault. (b) If hydraulic oil pressure is not \nbetween 160 and 3625 psi (1103.2 and 24994.4 kPa), shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and \nreplace attachment control valve \n(Para 18-6). TR01249TR01809", "snippet": "kPa), shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and test and replace piston pump \n(Para 18-4). ATTACHMENT CONTROL VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non attachment control valve."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p852_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 852, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7943. FORK TILT CYLINDER DO ES NOT OPERATE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK.\nHydraulic hoses OK.\nHose/tube connections OK.Piston pump OK.\nAttachment control valve OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFork tilt cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf fork tilt cylinder or housing is \ndamaged, tilt cylinder will not operate.6\nIs fork tilt cylinder or its \nhousing free from signs of \ndamage?\nYESNOReplace damaged \ncylinder and \nhousing \n(Para 18-28).\nGo to Electrical System Troubleshooting, Fault 33, Electric joystick control does not operate.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7943. FORK TILT CYLINDER DO ES NOT OPERATE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK.\nHydraulic hoses OK.\nHose/tube connections OK.Piston pump OK.\nAttachment control valve OK.\nPOSSIBLE"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p853_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 853, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-795FORK TILT\u001f\nCYLINDERTILT CYLINDER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect fork tilt cylinder and its \nhousing for signs of damage.\n(a) If fork tilt cylinder and/or its housing \nare not damaged, see Electrical System Troubleshooting, Fault 33, \nElectric joystick control does not \noperate.\n(b) If fork tilt cylinder and/or its housing \nare damaged, shut down engine \n(TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace cylinder and its housing \n(Para 18-28).\nTR01250", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-795FORK TILT\u001f\nCYLINDERTILT CYLINDER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect fork tilt cylinder and its \nhousing for signs of damage.\n(a) If fork tilt cylinder and/or its housing \nare "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p854_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 854, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7962\n4. ATTACHMENT CYLINDER DOES NOT OPERATE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine running (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)Personnel Required\nTwo\nReferences\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nLow hydraulic oil level. Attachment hydraulic hoses faulty. Attachment hose connections/\ntubes faulty. Piston pump faulty.Attachment control valve faulty.Attachment cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7962\n4. ATTACHMENT CYLINDER DOES NOT OPERATE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nE"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p854_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 854, "text": "ttachment hydraulic hoses faulty. Attachment hose connections/\ntubes faulty. Piston pump faulty.Attachment control valve faulty.Attachment cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs hydraulic oil tank filled to \nproper level?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOFill hydr aulic oil \ntank to proper level \n(Para 18-2).", "snippet": "ttachment hydraulic hoses faulty. Attachment hose connections/\ntubes faulty. Piston pump faulty.Attachment control valve faulty.Attachment cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p855_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 855, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-797HYDRAULIC OIL TANKHYDRAULIC \u001f\nOIL LEVEL GAGEWARNING\n• Hydraulic oil in system can be under pressures over 3000 psi With engine OFF. Always relieve pres sure in hydraulic lines befor e \nattempting to remove any component in hydraulic system. Wi th engine OFF, starter switch in RUN position, and MLRS \nattachment on ground, move control levers through all operating pos itions several times to relieve live pressure. Relieve pressure \nin hydraulic oil tank by loosening filler cap very slowly. Failure to follow these precautions could result in serious personal  injury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries. Allow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-797HYDRAULIC OIL TANKHYDRAULIC \u001f\nOIL LEVEL GAGEWARNING\n• Hydraulic oil in system can be under pressures over 3000 psi With engine OFF. Always relieve pres sure in hydraulic lines"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p855_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 855, "text": " operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries. Allow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines.\nCAUTION\nWipe area clean around all hydrau lic connections to be opened during removal and disa ssembly. Cap oil lines and plug holes afte r \nremoving lines. Contamination of hydraulic s ystem could result in premature failure. HYDRAULIC OIL LEVEL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Visually inspect hydraulic oil level \ngauge to determine if tank is filled to \nproper level. (2) Hydraulic oil should be visible between \nADD and FULL marks.(a) If hydraulic oil is visible between \nADD and FULL marks, go to \nStep 2 of this fault. (b) If hydraulic oil is not visible, inspect \ntank for leaks. Then fill tank to FULL mark with hydraulic oil \n(TM 10-3930-673-20) and go to \nStep 2 of this fault. TR01251", "snippet": " operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries. Allow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p856_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 856, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7984. ATTACHMENT CYLINDER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAttachment hydraulic hoses faulty. Attachment hose connections/\ntubes faulty. Piston pump faulty.Attachment control valve faulty.Attachment cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf any hoses are cracked, kinked, \nor pinched, attachment cylinder will not operate. KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK. Hydraulic hoses OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAttachment hose connections/\ntubes faulty. Piston pump faulty. Attachment control valve faulty. Attachment cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-7984. ATTACHMENT CYLINDER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAttachment hydraulic hoses faulty. Attachment hose connections/\ntubes faulty. Pi"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p856_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 856, "text": "lic hoses OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAttachment hose connections/\ntubes faulty. Piston pump faulty. Attachment control valve faulty. Attachment cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf any hydraulic hose connections \nand tubes are leaking, attachment cylinder will not \noperate.2\nAre hydraulic hoses leading \nto attachment cylinder free of \ncracked, kinked, or pinched sections? YESNOReplace faulty \nhoses (Para 18-25). 3\nAre hydraulic hose \nconnections leading to \nattachment cylinder tight and free of leaks? Is hydraulic \ntube undamaged and intact? YESNOTighten or replace \nfaulty fittings \n(Para 18-25). Replace damaged tube (Para 18-26).", "snippet": "lic hoses OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAttachment hose connections/\ntubes faulty. Piston pump faulty. Attachment control valve faulty. Attachment cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p857_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 857, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-799HYDRAULIC\nHOSES\nHYDRAULIC\nCONNECTIONS HYDRAULIC HOSES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hoses leading to \nattachment cylinder for cracks, kinks, or \npinches. (a) If there are no cracks, kinks, or \npinches in hydraulic hoses, go to Step 3 of this fault. (b) If there are cracks, kinks, or \npinches in one or more of hydraulic hoses, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace \nhose(s) (Para 18-24). HYDRAULIC HOSE CONNECTIONS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hose connections \nleading to attachment cylinder for leaks. Visaully inspect hydr aulic tube on cylinder \nhousing. (a) If there are no leaks at hydraulic \nhose connections and tube is not damaged, go to Step 4 of this fault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-799HYDRAULIC\nHOSES\nHYDRAULIC\nCONNECTIONS HYDRAULIC HOSES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hoses leading to \nattachment cylinder for cracks, kinks, or \npinches. (a) I"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p857_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 857, "text": "g to attachment cylinder for leaks. Visaully inspect hydr aulic tube on cylinder \nhousing. (a) If there are no leaks at hydraulic \nhose connections and tube is not damaged, go to Step 4 of this fault.\n(b) If there are leaks at one or more of \nconnections, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and tighten \nor replace connection(s) \n(Para 18-25). If tube is damaged, replace tube (Para 18-26). TR01252", "snippet": "g to attachment cylinder for leaks. Visaully inspect hydr aulic tube on cylinder \nhousing. (a) If there are no leaks at hydraulic \nhose connections and tube is not damaged, go to Step 4 of this fault."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p858_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 858, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8004. ATTACHMENT CYLINDER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK. Hydraulic hoses OK. Hose/tube connections OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nPiston pump faulty. Attachment control valve faulty. Attachment cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil pressure is not \n160 psi, piston pump is faulty. KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK. Hydraulic hoses OK. Hose/tube connections OK. Piston pump OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAttachment control valve faulty. Attachment cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil pressure is not \nbetween 160 and 3625 psi, attachment control valve is faulty.4\nIs hydraulic oil pressure at \npiston pump 160 psi \n(1103.2 kPa)?", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8004. ATTACHMENT CYLINDER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK. Hydraulic hoses OK. Hose/tube connections OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nPiston pump faulty. Attachment con"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p858_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 858, "text": "st. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil pressure is not \nbetween 160 and 3625 psi, attachment control valve is faulty.4\nIs hydraulic oil pressure at \npiston pump 160 psi \n(1103.2 kPa)?\nYESNOTest/replace faulty \npiston pump (Para 18-4). 5\nIs hydraulic oil pressure at \nattachment control valve \nbetween 160 psi (minimum stand-by pressure) and \n3625 psi (maximum operating \npressure) (1103.2 and \n24994.4 kPa)? YESNOReplace faulty \nattachment control \nvalve (Para 18-6).", "snippet": "st. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil pressure is not \nbetween 160 and 3625 psi, attachment control valve is faulty.4\nIs hydraulic oil pressure at \npiston pump 160 psi \n(1103.2 kPa)?"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p859_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 859, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-801COVER ATTACHMENT CONTROL VALVE\nPISTON PUMP\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non piston pump. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P). (3) Read output pressure on test device. (a) If hydraulic oil pressure is 160 psi \n(1103.2 kPa), go to Step 5 of this \nfault. (b) If hydraulic oil pressure is not \n160 psi (1103.2 kPa), shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and \ntest and replace piston pump (Para 18-4). ATTACHMENT CONTROL VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non attachment control valve. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P)\n(3) Read output pressure on test device. (a) If hydraulic oil pressure is between \n160 and 3625 psi (1103.2 and \n24994.4 kPa), go to Step 6 of this \nfault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-801COVER ATTACHMENT CONTROL VALVE\nPISTON PUMP\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non piston pump. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P). (3) Rea"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p859_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 859, "text": "rform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P)\n(3) Read output pressure on test device. (a) If hydraulic oil pressure is between \n160 and 3625 psi (1103.2 and \n24994.4 kPa), go to Step 6 of this \nfault.\n(b) If hydraulic oil pressure is not \nbetween 160 and 3625 psi (1103.2 and 24994.4 kPa), shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and \nreplace attachment control valve \n(Para 18-6). TR01253TR01811", "snippet": "rform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P)\n(3) Read output pressure on test device. (a) If hydraulic oil pressure is between \n160 and 3625 psi (1103.2 and \n24994.4 kPa), go to Step 6 of this \nfault."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p860_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 860, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8024. ATTACHMENT CYLINDER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK.\nHydraulic hoses OK.\nHose/tube connections OK.Piston pump OK.\nAttachment control valve OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nAttachment cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf attachment cylinder or housing is \ndamaged, attachment cylinder will not operate.6\nIs attachment cylinder or its \nhousing free from signs of \ndamage?\nYESNOReplace damaged \ncylinder and \nhousing \n(Para 18-15).\nGo to Electrical System Troubleshooting, Fault 33, Electric joystick control does \nnot operate.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8024. ATTACHMENT CYLINDER DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK.\nHydraulic hoses OK.\nHose/tube connections OK.Piston pump OK.\nAttachment control valve OK.\nPOSSIBLE"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p861_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 861, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-803ATTACHMENT\u001f\nCYLINDERATTACHMENT CYLINDER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect attach ment cylinder and its \nhousing for signs of damage.\n(a) If attachment cylinder and/or its \nhousing are not damaged, see \nElectrical System Troubleshooting, \nFault 33, Electric joystick control does not operate.\n(b) If attachment cylinder and/or its \nhousing are damaged, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and \nreplace cylinder and its housing \n(Para 18-15).\nTR01254", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-803ATTACHMENT\u001f\nCYLINDERATTACHMENT CYLINDER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect attach ment cylinder and its \nhousing for signs of damage.\n(a) If attachment cylinder and/or its \nho"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p862_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 862, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8042\n5. LEFT AND/OR RIGHT HOIST CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine running (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)Personnel Required\nTwo\nReferences\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nLow hydraulic oil level. Hoist cylinder hydraulic hoses \nfaulty. Hoist cylinder hose connections/\ntubes faulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Main control valve faulty. Hydraulic joystick control valve \nfaulty. Left and/or right hoist cylinders \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. STE/ICE-R #51.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8042\n5. LEFT AND/OR RIGHT HOIST CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R ("}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p862_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 862, "text": "ns/\ntubes faulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Main control valve faulty. Hydraulic joystick control valve \nfaulty. Left and/or right hoist cylinders \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. STE/ICE-R #51.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs hydraulic oil tank filled to \nproper level?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOFill hydr aulic oil \ntank to proper level \n(Para 18-2).", "snippet": "ns/\ntubes faulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Main control valve faulty. Hydraulic joystick control valve \nfaulty. Left and/or right hoist cylinders \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. STE/ICE-R #51."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p863_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 863, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-805HYDRAULIC OIL TANKHYDRAULIC \u001f\nOIL LEVEL GAGEWARNING\n• Hydraulic oil in the system can be under pr essures over 3000 psi With engine OFF. Alwa ys relieve pressure in hydraulic lines \nbefore attempting to remove any component in the hydraulic system. With engine OFF, starter switch in RUN position, and MLRS \nattachment on the ground, move control levers through all operati ng positions several times to relieve live pressure. Relieve \npressure in hydraulic oil tank by looseni ng filler cap very slowly. Failure to follow these precautions could result in serious  \npersonal injury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-805HYDRAULIC OIL TANKHYDRAULIC \u001f\nOIL LEVEL GAGEWARNING\n• Hydraulic oil in the system can be under pr essures over 3000 psi With engine OFF. Alwa ys relieve pressure in hydraulic "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p863_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 863, "text": "ollow these precautions could result in serious  \npersonal injury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries.\nAllow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines. CAUTION\nWipe the area clean around all hydraulic conne ctions to be opened during removal and disa ssembly. Cap oil lines and plug holes \nafter removing lines. Contamination of the hydraulic system could result in premature failure. HYDRAULIC OIL LEVEL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Visually inspect hydraulic oil level \ngauge to determine if tank is filled to \nproper level (Para 18-2). (2) Hydraulic oil should be visible between \nADD and FULL marks.(a) If hydraulic oil is visible between \nADD and FULL marks, go to \nStep 2 of this fault. (b) If hydraulic oil is not visible, inspect \ntank for leaks. Then fill tank to FULL mark with hydraulic oil\n(Para 18-2). Go to Step 2 of this \nfault. TR01255", "snippet": "ollow these precautions could result in serious  \npersonal injury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p864_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 864, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8065. LEFT AND/OR RIGHT HOIST CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHoist cylinder hydraulic hoses \nfaulty. Hoist cylinder hose connections/\ntubes faulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Main control valve faulty. Hydraulic joystick control valve \nfaulty. Left and/or right hoist cylinders \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf any hoses are cracked, kinked, \nor pinched, hoist cylinders will not operate. KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK. Hoist cylinder hydraulic hoses OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHoist cylinder hose connections/\ntubes faulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Main control valve faulty. Hydraulic joystick control valve \nfaulty.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8065. LEFT AND/OR RIGHT HOIST CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHoist cylinder hydraulic hoses \nfaulty. Hoist cylinder hos"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p864_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 864, "text": "il OK. Hoist cylinder hydraulic hoses OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHoist cylinder hose connections/\ntubes faulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Main control valve faulty. Hydraulic joystick control valve \nfaulty.\nLeft and/or right hoist cylinders \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf any hydraulic hose connections/\ntubes are leaking, hoist cylinders will not operate.2\nAre hydraulic hoses leading \nto hoist cylinders free of \ncracked, kinked, or pinched sections? YESNOReplace faulty \nhoses (Para 18-25). 3\nAre hydraulic hose \nconnections leading to hoist \ncylinders tight and free of leaks? Are hydraulic tubes \nundamaged and intact? YESNOTighten or replace \nfaulty fittings\n(Para 18-25). Replace damaged tube (Para 18-26).", "snippet": "il OK. Hoist cylinder hydraulic hoses OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHoist cylinder hose connections/\ntubes faulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Main control valve faulty. Hydraulic joystick control valve \nfaulty."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p865_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 865, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-807\nHYDRAULIC HOSES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hoses leading to \nleft and/or right hoist cylinders for cracks, \nkinks, or pinches. (a) If there are no cracks, kinks, or \npinches in hydraulic hoses, go to \nStep 3 of this fault. (b) If there are cracks, kinks, or \npinches in one or more hydraulic hoses, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace \nhose(s) (Para 18-25). HYDRAULIC HOSE CONNECTIONS/\nTUBES VISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hose connections \nleading to hoist cylinder s for leaks. Visually \ninspect hydraulic tubes on cylinder housings. (a) If there are no leaks at hydraulic \nhose connections and tubes are \nnot damaged, go to Step 4 of this fault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-807\nHYDRAULIC HOSES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hoses leading to \nleft and/or right hoist cylinders for cracks, \nkinks, or pinches. (a) If there are no cracks, "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p865_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 865, "text": " to hoist cylinder s for leaks. Visually \ninspect hydraulic tubes on cylinder housings. (a) If there are no leaks at hydraulic \nhose connections and tubes are \nnot damaged, go to Step 4 of this fault.\n(b) If there are leaks at one or more \nconnections, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and tighten \nor replace connection(s) \n(Para 18-25). If tubes are damaged, replace tubes \n(Para 18-26). TR01256", "snippet": " to hoist cylinder s for leaks. Visually \ninspect hydraulic tubes on cylinder housings. (a) If there are no leaks at hydraulic \nhose connections and tubes are \nnot damaged, go to Step 4 of this fault."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p866_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 866, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-808      Change 15. LEFT AND/OR RIGHT HOIST CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK. Hoist cylinder hydraulic hoses OK. Hoist cylinder hose connections/\ntubes OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTandem gear pump faulty.Main control valve faulty.Hydraulic joystick control valve \nfaulty. Left and/or right hoist cylinders \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil flow rate is not at \n42.3 to 55.2 gpm (160 to 209 Lpm), tandem gear pump faulty. KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK. Hoist cylinder hydraulic hoses OK. Hoist cylinder hose connections/\ntubes OK. Tandem gear pump OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nMain control valve faulty. Hydraulic joystick control valve \nfaulty.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-808      Change 15. LEFT AND/OR RIGHT HOIST CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK. Hoist cylinder hydraulic hoses OK. Hoist cylinder hose connect"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p866_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 866, "text": "raulic oil OK. Hoist cylinder hydraulic hoses OK. Hoist cylinder hose connections/\ntubes OK. Tandem gear pump OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nMain control valve faulty. Hydraulic joystick control valve \nfaulty.\nLeft and/or right hoist cylinders \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test.STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil pressure is not at \n3050 +/- 50 psi (21029.75 +/- 345 kPa), main control valve \nfaulty.4\nIs hydraulic oil flow rate at \ntandem gear pump at 42.3 to \n55.2 gpm (160 to 209 Lpm)? YESNOTest/replace faulty \ntandem gear pump (Para 18-3). 5\nIs hydraulic oil pressure at \nmain control valve between \n3050 +/- 50 psi (21029.75 +/- 345 kPa)? YESNOReplace faulty main \ncontrol valve \n(Para 18-5).", "snippet": "raulic oil OK. Hoist cylinder hydraulic hoses OK. Hoist cylinder hose connections/\ntubes OK. Tandem gear pump OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nMain control valve faulty. Hydraulic joystick control valve \nfaulty."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p867_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 867, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1      2-809\nMAIN CONTROL VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non main control valve.\n(2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P).\n(3) Read output pressure on test device.\n(a) If hydraulic oil pressure is at 3050 \n+/- 50 psi (21029.75 +/- 345 kPa), \ngo to Step 6 of this fault.\n(b) If hydraulic oil pr essure is not at \n3050 +/- 50 psi (21029.75 +/- 345 kPa), shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace \nmain control valve (Para 18-5).\nTR01257TR01812", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1      2-809\nMAIN CONTROL VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non main control valve.\n(2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P).\n(3) Read "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p868_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 868, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-810      Change 15. LEFT AND/OR RIGHT HOIST CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK.\nHoist cylinder hydraulic hoses OK.\nHoist cylinder hose connections/\ntubes OK.\nMain control valve OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHydraulic joystick control valve \nfaulty.\nLeft and/or right hoist cylinders \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure Test.\nSTE/ICE-R #51.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic joysti ck control valve \ndoes not operate properly, control valve is faulty.6\nDoes hydraulic joystick \ncontrol valve operate \nproperly?\nYESNOReplace faulty \nhydraulic control valve (Para 18-11).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-810      Change 15. LEFT AND/OR RIGHT HOIST CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK.\nHoist cylinder hydraulic hoses OK.\nHoist cylinder hose connect"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p869_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 869, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1      2-811\nHYDRAULIC JOYSTICK CONTROL VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Place hydraulic joystick in neutral \nposition. Attach 3-way gage and check \npressure at “P” port on joystick.(a) If hydraulic oil pressure is null go to \nStep (2) of this fault. (b) If not, replace joystick control valve \n(Para 18-11). (2) Place hydraulic joystick in raise \nposition and then in lower position. Check pressure at input lines to main \ncontrol valve. (a) If hydraulic oil pressure is at 1700 \nto 1800 psi (11721 to 12411 kPa), \ngo to Step 7 of this fault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1      2-811\nHYDRAULIC JOYSTICK CONTROL VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Place hydraulic joystick in neutral \nposition. Attach 3-way gage and check \npressure at “P” port on joystick."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p869_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 869, "text": "ise \nposition and then in lower position. Check pressure at input lines to main \ncontrol valve. (a) If hydraulic oil pressure is at 1700 \nto 1800 psi (11721 to 12411 kPa), \ngo to Step 7 of this fault.\n(b) If hydraulic oil pr essure is not at \n1700 to 1800 psi (11721 to 12411 kPa), shut down engine (TM 10-\n3930-673-10) and replace the joystick control valve.TR01813\nTR01258NOTE\nRelieve accumulator pressure before remo ving pressure hose on hydraulic joystick.", "snippet": "ise \nposition and then in lower position. Check pressure at input lines to main \ncontrol valve. (a) If hydraulic oil pressure is at 1700 \nto 1800 psi (11721 to 12411 kPa), \ngo to Step 7 of this fault."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p870_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 870, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-812      Change 15. LEFT AND/OR RIGHT HOIST CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK.\nHoist cylinder hydraulic hoses OK.\nHoist cylinder hose connections/\ntubes OK.\nMain control valve OK.\nHydraulic joystick control valve OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nLeft and/or right hoist cylinders \nfaulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nSTE/ICE-R #51.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hoist cylinders or housings are \ndamaged, hoist cylinders will not operate.7\nAre hoist cylinders and their \nhousings free from signs of \ndamage?\nYESNOReplace damaged \ncylinder and \nhousing \n(Para 18-29).\nGo to Electrical Troubleshooting Section to continue.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-812      Change 15. LEFT AND/OR RIGHT HOIST CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK.\nHoist cylinder hydraulic hoses OK.\nHoist cylinder hose connect"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p871_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 871, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-813BOOM\u001f\nHOIST\u001fCYLINDERVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hoi st cylinders and their \nhousings for signs of damage.\n(a) If hoist cylinders and/or their \nhousings are not damaged, go to \nElectrical Troubleshooting Section to continue.\n(b) If hoist cylinders and/or their \nhousings are damaged, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and \nreplace cylinders and their \nhousings (Para 18-29).\nTR01259", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-813BOOM\u001f\nHOIST\u001fCYLINDERVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hoi st cylinders and their \nhousings for signs of damage.\n(a) If hoist cylinders and/or their \nhousings are not damaged,"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p872_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 872, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8142\n6. BOOM EXTEND CYLINDER SLOW OR DOES NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine running (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)Personnel Required\nTwo\nReferences\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nLow hydraulic oil level. Extend cylinder hydraulic hoses \nfaulty. Extend cylinder hose connections \nfaulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Main control valve faulty. Hydraulic joystick control valve \nfaulty. Extend cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. STE/ICE-R #51.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8142\n6. BOOM EXTEND CYLINDER SLOW OR DOES NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Ite"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p872_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 872, "text": "d cylinder hose connections \nfaulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Main control valve faulty. Hydraulic joystick control valve \nfaulty. Extend cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. STE/ICE-R #51.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.Is hydraulic oil tank filled to \nproper level?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOFill hydraulic oil \ntank to proper level \n(Para 18-2).", "snippet": "d cylinder hose connections \nfaulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Main control valve faulty. Hydraulic joystick control valve \nfaulty. Extend cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. STE/ICE-R #51."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p873_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 873, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-815HYDRAULIC OIL TANKHYDRAULIC \u001f\nOIL LEVEL GAGEWARNING\n• Hydraulic oil in the system can be under pr essures over 3000 psi With engine OFF. Alwa ys relieve pressure in hydraulic lines \nbefore attempting to remove any component in the hydraulic system. With engine OFF, starter switch in RUN position, and MLRS \nattachment on the ground, move control levers through all operati ng positions several times to relieve live pressure. Relieve \npressure in hydraulic oil tank by looseni ng filler cap very slowly. Failure to follow these precautions could result in serious  \npersonal injury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-815HYDRAULIC OIL TANKHYDRAULIC \u001f\nOIL LEVEL GAGEWARNING\n• Hydraulic oil in the system can be under pr essures over 3000 psi With engine OFF. Alwa ys relieve pressure in hydraulic "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p873_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 873, "text": "ollow these precautions could result in serious  \npersonal injury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries.\nAllow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines. CAUTION\nWipe the area clean around all hydraulic conne ctions to be opened during removal and disa ssembly. Cap oil lines and plug holes \nafter removing lines. Contamination of the hydraulic system could result in premature failure. HYDRAULIC OIL LEVEL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Visually inspect hydraulic oil level gage \nto determine if tank is filled to proper \nlevel (Para 18-2). (2) Hydraulic oil should be visible between \nADD and FULL marks.(a) If hydraulic oil is visible between \nADD and FULL marks, go to \nStep 2 of this fault. (b) If hydraulic oil is not visible, inspect \ntank for leaks. Then fill tank to FULL mark with hydraulic oil \n(Para 18-2). TR01261", "snippet": "ollow these precautions could result in serious  \npersonal injury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p874_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 874, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8166. BOOM EXTEND CYLINDER SLOW OR DOES NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nExtend cylinder hydraulic hoses \nfaulty. Extend cylinder hose connections \nfaulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Main control valve faulty. Hydraulic joystick control valve \nfaulty. Extend cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf any hoses are cracked, kinked, \nor pinched, extend cylinder will not function. KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK. Extend cylinder hydraulic hoses \nOK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nExtend cylinder hose connections \nfaulty. Tandem gear pump faulty.Main control valve faulty.Hydraulic joystick control valve \nfaulty. Extend cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8166. BOOM EXTEND CYLINDER SLOW OR DOES NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nExtend cylinder hydraulic hoses \nfaulty. Extend cylinder hose"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p874_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 874, "text": "tend cylinder hose connections \nfaulty. Tandem gear pump faulty.Main control valve faulty.Hydraulic joystick control valve \nfaulty. Extend cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic hose connections show \nsigns of leaks, hydraulic system will lose pressure.2\nAre hydraulic hoses leading \nto boom extend cylinder free \nof cracked, kinked, or pinched sections? YESNOReplace faulty \nhoses (Para 18-25). 3\nAre hydraulic hose \nconnections leading to boom \nextend cylinder tight and free of leaks? YESNOTighten or replace \nfaulty fittings\n(Para 18-25).", "snippet": "tend cylinder hose connections \nfaulty. Tandem gear pump faulty.Main control valve faulty.Hydraulic joystick control valve \nfaulty. Extend cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p875_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 875, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-817\nHYDRAULIC HOSES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hoses leading to \nextend cylinder for crac ks, kinks, or pinches. Note especially points of possible wear: at \nboom hoist pulley, for example, and at \ntensioner. (a) If there are no cracks, kinks, or \npinches in hydraulic hoses, go to Step 3 of this fault. (b) If there are cracks, kinks, or \npinches in one or more hydraulic hoses, shut down engine \n(TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace hose(s) (Para 18-25). HYDRAULIC HOSE CONNECTIONS \nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hose connections \nleading to extend cylinder for leaks. (a) If there are no leaks at hydraulic \nhose connections, go to Step 4 of \nthis fault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-817\nHYDRAULIC HOSES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hoses leading to \nextend cylinder for crac ks, kinks, or pinches. Note especially points of possible wear: at \nb"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p875_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 875, "text": "ONNECTIONS \nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hose connections \nleading to extend cylinder for leaks. (a) If there are no leaks at hydraulic \nhose connections, go to Step 4 of \nthis fault.\n(b) If there are leaks at one or more \nconnections, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and tighten \nor replace the connection(s) (Para 18-25).TR01262", "snippet": "ONNECTIONS \nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hose connections \nleading to extend cylinder for leaks. (a) If there are no leaks at hydraulic \nhose connections, go to Step 4 of \nthis fault."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p876_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 876, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-818      Change 16. BOOM EXTEND CYLINDER SLOW OR DOES NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK. Extend cylinder hydraulic hoses \nOK. Extend cylinder hose connections \nOK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTandem gear pump faulty. Main control valve faulty. Hydraulic joystick control valve \nfaulty. Extend cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test.STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil flow rate is not at \n41.3 to 55.2 gpm (156 to 209 Lpm), tandem gear pump faulty. KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK. Extend cylinder hydraulic hoses \nOK. Extend cylinder hose connections \nOK. Tandem gear pump OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nMain control valve faulty.Hydraulic joystick control valve \nfaulty. Extend cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test.STE/ICE-R #51.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-818      Change 16. BOOM EXTEND CYLINDER SLOW OR DOES NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK. Extend cylinder hydraulic hoses \nOK. Extend cylinder hose connect"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p876_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 876, "text": "linder hose connections \nOK. Tandem gear pump OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nMain control valve faulty.Hydraulic joystick control valve \nfaulty. Extend cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test.STE/ICE-R #51.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil pressure is not at \n3050 +/- 50 psi (21029.75 +/- 345 kPa), main control valve faulty.4\nIs hydraulic oil flow rate at \nlarge section of tandem gear \npump at 41.3 to 55.2 gpm (156 to 209 Lpm)? YESNOTest/replace faulty \ntandem gear pump (Para 18-3). 5\nIs hydraulic oil pressure at \nmain control valve at 3050 +/- \n50 psi (21029.75 +/- 345 kPa)? YESNOReplace faulty main \ncontrol valve \n(Para 18-5).", "snippet": "linder hose connections \nOK. Tandem gear pump OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nMain control valve faulty.Hydraulic joystick control valve \nfaulty. Extend cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test.STE/ICE-R #51."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p877_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 877, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1      2-819\nTANDEM GEAR PUMP\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non large section tandem gear pump. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P). (3) Read output pressure on test device. (a) If hydraulic oil pressure is at \n3050+/- 50 psi (21029.75 +/- \n345 kPa), go to Step 5 of this fault. (b) If hydraulic oil pr essure is not at \n3050+/- 50 psi (21029.75 +/- 345 kPa), shut down engine (TM \n10-3930-673-10) and test and replace tandem gear pump (Para \n18-3). MAIN CONTROL VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non main control valve. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P).", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1      2-819\nTANDEM GEAR PUMP\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non large section tandem gear pump. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p877_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 877, "text": "d test and replace tandem gear pump (Para \n18-3). MAIN CONTROL VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non main control valve. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P).\n(3) Read the output pressure on test \ndevice.(a) If hydraulic oil pressure is at 3050 \n+/- 50 psi (21029.75 +/- 345 kPa), \ngo to Step 6 of this fault. (b) If the hydraulic oil pressure is not \nat 3050 +/- 50 psi (21029.75 +/- 345 kPa), shut down engine \n(TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace \nmain control valve (Para 18-5).TR01263\nTR01814", "snippet": "d test and replace tandem gear pump (Para \n18-3). MAIN CONTROL VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non main control valve. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P)."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p878_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 878, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-820      Change 16. BOOM EXTEND CYLINDER SLOW OR DOES NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK.\nExtend cylinder hydraulic hoses \nOK.\nExtend cylinder hose connections \nOK.\nTandem gear pump OK.\nMain control valve OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHydraulic joystick control valve \nfaulty.\nExtend cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test.\nSTE/ICE-R #51.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic joysti ck control valve \ndoes not operate properly, control valve is faulty.6\nDoes hydraulic joystick \ncontrol valve operate \nproperly?\nYESNOReplace faulty \nhydraulic control \nvalve (Para 18-11).\nNotify Direct Support about this \nfault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-820      Change 16. BOOM EXTEND CYLINDER SLOW OR DOES NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil OK.\nExtend cylinder hydraulic hoses \nOK.\nExtend cylinder hose connect"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p879_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 879, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1      2-821\nHYDRAULIC JOYSTICK CONTROL VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Place hydraulic joystick in neutral \nposition and check the pressure at the \nhydraulic joystick control valve “p” port.(a) If hydraulic oil pressure is null go to \nStep (2) below. (b) If not, replace joystick control valve \n(Para 18-11). (2) Place hydraulic joystick in raise \nposition and then in lower position. Check pressure at input lines to \nhydraulic joystick control valve. (a) If hydraulic oil pressure is at 1700 \nto 1800 psi (11721 to 12411 kPa), \nnotify Direct Support of faulty \nextend cylinder. (b) If hydraulic oil pr essure is not at \n1700 to 1800 psi (11721 to 12411 kPa), shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace \nhydraulic joystick control valve.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1      2-821\nHYDRAULIC JOYSTICK CONTROL VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Place hydraulic joystick in neutral \nposition and check the pressure at the \nhydraulic joystick control valve"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p879_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 879, "text": "Support of faulty \nextend cylinder. (b) If hydraulic oil pr essure is not at \n1700 to 1800 psi (11721 to 12411 kPa), shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace \nhydraulic joystick control valve.\nTR01264TR01815WARNING\n• Hydraulic oil in the system can be under pr essures over 3000 psi with engine OFF. Alwa ys relieve pressure in hydraulic lines \nbefore attempting to remove any component in the hydraulic syst em. With engine OFF, starter switch in RUN position, and MLRS \nattachment on the ground, move control levers through all operatin g positions several times to re lieve live pressure. Relieve \npressure in hydraulic oil tank by  loosening filler cap very slowly. Failure to follow these precautions could result in serious \npersonal injury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is  hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries. Allow hydraul ic oil to cool be fore \ndisconnecting any hydraulic lines.", "snippet": "Support of faulty \nextend cylinder. (b) If hydraulic oil pr essure is not at \n1700 to 1800 psi (11721 to 12411 kPa), shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace \nhydraulic joystick control valve."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p880_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 880, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8222\n7. LEFT AND RIGHT REAR STEERING CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine running (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)Personnel Required\nTwo\nReferences\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right front steering \ncylinders extend or retract. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHydraulic oil level low. Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nfaulty. Steering cylinders hose \nconnections faulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Priority valve faulty. Steering control valve faulty.Steering select valve faulty.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8222\n7. LEFT AND RIGHT REAR STEERING CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/IC"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p880_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 880, "text": "ow. Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nfaulty. Steering cylinders hose \nconnections faulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Priority valve faulty. Steering control valve faulty.Steering select valve faulty.\nLeft and/or right rear steering \ncylinders faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual Inspection\nSTE/ICE-R #51\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs hydraulic oil tank filled to \nproper level?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOFill hydr aulic oil \ntank to proper level \n(Para 18-2).", "snippet": "ow. Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nfaulty. Steering cylinders hose \nconnections faulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Priority valve faulty. Steering control valve faulty.Steering select valve faulty."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p881_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 881, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-823HYDRAULIC OIL TANKHYDRAULIC \u001f\nOIL LEVEL GAGEWARNING\n• Hydraulic oil in the system can be under pressures over 3000 ps i with engine OFF. Always relieve pressure in hydraulic lines \nbefore attempting to remove any component in the hydraulic system. With engine OFF, starter switch in RUN position, and MLRS \nattachment on the ground, move control levers through all operati ng positions several times to relieve live pressure. Relieve \npressure in hydraulic oil tank by looseni ng filler cap very slowly. Failure to follow these precautions could result in serious  \npersonal injury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-823HYDRAULIC OIL TANKHYDRAULIC \u001f\nOIL LEVEL GAGEWARNING\n• Hydraulic oil in the system can be under pressures over 3000 ps i with engine OFF. Always relieve pressure in hydraulic l"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p881_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 881, "text": "ollow these precautions could result in serious  \npersonal injury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries.\nAllow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines. CAUTION\nWipe the area clean around all hydraulic conne ctions to be opened during removal and disa ssembly. Cap oil lines and plug holes \nafter removing lines. Contamination of the hydraulic system could result in premature failure. HYDRAULIC OIL LEVEL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Visually inspect hydraulic oil level gage \nto determine if tank is filled to proper level. (2) Hydraulic oil should be visible between \nADD and FULL marks.(a) If hydraulic oil is visible between \nADD and FULL marks, go to Step 2 of this fault. (b) If hydraulic oil is not visible, inspect \ntank for leaks. Then fill tank to FULL mark with hydraulic oil (Para 18-2). TR01265", "snippet": "ollow these precautions could result in serious  \npersonal injury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p882_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 882, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8247. LEFT AND RIGHT REAR STEERING CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right front steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nSteering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nfaulty. Steering cylinders hose \nconnections faulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Priority valve faulty.Steering control valve faulty. Steering select valve faulty. Left and/or right rear steering \ncylinders faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual Inspection. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hoses are cracked, kinked, or \npinched, hoses are faulty and will need replaced. KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right frontsteering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK. Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nOK.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8247. LEFT AND RIGHT REAR STEERING CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right front steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK. POSSI"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p882_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 882, "text": "ked, kinked, or \npinched, hoses are faulty and will need replaced. KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right frontsteering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK. Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nOK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nSteering cylinders hose \nconnections faulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Priority valve faulty. Steering control valve faulty.Steering select valve faulty. Left and/or right rear steering \ncylinders faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hose connections are loose or \nshow signs of leaks, hose connections will need to be \ntightened or replaced.2\nAre hydraulic hoses leading \nto left and right rear steering cylinders free of cracked, \nkinked, or pinched sections? YESNOReplace faulty \nhoses (Para 14-6). 3\nAre hydraulic hose \nconnections leading to left \nand right rear steering cylinders tight and free of \nleaks? YESNOTIghten or replace \nfaulty fittings \n(Para 14-6).", "snippet": "ked, kinked, or \npinched, hoses are faulty and will need replaced. KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right frontsteering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK. Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nOK."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p883_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 883, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1 2-825HYDRAULIC HOSES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hoses leading to \nrear steering cylinders for cracks, kinks, or \npinches. (a) If there are no cracks, kinks, or \npinches in hydraulic hoses, go to Step 3 of this fault. (b) If there are cracks, kinks, or \npinches in one or more of hydraulic hoses, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace \nhose(s) (Para 14-6). NOTE\nHydraulic hoses must be mounted (indexed) in such a way to allow movement of hoses \nduring steering without rubbing, crimping, or exceeding minimum bend radius. HYDRAULIC HOSE CONNECTIONS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hose connections \nleading to left and right rear steering cylinders for leaks.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1 2-825HYDRAULIC HOSES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hoses leading to \nrear steering cylinders for cracks, kinks, or \npinches. (a) If there are no cracks, ki"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p883_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 883, "text": "ing, crimping, or exceeding minimum bend radius. HYDRAULIC HOSE CONNECTIONS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hose connections \nleading to left and right rear steering cylinders for leaks.\n(a) If there are no leaks at hydraulic \nhose connections, go to Step 4 of this fault. (b) If there are leaks at one or more of \nconnections, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and tighten or replace connection(s) \n(Para 14-6). TR01266", "snippet": "ing, crimping, or exceeding minimum bend radius. HYDRAULIC HOSE CONNECTIONS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hose connections \nleading to left and right rear steering cylinders for leaks."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p884_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 884, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-826      Change 17. LEFT AND RIGHT REAR STEERING CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right front steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK. Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nOK. Steering cylinders hose \nconnections OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTandem gear pump faulty.Priority valve faulty.Steering control valve faulty. Steering select valve faulty. Left and/or right rear steering \ncylinders faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure Test. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil flow rate is not at \n41.3 to 55.2 gpm (156 to 55.2 Lpm), tandem gear pump is \nfaulty. KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right front steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK.Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nOK.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-826      Change 17. LEFT AND RIGHT REAR STEERING CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right front steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil l"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p884_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 884, "text": " \n41.3 to 55.2 gpm (156 to 55.2 Lpm), tandem gear pump is \nfaulty. KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right front steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK.Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nOK.\nSteering cylinders hose \nconnections OK. Tandem gear pump OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nPriority valve faulty.Steering control valve faulty.Steering select valve faulty. Left and/or right rear steering \ncylinders faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure Test. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil pressure is not at \n2500 +/- 50 psi (17237.5 +/- 345 kPa), priority valve is faulty.4\nIs hydraulic oil flow rate at \nlarge section of tandem gear pump at 41.3 to 55.2 gpm (156 \nto 209 Lpm)? YESNOTest/replace faulty \ntandem gear pump \n(Para 18-3). 5\nIs hydraulic oil pressure at \npriority valve at 2500 +/- 50 psi \n(17237.5 +/- 345 kPa)? YESNOTest/replace faulty \npriority valve (Para 18-7).", "snippet": " \n41.3 to 55.2 gpm (156 to 55.2 Lpm), tandem gear pump is \nfaulty. KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right front steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK.Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nOK."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p885_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 885, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1 2-827\nPRIORITY VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \nat priority valve.\n(2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P).\n(3) Read output pressure on test device.\n(a) If hydraulic oil pressure is at 2500 \n+/- 50 psi (17237.5 +/- 345 kPa), \ngo to Step 6 of this fault.\n(b) If hydraulic oil pr essure is not at \n2500 +/- 50 psi (17237.5 +/- 345 kPa), shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace \npriority valve (Para 18-7).\nTR01267", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1 2-827\nPRIORITY VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \nat priority valve.\n(2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P).\n(3) Read output pressu"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p886_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 886, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-828      Change 17. LEFT AND RIGHT REAR STEERING CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right front steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK. Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nOK. Steering cylinders hose \nconnections OK. Tandem gear pump OK.Priority valve OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nSteering control valve faulty. Steering select valve faulty. Left and/or right rear steering \ncylinders faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure Test. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil pressure is not at \n2500 +/- 50 psi (17237.5 +/- 345 kPa), steering control valve \nis faulty. KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right front steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK.Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nOK.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-828      Change 17. LEFT AND RIGHT REAR STEERING CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right front steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil l"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p886_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 886, "text": "- 50 psi (17237.5 +/- 345 kPa), steering control valve \nis faulty. KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right front steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK.Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nOK.\nSteering cylinders hose \nconnections OK. Tandem gear pump OK. Priority valve OK. Steering control valve OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nSteering select valve faulty. Left and/or right rear steering \ncylinders faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nContinuity Test. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf continuity is not indicated at \nsolenoids, steering select valve is faulty.6\nIs hydraulic oil pressure at \nsteering control valve at 2500 +/- 50 psi (17237.5 +/-\n345 kPa)? YESNOReplace faulty \nsteering control \nvalve (Para 14-9). 7\nIs continuity indicated at \nsolenoids on steering select \nvalve? YESNOReplace faulty \nsteering select \nvalve (Para 14-10).", "snippet": "- 50 psi (17237.5 +/- 345 kPa), steering control valve \nis faulty. KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right front steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK.Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nOK."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p887_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 887, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1 2-829\nSTEERING CONTROL VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Use test tee and 0 to 5000 psi (0 to \n34475 kPa) pressure gage to test \nhydraulic pressure at steering control valve output line. (2) Read output pressure on test device. (a) If hydraulic oil pressure is at 2500 \n+/- 50 psi (17237.5 +/- 345 kPa), \ngo to Step 7 of this fault. (b) If hydraulic oil pr essure is not at \n2500 +/- 50 psi (17237.5 +/- 345 kPa), shut down engine \n(TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace steering control valve (Para 14-9). STEERING SELECT VALVE SOLENOID\nCONTINUITY TEST\n(1) Perform continuity test on solenoids at \nsteering select valve. (2) Read output of ohmmeter. (a) If ohmmeter indicates continuity, \ngo to Step 8 of this fault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1 2-829\nSTEERING CONTROL VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Use test tee and 0 to 5000 psi (0 to \n34475 kPa) pressure gage to test \nhydraulic pressure at steering control valve output "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p887_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 887, "text": "ELECT VALVE SOLENOID\nCONTINUITY TEST\n(1) Perform continuity test on solenoids at \nsteering select valve. (2) Read output of ohmmeter. (a) If ohmmeter indicates continuity, \ngo to Step 8 of this fault.\n(b) If continuity is not indicated, shut \ndown engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace steering select valve (Para 14-10).TR01268", "snippet": "ELECT VALVE SOLENOID\nCONTINUITY TEST\n(1) Perform continuity test on solenoids at \nsteering select valve. (2) Read output of ohmmeter. (a) If ohmmeter indicates continuity, \ngo to Step 8 of this fault."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p888_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 888, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8307. LEFT AND RIGHT REAR STEERING CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nLeft and right front steering \ncylinders extend or retract.\nHydraulic oil level OK.\nSteering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nOK.\nSteering cylinders hose \nconnections OK.\nTandem gear pump OK.Priority valve OK.Steering control valve OK.\nSteering select valve OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nLeft and/or right rear steering \ncylinders faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual Inspection.STE/ICE-R #51.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf steering cylinders and their \nhousings show signs of damage, cylinders and/or their housings \nwill not function.8\nAre rear steering cylinders \nand their housings free from \nsigns of damage?\nYESNOReplace damaged \ncylinder and \nhousing (Para 14-7).\nNotify Direct Support about this \nfault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8307. LEFT AND RIGHT REAR STEERING CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nLeft and right front steering \ncylinders extend or retract.\nHydraulic oil level OK.\nSteer"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p889_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 889, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1 2-831REAR STEERING CYLINDER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect rear steering cylinders and \ntheir housings for signs of damage.\n(a) If steering cylinders and/or their \nhousings are not damaged, notify Direct Support about this fault.\n(b) If rear steering cylinders and/or \ntheir housings are damaged, shut down engine \n(TM 10-3930-6573-10) and replace \ncylinders and their housings (Para 14-7).\nTR01269", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1 2-831REAR STEERING CYLINDER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect rear steering cylinders and \ntheir housings for signs of damage.\n(a) If steering cylinders and/or their \nhou"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p890_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 890, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8322\n8. LEFT AND RIGHT FRONT STEERING CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine running (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)Personnel Required\nTwo\nReferences\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right rear steering \ncylinders  extend or retract. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHydraulic oil level low. Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nfaulty. Steering cylinders hose \nconnections faulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Priority valve faulty. Steering control valve faulty.Steering select valve faulty.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8322\n8. LEFT AND RIGHT FRONT STEERING CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/I"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p890_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 890, "text": "ow. Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nfaulty. Steering cylinders hose \nconnections faulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Priority valve faulty. Steering control valve faulty.Steering select valve faulty.\nLeft and/or right front steering \ncylinders faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs hydraulic oil tank filled to \nproper level?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOFill hydr aulic oil \ntank to proper level \n(Para 18-2).", "snippet": "ow. Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nfaulty. Steering cylinders hose \nconnections faulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Priority valve faulty. Steering control valve faulty.Steering select valve faulty."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p891_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 891, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-833HYDRAULIC OIL TANKHYDRAULIC \u001f\nOIL LEVEL GAGEWARNING\n• Hydraulic oil in the system can be under pressures over 3000 ps i with engine OFF. Always relieve pressure in hydraulic lines \nbefore attempting to remove any component in the hydraulic system. With engine OFF, starter switch in RUN position, and MLRS \nattachment on the ground, move control levers through all operati ng positions several times to relieve live pressure. Relieve \npressure in hydraulic oil tank by looseni ng filler cap very slowly. Failure to follo w these precautions could result in serious  personal \ninjury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-833HYDRAULIC OIL TANKHYDRAULIC \u001f\nOIL LEVEL GAGEWARNING\n• Hydraulic oil in the system can be under pressures over 3000 ps i with engine OFF. Always relieve pressure in hydraulic l"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p891_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 891, "text": "llo w these precautions could result in serious  personal \ninjury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries.\nAllow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines. CAUTION\nWipe the area clean around all hydraulic conne ctions to be opened during removal and disa ssembly. Cap oil lines and plug holes \nafter removing lines. Contamination of the hydraulic system could result in premature failure. HYDRAULIC OIL LEVEL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Visually inspect hydraulic oil level gage \nto determine if tank is filled to proper level. (2) Hydraulic oil should be visible between \nADD and FULL marks.(a) If hydraulic oil is visible between \nADD and FULL marks, go to \nStep 2 of this fault. (b) If hydraulic oil is not visible, inspect \ntank for leaks. Then fill tank to FULL mark with hydraulic oil \n(Para 18-2). TR01270", "snippet": "llo w these precautions could result in serious  personal \ninjury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p892_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 892, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8348. LEFT AND RIGHT FRONT STEERING CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right rear steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nSteering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nfaulty. Steering cylinders hose \nconnections faulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Priority valve faulty.Steering control valve faulty. Steering select valve faulty. Left and/or right front steering \ncylinders faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hoses are cracked, kinked, or \npinched, cylinders will not function. KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right rear steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK. Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nOK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nSteering cylinders hose \nconnections faulty.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8348. LEFT AND RIGHT FRONT STEERING CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right rear steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK. POSSI"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p892_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 892, "text": "on. KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right rear steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK. Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nOK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nSteering cylinders hose \nconnections faulty.\nTandem gear pump faulty. Priority valve faulty. Steering control valve faulty.Steering select valve faulty. Left and/or right front steering \ncylinders faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hose connections are loose or \nshow signs of leaks, cylinders will not function.2\nAre hydraulic hoses leading \nto left and right front steering cylinders free of cracked, \nkinked, or pinched sections? YESNOReplace faulty \nhoses (Para 14-6). 3\nAre hydraulic hose \nconnections leading to left \nand right front steering cylinders tight and free of \nleaks? YESNOTighten or replace \nfaulty fittings \n(Para 14-6).", "snippet": "on. KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right rear steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK. Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nOK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nSteering cylinders hose \nconnections faulty."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p893_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 893, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1      2-835\nHYDRAULIC HOSES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hoses leading to \nfront steering cylinders for cracks, kinks, or \npinches. (a) If there are no cracks, kinks, or \npinches in hydraulic hoses, go to Step 3 of this fault. (b) If there are cracks, kinks, or \npinches in one or more of hydraulic hoses, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace \nhose(s) (Para 14-6). NOTE\nHydraulic hoses must be mounted (indexed) in such a way to allow movement of hoses \nduring steering without rubbing, crimping, or exceeding minimum bend radius. HYDRAULIC HOSE CONNECTIONS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hose connections \nleading to left and right front steering cylinders for leaks.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1      2-835\nHYDRAULIC HOSES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hoses leading to \nfront steering cylinders for cracks, kinks, or \npinches. (a) If there are no cra"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p893_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 893, "text": "ng, crimping, or exceeding minimum bend radius. HYDRAULIC HOSE CONNECTIONS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hose connections \nleading to left and right front steering cylinders for leaks.\n(a) If there are no leaks at hydraulic \nhose connections, go to Step 4 of this fault. (b) If there are leaks at one or more of \nconnections, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and tighten \nor replace connection(s) \n(Para 14-6). TR01271", "snippet": "ng, crimping, or exceeding minimum bend radius. HYDRAULIC HOSE CONNECTIONS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hose connections \nleading to left and right front steering cylinders for leaks."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p894_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 894, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8368. LEFT AND RIGHT FRONT STEERING CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right rear steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK. Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nOK. Steering cylinders hose \nconnections OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTandem gear pump faulty.Priority valve faulty.Steering control valve faulty. Steering select valve faulty. Left and/or right front steering \ncylinders faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil pressure is not at \n2600 to 2700 psi (17940 to 18630 kPa), tandem gear pump \nis faulty. KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right rear steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK.Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nOK. Steering cylinders hose \nconnections OK.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8368. LEFT AND RIGHT FRONT STEERING CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right rear steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK. Steer"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p894_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 894, "text": "ndem gear pump \nis faulty. KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right rear steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK.Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nOK. Steering cylinders hose \nconnections OK.\nTandem gear pump OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nPriority valve faulty.Steering control valve faulty.Steering select valve faulty. Left and/or right front steering \ncylinders faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil pressure is not at \n2600 to 2700 psi (17940 to 18630 kPa), priority valve is \nfaulty.4\nIs hydraulic oil pressure at \nlarge section of tandem gear pump at 2600 to 2700 psi \n(17940 to 18630 kPa)? YESNOTest/replace faulty \ntandem gear pump \n(Para 18-3). 5\nIs hydraulic oil pressure at \npriority valve at 2600 to \n2700 psi (17940 to 18630 kPa)? YESNOTest/replace faulty \npriority valve (Para 18-7).", "snippet": "ndem gear pump \nis faulty. KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right rear steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK.Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nOK. Steering cylinders hose \nconnections OK."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p895_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 895, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-837PRIORITY\u001f\nVALVETANDEM\u001f\nGEAR\u001f\nPUMP\nLARGE\u001f\nSECTION\nSMALL\u001f\nSECTIONTANDEM GEAR PUMP\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non large section of tandem gear pump. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P). (3) Read output pressure on test device. (a) If hydraulic oil pressure is at 2600 \nto 2700 psi (17940 to 18630 kPa), \ngo to Step 5 of this fault. (b) If hydraulic oil pr essure is not at \n2600 to 2700 psi (17940 to 18630 kPa), shut down engine \n(TM 10-3930-673-10) and test and replace tandem gear pump \n(Para 18-3). PRIORITY VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \nat priority valve. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P). (3) Read output pressure on test device.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-837PRIORITY\u001f\nVALVETANDEM\u001f\nGEAR\u001f\nPUMP\nLARGE\u001f\nSECTION\nSMALL\u001f\nSECTIONTANDEM GEAR PUMP\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non large section of tandem gear pump. "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p895_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 895, "text": "mp \n(Para 18-3). PRIORITY VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \nat priority valve. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P). (3) Read output pressure on test device.\n(a) If hydraulic oil pressure is at 2600 \nto 2700 psi (17940 to 18630 kPa), \ngo to Step 6 of this fault. (b) If hydraulic oil pr essure is not at \n2600 to 2700 psi (17940 to 18630 kPa), shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace \npriority valve (Para 18-7). TR01272", "snippet": "mp \n(Para 18-3). PRIORITY VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \nat priority valve. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P). (3) Read output pressure on test device."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p896_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 896, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8388. LEFT AND RIGHT FRONT STEERING CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right rear steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK. Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nOK. Steering cylinders hose \nconnections OK. Tandem gear pump OK.Priority valve OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nSteering control valve faulty. Steering select valve faulty. Left and/or right front steering \ncylinders faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil pressure is not at \n2600 to 2700 psi (17940 to 18630 kPa), steering control \nvalve is faulty. KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right rear steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK.Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nOK. Steering cylinders hose \nconnections OK.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8388. LEFT AND RIGHT FRONT STEERING CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right rear steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK. Steer"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p896_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 896, "text": " control \nvalve is faulty. KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right rear steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK.Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nOK. Steering cylinders hose \nconnections OK.\nTandem gear pump OK. Priority valve OK. Steering control valve OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nSteering select valve faulty. Left and/or right front steering \ncylinders faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nContinuity test. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf continuity is not indicated at \nsolenoids, steering select valve is faulty.6\nIs hydraulic oil pressure at \nsteering control valve at 2600 to 2700 psi (17940 to \n18630 kPa)? YESNOReplace faulty \nsteering control \nvalve (Para 14-9). 7\nIs continuity indicated at \nsolenoids on steering select \nvalve? YESNOReplace faulty \nsteering select \nvalve (Para 14-10).", "snippet": " control \nvalve is faulty. KNOWN INFO\nLeft and right rear steering \ncylinders extend or retract. Hydraulic oil level OK.Steering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nOK. Steering cylinders hose \nconnections OK."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p897_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 897, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-839\nSTEERING CONTROL VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Use test tee and 0 to 5000 psi (0 to \n34475 kPa) pressure gage to test \nhydraulic pressure at steering control valve output line. (2) Read output pressure on test device. (a) If hydraulic oil pressure is at 2600 \nto 2700 psi (17940 to 18630 kPa), \ngo to Step 7 of this fault. (b) If hydraulic oil pr essure is not at \n2500 psi (17237.5 kPa), shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and \nreplace steering control valve (Para 14-9). STEERING SELECT VALVE SOLENOID\nCONTINUITY TEST\n(1) Perform continuity test on solenoids at \nsteering select valve. (2) Read output of ohmmeter. (a) If ohmmeter indicates continuity, \ngo to Step 8 of this fault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-839\nSTEERING CONTROL VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Use test tee and 0 to 5000 psi (0 to \n34475 kPa) pressure gage to test \nhydraulic pressure at steering control valve output line. (2)"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p897_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 897, "text": "ELECT VALVE SOLENOID\nCONTINUITY TEST\n(1) Perform continuity test on solenoids at \nsteering select valve. (2) Read output of ohmmeter. (a) If ohmmeter indicates continuity, \ngo to Step 8 of this fault.\n(b) If continuity is not indicated, shut \ndown engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace steering select valve (Para 14-10).TR01273", "snippet": "ELECT VALVE SOLENOID\nCONTINUITY TEST\n(1) Perform continuity test on solenoids at \nsteering select valve. (2) Read output of ohmmeter. (a) If ohmmeter indicates continuity, \ngo to Step 8 of this fault."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p898_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 898, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8408. LEFT AND RIGHT FRONT STEERING CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nLeft and right rear steering \ncylinders extend or retract.\nHydraulic oil level OK.\nSteering cylinders hydraulic hoses \nOK.\nSteering cylinders hose \nconnections OK.\nTandem gear pump OK.Priority valve OK.Steering control valve OK.\nSteering select valve OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nLeft and/or right front steering \ncylinders faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf steering cylinders and their \nhousings show signs of damage, cylinders will not function.8\nAre front steering cylinders \nand their housings free from \nsigns of damage?\nYESNOReplace damaged \ncylinder and \nhousing (Para 14-7).\nNotify Direct Support about this \nfault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8408. LEFT AND RIGHT FRONT STEERING CYLINDERS DO NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nLeft and right rear steering \ncylinders extend or retract.\nHydraulic oil level OK.\nSteer"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p899_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 899, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1 2-841\nFRONT STEERING CYLINDER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect front steering cylinders and \ntheir housings for signs of damage.\n(a) If steering cylinders and/or their \nhousings are not damaged, notify Direct Support about this fault.\n(b) If steering cylinders and/or their \nhousings are damaged, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and \nreplace cylinders and their \nhousings (Para 14-7).\nTR01274", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1 2-841\nFRONT STEERING CYLINDER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect front steering cylinders and \ntheir housings for signs of damage.\n(a) If steering cylinders and/or their \n"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p900_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 900, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8422\n9. FRAME TILT CYLINDER DOES NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine running (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)Personnel Required\nTwo\nReferences\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHydraulic oil level low. Frame tilt cylinde r hydraulic hoses \nfaulty. Frame tilt cylinder hose \nconnections/tube faulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Frame tilt/brakes relief valve faulty. Frame tilt valve faulty. Frame tilt cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8422\n9. FRAME TILT CYLINDER DOES NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, App"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p900_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 900, "text": "ylinder hose \nconnections/tube faulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Frame tilt/brakes relief valve faulty. Frame tilt valve faulty. Frame tilt cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs hydraulic oil tank filled to \nproper level?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOFill hydraulic oil \ntank to proper level \n(Para 18-2).", "snippet": "ylinder hose \nconnections/tube faulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Frame tilt/brakes relief valve faulty. Frame tilt valve faulty. Frame tilt cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p901_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 901, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-843HYDRAULIC OIL TANKHYDRAULIC \u001f\nOIL LEVEL GAGEWARNING\n• Hydraulic oil in system can be under pressures over 3000 psi with e ngine OFF. Always relieve pressure in hydraulic lines befor e \nattempting to remove any component in hydraulic system. Wi th engine OFF, starter switch in RUN position, and MLRS \nattachment on ground, move control levers through all operating pos itions several times to relieve live pressure. Relieve pressure \nin hydraulic oil tank by loosening filler cap very slowly. Failure to follow these precautions could result in serious personal  injury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries. Allow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-843HYDRAULIC OIL TANKHYDRAULIC \u001f\nOIL LEVEL GAGEWARNING\n• Hydraulic oil in system can be under pressures over 3000 psi with e ngine OFF. Always relieve pressure in hydraulic lines"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p901_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 901, "text": " operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries. Allow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines.\nCAUTION\nWipe area clean around all hydrau lic connections to be opened during removal and disa ssembly. Cap oil lines and plug holes afte r \nremoving lines. Contamination of hydraulic s ystem could result in premature failure. HYDRAULIC OIL LEVEL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Visually inspect hydraulic oil level gage \nto determine if tank is filled to proper \nlevel. (2) Hydraulic oil should be visible between \nADD and FULL marks.(a) If hydraulic oil is visible between \nADD and FULL marks, go to \nStep 2 of this fault. (b) If hydraulic oil is not visible, inspect \ntank for leaks. Then fill tank to FULL mark with hydraulic oil \n(Para 18-2). TR01275", "snippet": " operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries. Allow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p902_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 902, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8449. FRAME TILT CYLINDER DOES NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFrame tilt cylinde r hydraulic hoses \nfaulty. Frame tilt cylinder hose \nconnections/tube faulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Frame tilt/brakes relief valve faulty. Frame tilt valve faulty. Frame tilt cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic hoses are cracked, \nkinked, or pinc hed, hydraulic \nsystem will lose pressure. KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK. Frame tilt cylinde r hydraulic hoses \nOK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFrame tilt cylinder hose \nconnections/tube faulty. Tandem gear pump faulty.Frame tilt/brakes relief valve faulty.Frame tilt valve faulty. Frame tilt cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8449. FRAME TILT CYLINDER DOES NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFrame tilt cylinde r hydraulic hoses \nfaulty. Frame tilt cylinde"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p902_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 902, "text": "EMS\nFrame tilt cylinder hose \nconnections/tube faulty. Tandem gear pump faulty.Frame tilt/brakes relief valve faulty.Frame tilt valve faulty. Frame tilt cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nSTE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic hose connections and \ntube are damaged or leaking, hydraulic system will lose \npressure.2\nAre hydraulic hoses leading \nto the frame tilt cylinder free of cracked, kinked, or pinched \nsections? YESNOReplace faulty \nhoses \n(Para 18-25). 3\nAre hydraulic hose \nconnections leading to frame \ntilt cylinder tight and free of leaks? Is hydraulic tube \nundamaged and intact? YESNOTighten or replace \nfaulty fittings (Para \n18-25). Replace \ndamaged tube (Para 18-26).", "snippet": "EMS\nFrame tilt cylinder hose \nconnections/tube faulty. Tandem gear pump faulty.Frame tilt/brakes relief valve faulty.Frame tilt valve faulty. Frame tilt cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p903_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 903, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-845\nHYDRAULIC HOSES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hoses leading to \nframe tilt cylinder for cracks, kinks, or \npinches. (a) If there are no cracks, kinks, or \npinches in hydraulic hoses, go to Step 3 of this fault. (b) If there are cracks, kinks, or \npinches in one or more of hydraulic hoses, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace \nhose(s) (Para 18-25). HYDRAULIC HOSE CONNECTIONS/TUBE\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hose connections \nleading to frame tilt cylinder for leaks. Visually inspect hydr aulic tube on cylinder \nhousing. (a) If there are no leaks at hydraulic \nhose connections, and tube is not \ndamaged, go to Step 4 of this fault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-845\nHYDRAULIC HOSES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hoses leading to \nframe tilt cylinder for cracks, kinks, or \npinches. (a) If there are no cracks, kinks, or \npin"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p903_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 903, "text": "to frame tilt cylinder for leaks. Visually inspect hydr aulic tube on cylinder \nhousing. (a) If there are no leaks at hydraulic \nhose connections, and tube is not \ndamaged, go to Step 4 of this fault.\n(b) If there are leaks at one or more \nconnections, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and tighten \nor replace connection(s) (Para 18-25). If tube is damaged, \nreplace tube (Para 18-26). TR01276", "snippet": "to frame tilt cylinder for leaks. Visually inspect hydr aulic tube on cylinder \nhousing. (a) If there are no leaks at hydraulic \nhose connections, and tube is not \ndamaged, go to Step 4 of this fault."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p904_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 904, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-846      Change 19. FRAME TILT CYLINDER DOES NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK. Frame tilt cylinde r hydraulic hoses \nOK. Frame tilt cylinder hose \nconnections/tube OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTandem gear pump faulty. Frame tilt/brakes relief valve faulty. Frame tilt valve faulty. Frame tilt cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil pressure is not at \n2600 to 2700 psi (17940 to 18630 kPa), tandem gear pump \nis faulty. KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK.Frame tilt cylinde r hydraulic hoses \nOK. Frame tilt cylinder hose \nconnections/tube OK. Tandem gear pump OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFrame tilt/brakes relief valve faulty.Frame tilt valve faulty.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-846      Change 19. FRAME TILT CYLINDER DOES NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK. Frame tilt cylinde r hydraulic hoses \nOK. Frame tilt cylinder hose \n"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p904_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 904, "text": " level OK.Frame tilt cylinde r hydraulic hoses \nOK. Frame tilt cylinder hose \nconnections/tube OK. Tandem gear pump OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFrame tilt/brakes relief valve faulty.Frame tilt valve faulty.\nFrame tilt cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil pressure is not at \n2600 to 2700 psi (17940 to 18630 kPa), relief valve is faulty.4\nIs hydraulic oil pressure at \nsmall section of tandem gear pump at 2600 to 2700 psi \n(17940 to 18630 kPa)? YESNOTest/replace faulty \ntandem gear pump \n(Para 18-3). 5\nIs hydraulic oil pressure at \nthe frame tilt/brakes relief \nvalve at 2600 to 2700 psi (17940 to 18630 kPa)? YESNOAdjust pressure or \nreplace faulty relief \nvalve (Para 18-8).", "snippet": " level OK.Frame tilt cylinde r hydraulic hoses \nOK. Frame tilt cylinder hose \nconnections/tube OK. Tandem gear pump OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFrame tilt/brakes relief valve faulty.Frame tilt valve faulty."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p905_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 905, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1      2-847\nTANDEM GEAR PUMP\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non small section of tandem gear pump. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P). (3) Read output pressure on test device. (a) If hydraulic oil pressure is at 2600 \nto 2700 psi (17927 to 18616 kPa), \ngo to Step 5 of this fault. (b) If hydraulic oil pr essure is not at \n2600 to 2700 psi (17940 to 18630 kPa), shut down engine \n(TM 10-3930-673-10) and test and replace tandem gear pump\n(Para 18-3). FRAME TILT/BRAKES RELIEF VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Install test tee on inlet line to frame tilt/\nbrakes relief valve. (2) Connect 0 to 5000 psi (0 to 34475 kPa) \npressure gage to test tee. (3) Read output pressure on test device.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1      2-847\nTANDEM GEAR PUMP\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non small section of tandem gear pump. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p905_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 905, "text": "LVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Install test tee on inlet line to frame tilt/\nbrakes relief valve. (2) Connect 0 to 5000 psi (0 to 34475 kPa) \npressure gage to test tee. (3) Read output pressure on test device.\n(a) If hydraulic oil pressure is at 2600 \nto 2700 psi (17940 to 18630 kPa), \ngo to Step 6 of this fault. (b) If hydraulic oil pr essure is not at \n2600 to 2700 psi (17940 to 18630 kPa), go to Step (4) below. (4) If valve is not damaged, adjust relief \npressure to specified psi and remove pressure gage and test tee.(a) If hydraulic oil pressure is adjusted \nto specified psi, go to Step 6 of this fault. (b) If hydraulic oil pressure cannot be \nadjusted, replace relief valve (Para 18-8). TR01277", "snippet": "LVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Install test tee on inlet line to frame tilt/\nbrakes relief valve. (2) Connect 0 to 5000 psi (0 to 34475 kPa) \npressure gage to test tee. (3) Read output pressure on test device."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p906_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 906, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-848      Change 19. FRAME TILT CYLINDER DOES NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK. Frame tilt cylinde r hydraulic hoses \nOK. Frame tilt cylinder hose \nconnections/tube OK. Tandem gear pump OK. Frame tilt/brakes relief valve OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFrame tilt valve faulty.Frame tilt cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil pressure is not at \n2600 to 2700 psi (17940 to 18630 kPa), frame tilt valve is \nfaulty. KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK.Frame tilt cylinde r hydraulic hoses \nOK. Frame tilt cylinder hose \nconnections/tube OK. Tandem gear pump OK.Frame tilt/brakes relief valve OK.Frame tilt valve OK.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-848      Change 19. FRAME TILT CYLINDER DOES NOT EXTEND OR RETRACT (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK. Frame tilt cylinde r hydraulic hoses \nOK. Frame tilt cylinder hose \n"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p906_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 906, "text": "y. KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK.Frame tilt cylinde r hydraulic hoses \nOK. Frame tilt cylinder hose \nconnections/tube OK. Tandem gear pump OK.Frame tilt/brakes relief valve OK.Frame tilt valve OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFrame tilt cylinder faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf frame tilt cylinder and its housing \nis damaged, cylinder will not function.6\nIs hydraulic oil at 2600 to 2700 \npsi (17940 to 18630 kPa)? YESNOReplace faulty \nframe tilt valve(Para 18-10). 7\nIs frame tilt cylinder and its \nhousing free from signs of \ndamage? YESNOReplace damaged \ncylinder and housing \n(Para 18-13). Notify Direct Support about this fault.", "snippet": "y. KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK.Frame tilt cylinde r hydraulic hoses \nOK. Frame tilt cylinder hose \nconnections/tube OK. Tandem gear pump OK.Frame tilt/brakes relief valve OK.Frame tilt valve OK."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p907_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 907, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1      2-849\nFRAME TILT VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P). (3) Read output pressure on test device. (a) If hydraulic oil pressure is at 2600 \nto 2700 psi (17940 to 18630 kPa), \ngo to Step 7 of this fault. (b) If hydraulic oil pr essure is not at \n2600 to 2700 psi (17940 to 18630 kPa), shut down engine (TM 10-\n3930-673-10) and replace frame tilt \nvalve. FRAME TILT CYLINDER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect frame tilt cylinder and its housing for signs of damage. (a) If frame tilt cylinder and/or its \nhousing are not damaged, notify Direct Support about this fault.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1      2-849\nFRAME TILT VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P). (3) Read output pressure on test d"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p907_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 907, "text": "NDER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect frame tilt cylinder and its housing for signs of damage. (a) If frame tilt cylinder and/or its \nhousing are not damaged, notify Direct Support about this fault.\n(b) If frame tilt cylinder and/or its \nhousing are damaged, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and \nreplace cylinder and/or its housing \n(Para 18-13). TR01278", "snippet": "NDER\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect frame tilt cylinder and its housing for signs of damage. (a) If frame tilt cylinder and/or its \nhousing are not damaged, notify Direct Support about this fault."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p908_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 908, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8502\n10. BRAKE ACTUATORS (LEFT AND/OR RIGHT REAR AND LEFT AND/OR RIGHT FRONT) DO NOT \nOPERATE CORRECTLY\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nSTE/ICE-R (Item 17, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine running (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)Personnel Required\nTwo\nReferences\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHydraulic oil level low. Hydraulic hoses faulty. Hose connections/fittings faulty.Tandem gear pump faulty. Frame tilt/brakes relief valve faulty. Brake control valve faulty.Brake hydraulic accumulator faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8502\n10. BRAKE ACTUATORS (LEFT AND/OR RIGHT REAR AND LEFT AND/OR RIGHT FRONT) DO NOT \nOPERATE CORRECTLY\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automot"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p908_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 908, "text": " connections/fittings faulty.Tandem gear pump faulty. Frame tilt/brakes relief valve faulty. Brake control valve faulty.Brake hydraulic accumulator faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs hydraulic oil tank filled to \nproper level?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOFill hydr aulic oil \ntank to proper level \n(Para 18-2).", "snippet": " connections/fittings faulty.Tandem gear pump faulty. Frame tilt/brakes relief valve faulty. Brake control valve faulty.Brake hydraulic accumulator faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p909_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 909, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-851HYDRAULIC OIL TANKHYDRAULIC \u001f\nOIL LEVEL GAGEWARNING\n• Hydraulic oil in system can be under pressures over 3000 psi with e ngine OFF. Always relieve pressure in hydraulic lines befor e \nattempting to remove any component in hydraulic system. Wi th engine OFF, starter switch in RUN position, and MLRS \nattachment on ground, move control levers through all operating pos itions several times to relieve live pressure. Relieve pressure \nin hydraulic oil tank by loosening filler cap very slowly. Failure to follow these precautions could result in serious personal  injury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries. Allow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-851HYDRAULIC OIL TANKHYDRAULIC \u001f\nOIL LEVEL GAGEWARNING\n• Hydraulic oil in system can be under pressures over 3000 psi with e ngine OFF. Always relieve pressure in hydraulic lines"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p909_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 909, "text": " operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries. Allow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines.\nCAUTION\nWipe area clean around all hydrau lic connections to be opened during removal and disa ssembly. Cap oil lines and plug holes afte r \nremoving lines. Contamination of hydraulic s ystem could result in premature failure. HYDRAULIC OIL LEVEL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Visually inspect hydraulic oil level gage \nto determine if tank is filled to proper \nlevel. (2) Hydraulic oil should be visible between \nADD and FULL marks.(a) If hydraulic oil is visible between \nADD and FULL marks, go to \nStep 2. (b) If hydraulic oil is not visible, inspect \ntank for leaks. Then fill tank to FULL mark with hydraulic oil \n(Para 18-2). TR01279", "snippet": " operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries. Allow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p910_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 910, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-852     Change 110. BRAKE ACTUATORS (LEFT AND/OR RIGHT REAR  AND LEFT AND/OR RIGHT FRONT) DO NOT \nOPERATE CORRECTLY (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHydraulic hoses faulty. Hose connections/fittings faulty. Tandem gear pump faulty. Frame tilt/brakes relief valve faulty.Brake control valve faulty. Brake hydraulic accumulator faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic hoses are cracked, \nkinked, or pinched, actuators will not function. KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK.Hydraulic hoses OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHose connections/fittings faulty.Tandem gear pump faulty.Frame tilt/brakes relief valve faulty. Brake control valve faulty.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-852     Change 110. BRAKE ACTUATORS (LEFT AND/OR RIGHT REAR  AND LEFT AND/OR RIGHT FRONT) DO NOT \nOPERATE CORRECTLY (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nH"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p910_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 910, "text": "ion. KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK.Hydraulic hoses OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHose connections/fittings faulty.Tandem gear pump faulty.Frame tilt/brakes relief valve faulty. Brake control valve faulty.\nBrake hydraulic accumulator faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic hose connections/\nfittings are damaged or leaking, actuators will not function.2\nAre hydraulic hoses leading \nto brake actuators free of cracked, kinked, or pinches \nsections? YESNOReplace faulty \nhoses (Para 12-9). 3\nAre hydraulic hose \nconnections/fittings leading \nto brake actuators tight and free of leaks? YESNOTighten or replace \nfaulty connections/\nfittings (Para 12-9).", "snippet": "ion. KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK.Hydraulic hoses OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHose connections/fittings faulty.Tandem gear pump faulty.Frame tilt/brakes relief valve faulty. Brake control valve faulty."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p911_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 911, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-853\nHYDRAULIC HOSES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hoses leading to \nbrake actuators for cracks, kinks, or pinches.\n(a) If there are no cracks, kinks, or \npinches in hydraulic hoses, go to \nStep 3 of this fault.\n(b) If there are cracks, kinks, or \npinches in one or more of hydraulic hoses, shut down engine \n(TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace hose(s) (Para 12-9).\nHYDRAULIC HOSE CONNECTIONS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hose connections/\nfittings leading to brake actuators for leaks.\n(a) If there are no leaks at hydraulic \nhose connections/fittings, go to Step 4 of this fault.\n(b) If there are leaks at one or more of \nconnections/fittings, shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and  tighten or replace connections/\nfittings (Para 12-9).TR01280", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-853\nHYDRAULIC HOSES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hoses leading to \nbrake actuators for cracks, kinks, or pinches.\n(a) If there are no cracks, kinks, or \npinches "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p912_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 912, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-854     Change 110. BRAKE ACTUATORS (LEFT AND/OR RIGHT REAR  AND LEFT AND/OR RIGHT FRONT) DO NOT \nOPERATE CORRECTLY (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK. Hydraulic hoses OK.Hose connections/fittings OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nTandem gear pump faulty.Frame tilt/brakes relief valve faulty.Brake control valve faulty. Brake hydraulic accumulator faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil pressure is not at \n2600 to 2700 psi (17940 to 18630 kPa), tandem gear pump is faulty. KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK.Hydraulic hoses OK. Hose connections/fittings OK. Tandem gear pump OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFrame tilt/brakes relief valve faulty. Brake control valve faulty.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-854     Change 110. BRAKE ACTUATORS (LEFT AND/OR RIGHT REAR  AND LEFT AND/OR RIGHT FRONT) DO NOT \nOPERATE CORRECTLY (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK. Hydraulic hoses OK."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p912_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 912, "text": "is faulty. KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK.Hydraulic hoses OK. Hose connections/fittings OK. Tandem gear pump OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFrame tilt/brakes relief valve faulty. Brake control valve faulty.\nBrake hydraulic accumulator faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test.STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil pressure is not at \n2600 to 2700 psi (17940 and 18630 kPa), relief valve is faulty.4\nIs hydraulic oil pressure at \nsmall section of tandem gear pump at 2600 to 2700 psi \n(17940 to 18630 kPa)? YESNOTest/replace \ntandem gear pump \n(Para 18-3). 5\nIs hydraulic oil pressure at \nframe tilt/brakes relief valve at \n2600 and 2700 psi (17940 and 18630 kPa)? YESNOAdjust pressure or \nreplace faulty relief valve (Para 18-8).", "snippet": "is faulty. KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK.Hydraulic hoses OK. Hose connections/fittings OK. Tandem gear pump OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nFrame tilt/brakes relief valve faulty. Brake control valve faulty."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p913_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 913, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1      2-855\nTANDEM GEAR PUMP\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non small section of tandem gear pump. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P). (3) Read output pressure on test device. (a) If hydraulic oil pressure is at 2600 \nto 2700 psi (17940 to 18630 kPa), \ngo to Step 5 of this fault. (b) If hydraulic oil pr essure is not at \n2600 to 2700 psi (17940 to 18630 kPa), shut down engine \n(TM 10-3930-673-10) and test (small section) and replace tandem \ngear pump (Para 18-3). FRAME TILT/BRAKES RELIEF VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Install a test tee on inlet line to frame \ntilt/brakes relief valve. (2) Connect a 0 to 5000 psi \n(0 to 34475 kPa) pressure gage to test \ntee. (3) Read output pressure on test device.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1      2-855\nTANDEM GEAR PUMP\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non small section of tandem gear pump. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p913_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 913, "text": "RESSURE TEST\n(1) Install a test tee on inlet line to frame \ntilt/brakes relief valve. (2) Connect a 0 to 5000 psi \n(0 to 34475 kPa) pressure gage to test \ntee. (3) Read output pressure on test device.\n(a) If hydraulic oil pressure is between \n2350 to 2450 psi (16215 to 16905 kPa), go to Step 6 of this \nfault. (b) If hydraulic oil pressure is not \nbetween 2350 to 2450 psi (16215 to 16905 kPa), go to Step (4) \nbelow. (4) If valve is not damaged, adjust relief \npressure to specified psi and remove pressure gage and test tee.(a) If hydraulic oil pressure is adjusted \nto specified psi, go to Step 6 of this fault. (b) If hydraulic oil pressure cannot be \nadjusted, replace relief valve (Para 18-8). TR01281", "snippet": "RESSURE TEST\n(1) Install a test tee on inlet line to frame \ntilt/brakes relief valve. (2) Connect a 0 to 5000 psi \n(0 to 34475 kPa) pressure gage to test \ntee. (3) Read output pressure on test device."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p914_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 914, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-856     Change 110. BRAKE ACTUATORS (LEFT AND/OR RIGHT REAR  AND LEFT AND/OR RIGHT FRONT) DO NOT \nOPERATE CORRECTLY (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK. Hydraulic hoses OK.Hose connections/fittings OK. Tandem gear pump OK. Frame tilt/brakes relief valve OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nBrake control valve faulty. Brake hydraulic accumulator faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nPressure test. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic oil pressure is not 1700 \nto 1800 psi (11721 to 12411 kPa) (2350 to 2450 psi [16203 to 16892 kPa] fully charged), brake \ncontrol valve is faulty. KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK. Hydraulic hoses OK. Hose connections/fittings OK. Tandem gear pump OK.Frame tilt/brakes relief valve OK. Brake control valve OK.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-856     Change 110. BRAKE ACTUATORS (LEFT AND/OR RIGHT REAR  AND LEFT AND/OR RIGHT FRONT) DO NOT \nOPERATE CORRECTLY (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK. Hydraulic hoses OK."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p914_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 914, "text": "ed), brake \ncontrol valve is faulty. KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK. Hydraulic hoses OK. Hose connections/fittings OK. Tandem gear pump OK.Frame tilt/brakes relief valve OK. Brake control valve OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nBrake hydraulic accumulator faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. Pressure test. STE/ICE-R #51. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf brake accumulator is damaged, \naccumulator will not function. If pressure is not at precharged \nlimit, actuators will not function.6\nIs hydraulic oil pressure at \nbrake control valve between 1600 and 1700 psi (11040 and \n11730 kPa) (2350 to 2450 psi \n[16203 to 16892 kPa] fully charged)? YESNOReplace faulty \nbrake control valve \n(Para 0-1). 7\nIs brake hydraulic \naccumulator free from signs \nof damage? Is precharge pressure in brake hydraulic \naccumulator set at 900 psi \n(6210 kPa)? YESNOReplace damaged \naccumulator (Para 12-8) or \nadjust pressure to \n900 psi. Notify Direct Support about this fault.", "snippet": "ed), brake \ncontrol valve is faulty. KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK. Hydraulic hoses OK. Hose connections/fittings OK. Tandem gear pump OK.Frame tilt/brakes relief valve OK. Brake control valve OK."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p915_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 915, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1      2-857\nFRAME TILT VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non brake control valve. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P). (3) Read output pressure on test device. (a) If hydraulic oil pressure is at 1600 \nand 1700 psi (11040 and 11730 \nkPa) (2350 to 2450 psi [16203 to \n16892 kPa] fully charged), go to Step 7 of this fault. (b) If hydraulic oil pr essure is not at \n1600 and 1700 psi (11040 and 11730 kPa) (2350 to 2450 psi \n[16203 to 16892 kPa] fully \ncharged), shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace \nbrake control valve \n(Para 0-1). NOTE\nAdjust accumulator charging pressure at brake control valve to 1650 ±25 psi.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1      2-857\nFRAME TILT VALVE\nPRESSURE TEST\n(1) Remove cover from  diagnostic coupler \non brake control valve. (2) Perform STE/ICE Test #51 \n(TM 9-4910-571-12&P). (3) Read o"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p915_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 915, "text": "6203 to 16892 kPa] fully \ncharged), shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace \nbrake control valve \n(Para 0-1). NOTE\nAdjust accumulator charging pressure at brake control valve to 1650 ±25 psi.\nHYDRAULIC ACCUMULATOR VISUAL \nINSPECTION/PRESSURE TEST\n(1) Visually inspect brake hydraulic \naccumulator for signs of damage.(a) If brake hydraulic accumulator is \nnot damaged, go to Step (2) below. (b) If brake hydraulic accumulator is \ndamaged, replace accumulator \n(Para 12-8). (2) Shut down engine \n(TM 10-3930-673-10) and remove brake hydraulic accumulator \n(Para 12-8) and test precharge pressure in accumulator. (a) If precharge pressure in \naccumulator is at 900 psi \n(6210 kPa), reinstall accumulator \n(Para 12-8). (b) If precharge pressure in \naccumulator is less than 900 psi (6210 kPa), add nitrogen; if \npressure is greater than 900 psi (6210 kPa), bleed nitrogen from \naccumulator. Reinstall accumulator \n(Para 12-8).TR01282\nTR01819", "snippet": "6203 to 16892 kPa] fully \ncharged), shut down engine (TM 10-3930-673-10) and replace \nbrake control valve \n(Para 0-1). NOTE\nAdjust accumulator charging pressure at brake control valve to 1650 ±25 psi."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p916_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 916, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8582\n11. EMERGENCY STEERING PUMP DOES NOT OPERATE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine shut down (TM 10-3930-673-10)\nParking brake on (TM 10-3930-673-10)Wheels chocked (TM 10-3930-673-10)Personnel Required\nTwo\nReferences\nTM 10-3930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHydraulic oil level low. Hydraulic hoses faulty. Hose connections faulty.Emergency steering pump faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-8582\n11. EMERGENCY STEERING PUMP DOES NOT OPERATE\nINITIAL SETUP\nTools and Special Tools\nTool Kit, General Mechanics: Automotive\n(Item 18, Appendix F)\nEquipment Condition\nEngine s"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p916_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 916, "text": "930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHydraulic oil level low. Hydraulic hoses faulty. Hose connections faulty.Emergency steering pump faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nThis question eliminates possible \nproblems and determines where troubleshooting continues.1\nIs hydraulic oil tank filled to \nproper level?WARNING\nRead WARNING on\nfollowing page.START\nYESNOFill hydr aulic oil \ntank to proper level \n(Para 18-2).", "snippet": "930-673-10TM 9-4910-571-12&P. KNOWN INFO\nNone. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHydraulic oil level low. Hydraulic hoses faulty. Hose connections faulty.Emergency steering pump faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p917_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 917, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-859HYDRAULIC OIL TANKHYDRAULIC \u001f\nOIL LEVEL GAGEWARNING\n• Hydraulic oil in system can be under pressures over 3000 psi (20685 kPa) with engine OFF. Always relieve pressure in hydraulic  \nlines before attempting to remove any component in hydraulic s ystem. With engine OFF, starter switch in RUN position, and \nMLRS attachment on ground, move control levers through all operat ing positions several times to relieve live pressure. Relieve \npressure in hydraulic oil tank by loosening filler cap very slowly. Failure to follow these precautions could result in serious  \npersonal injury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-859HYDRAULIC OIL TANKHYDRAULIC \u001f\nOIL LEVEL GAGEWARNING\n• Hydraulic oil in system can be under pressures over 3000 psi (20685 kPa) with engine OFF. Always relieve pressure in hydr"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p917_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 917, "text": "ollow these precautions could result in serious  \npersonal injury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries.\nAllow hydraulic oil to cool befor e disconnecting any hydraulic lines. CAUTION\nWipe area clean around all hydrau lic connections to be opened during removal and disa ssembly. Cap oil lines and plug holes afte r \nremoving lines. Contamination of hydraulic s ystem could result in premature failure. HYDRAULIC OIL LEVEL\nVISUAL INSPECTION\n(1) Visually inspect hydraulic oil level gage \nto determine if tank is filled to proper \nlevel. (2) Hydraulic oil should be visible between \nADD and FULL marks.(a) If hydraulic oil is visible between \nADD and FULL marks, go to \nStep 2 of the fault. (b) If hydraulic oil is not visible, inspect \ntank for leaks. Then fill tank to FULL mark with hydraulic oil \n(Para 18-2). TR01283", "snippet": "ollow these precautions could result in serious  \npersonal injury. • At operating temperatures, hydraulic oil is hot and under pressu re. Hot oil is hot and under pressure. Hot oil can cause injuries."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p918_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 918, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-86011. EMERGENCY STEERING PUMP DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHydraulic hoses faulty. Hose connections faulty.Emergency steering pump faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic hoses are cracked, \nkinked, or pinched, emergency steering pump will not function. KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK.Hydraulic hoses OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHose connections faulty.Emergency steering pump faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection. REASON FOR QUESTION\nIf hydraulic hose connections are \ndamaged or leaking, hydraulic system will lose pressure.2\nAre hydraulic hoses leading \nto emergency steering pump free of cracked, kinked, or \npinches sections? YESNOReplace faulty \nhoses (Para 14-6)..", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-86011. EMERGENCY STEERING PUMP DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT). KNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK. POSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nHydraulic hoses faulty. Hose connections faulty.Emergency steering pu"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p918_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 918, "text": "amaged or leaking, hydraulic system will lose pressure.2\nAre hydraulic hoses leading \nto emergency steering pump free of cracked, kinked, or \npinches sections? YESNOReplace faulty \nhoses (Para 14-6)..\n3\nAre hydraulic hose \nconnections leading to \nemergency steering pump tight and free of leaks? YESNOTighten or replace \nfaulty connections/\nfittings (Para 14-6).", "snippet": "amaged or leaking, hydraulic system will lose pressure.2\nAre hydraulic hoses leading \nto emergency steering pump free of cracked, kinked, or \npinches sections? YESNOReplace faulty \nhoses (Para 14-6).."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p919_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 919, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-861EMERGENCY\u001f\nSTEERING\u001fPUMP\nHYDRAULIC\u001f\nCONNECTIONSHYDRAULIC\u001f\nHOSESHYDRAULIC HOSES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hoses leading to \nemergency steering pump for cracks, kinks, \nor pinches.\n(a) If there are no cracks, kinks, or \npinches in hydraulic hoses, go to Step 3 of this fault.\n(b) If there are cracks, kinks, or \npinches in one or more of hydraulic hoses, replace hose(s) (Para 14-6).\nHYDRAULIC HOSE CONNECTIONS\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hose connections \nleading to emergency steering pump for leaks.\n(a) If there are no leaks at hydraulic \nhose connections, go to Step 4 of this fault.\n(b) If there are leaks at one or more of \nconnections/fittings, tighten or replace connec tions/fittings \n(Para 14-6).TR01284", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-861EMERGENCY\u001f\nSTEERING\u001fPUMP\nHYDRAULIC\u001f\nCONNECTIONSHYDRAULIC\u001f\nHOSESHYDRAULIC HOSES\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect hydr aulic hoses leading to \nemergency steering pump for crac"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p920_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 920, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-86211. EMERGENCY STEERING PUMP DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK.\nHydraulic hoses OK.\nHose connections OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nEmergency steering pump faulty.TEST OPTIONS\nVisual inspection.\nREASON FOR QUESTION\nIf emergency steering pump is \ndamaged, pump will not function.4\nIs emergency steering pump \nfree from signs of damage?\nYESNOReplace damaged \nemergency steering \npump (Para 14-5).\nGo to Electrical System \nTroubleshooting, Fault 41, Emergency \nsteering pump does not operate.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-86211. EMERGENCY STEERING PUMP DOES NOT OPERATE (CONT).\nKNOWN INFO\nHydraulic oil level OK.\nHydraulic hoses OK.\nHose connections OK.\nPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS\nEmergency steering pump faul"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p921_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 921, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-863/( 2-864 blank)EMERGENCY\u001f\nSTEERING PUMPEMERGENCY STEERING PUMP\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect emergency steering pump \nfor signs of damage.\n(a) If emergency steering pump is not \ndamaged, go to Fault 41, Emergency steering pump does \nnot operate.\n(b) If emergency steering pump is \ndamaged, replace pump \n(Para 14-5).\nTR01285", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-863/( 2-864 blank)EMERGENCY\u001f\nSTEERING PUMPEMERGENCY STEERING PUMP\nVISUAL INSPECTION\nVisually inspect emergency steering pump \nfor signs of damage.\n(a) If emergency steering pump "}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p923_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 923, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1      2-865STE-ICE-R GO-Chain Testing Sequence\nThe following GO-Chain tests are made using vehicle DCA connector. All tests must be performed sequentially. The \nfollowing is a summary of each test. For normal readings, see vehicl e test card, page 2-912. 2-18.", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\nChange 1      2-865STE-ICE-R GO-Chain Testing Sequence\nThe following GO-Chain tests are made using vehicle DCA connector. All tests must be performed sequentially. The \nfollowing i"}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p923_chunk_1", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 923, "text": " GO-Chain tests are made using vehicle DCA connector. All tests must be performed sequentially. The \nfollowing is a summary of each test. For normal readings, see vehicl e test card, page 2-912. 2-18.\nSTE/ICE-R VEHICLE SYSTEM DIAGNOSTIC CHECK\nGO TEST \nNUMBER MODE TEST TITLEPAGE \nNUMBER\nGO1 DCA VTM Connection and Checkout 2-866\nGO2 DCA First Peak Test -Starter Current 2-870\nGO3 DCA Vehicle Gauges Check 2-872GO4 DCA Vehicle Voltmeter 2-875GO5 DCA Engine Temperature and Pressure Checks 2-876\nGO6 DCA Engine Idle Speed Check 2-878GO7 DCA Battery Voltage Check 2-879GO8 DCA-TK Engine Oil Pressure Test 2-880\nGO9 DCA Engine Power Test 2-882\nG10 DCA-TK Transmission Oil Pressure Test 2-883\nG11 DCA-TK Transmission Clutch Pressure Test 2-885\nG12 DCA-TK Transmission Converter  Charge Pressure Test 2-887\nG13 DCA-TK Transmission Brake Cutoff Valve Pressure Test 2-889\nG14 CDA-TK Fuel Return Line Pressure Test 2-891\nINDICATES VTM IS PROPERLY\nCONNECTED AND READYFOR TESTS\nINDICATES GO-CONDITIONAFTER CONFIDENCE TESTS\nPOWER\nSWITCHDCA CABLE\nW1\nP1", "snippet": " GO-Chain tests are made using vehicle DCA connector. All tests must be performed sequentially. The \nfollowing is a summary of each test. For normal readings, see vehicl e test card, page 2-912. 2-18."}
{"id": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf_p924_chunk_0", "source": "TM-10-3930-673-20-1.pdf", "page": 924, "text": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-866      Change 1NOTE\nPerform all GO steps until a NO-GO condition exists, then perform the NO-GO step indicated.2-18. STE/ICE-R VEHICLE SYSTE M DIAGNOSTIC CHECK (CONT)", "snippet": "TM 10-3930-673-20-1\n2-866      Change 1NOTE\nPerform all GO steps until a NO-GO condition exists, then perform the NO-GO step indicated.2-18. STE/ICE-R VEHICLE SYSTE M DIAGNOSTIC CHECK (CONT)"}
